window.LA=[{"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit I - Welfare", "q": "What do you mean by Pareto Optimality? Explain the Pareto optimality criteria (marginal conditions).", "y": "2018, 2022, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Vilfredo Pareto** (Italian economist) gave the criterion of welfare\n- **Meaning:** no one can be made better off without making someone worse off\n- **Pareto improvement:** a change that makes someone better off and nobody worse off\n- **Ordinal utility** is used, no interpersonal comparison needed\n- **Basis of modern welfare economics** and efficiency analysis\n# Assumptions\n- Two consumers, two goods, two factors (2x2x2 model)\n- Perfect competition in all markets\n- Utility is ordinal, given tastes\n- Production functions are known and given\n- Given resources and technology\n- No externalities, no public goods\n- Rational consumers and producers\n# Three Marginal Conditions\n- **1. Exchange optimum (consumption):** MRS of two goods equal for all consumers\n[[MRS(A) xy = MRS(B) xy]]\n- If MRS differ, consumers can gain by trading, so not optimal\n- Shown by **Edgeworth box** of exchange, contract curve is the locus of tangency\n- **2. Production optimum:** MRTS of two factors equal for all producers\n[[MRTS(X) lk = MRTS(Y) lk]]\n- If MRTS differ, shifting factors raises output of one good without lowering the other\n- Shown by Edgeworth box of production, production contract curve\n- **3. Product-mix optimum:** MRS equals MRT (marginal rate of transformation)\n[[MRS xy = MRT xy]]\n- If MRS is not equal to MRT, society can change the output mix to raise welfare\n- MRT is the slope of the production possibility curve\n- Grand utility possibility frontier is the combination of all three conditions\n# Pareto Optimality and Perfect Competition\n- **Perfect competition ensures** Pareto optimality because P = MC = MR\n- Each consumer equates MRS to price ratio, so MRS are equal\n- Each producer equates MRTS to factor price ratio\n- MRT = ratio of MCs = price ratio = MRS\n# Pareto Optimal Position\n- There are many Pareto optimal points, not only one\n- Each point is on the utility possibility curve\n- Social welfare function is needed to select the best point (Bergson, Samuelson)\n# Limitations and Criticism\n- Silent on **income distribution** and equity\n- Ignores **externalities** and public goods\n- Assumes perfect competition, which is unreal\n- No interpersonal comparison, so cannot judge policy that makes some lose\n- Ignores **second best** problem (Lipsey and Lancaster)\n- Status quo bias, depends on initial distribution of resources\n- Static view\n- Value judgements are hidden\n# Importance\n- Foundation of welfare economics (First and Second Fundamental Theorems)\n- Used in cost-benefit analysis and policy\n- Basis for compensation tests of Kaldor and Hicks\n> Conclusion: Pareto optimality is a minimum efficiency test, not a full test of social welfare.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit I - Welfare", "q": "Critically examine the Kaldor-Hicks compensation principle. Is it superior to Pareto criterion?", "y": "2016, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Problem:** Pareto criterion cannot judge changes where some gain and others lose\n- **Solution:** Kaldor (1939) and Hicks (1940) gave compensation tests\n- **Aim:** to make welfare economics useful for policy\n- **Basis:** potential Pareto improvement\n# Kaldor Criterion\n- A change is an improvement if **gainers can compensate losers** and still be better off\n- Compensation is **hypothetical,** not actually paid\n- Based on a comparison of the gains and the losses in money terms\n- Example: new road gives traders Rs 100 gain, farmers lose Rs 60, so improvement\n# Hicks Criterion\n- A change is an improvement if **losers cannot bribe gainers** to stop the change\n- Looks at the change from the viewpoint of losers\n- Both give the same result in many cases\n# Scitovsky Double Criterion\n- **Scitovsky paradox:** Kaldor test may approve a change and also its reversal\n- Scitovsky said both the change and its reversal must be tested\n- Change is an improvement if it passes the Kaldor test and the reverse move fails it\n# Little's Criticism and Other Tests\n- **I. M. D. Little:** added a distribution criterion (distribution should not worsen)\n- **Samuelson:** utility possibility frontier approach, tests are not sufficient\n- **Bergson:** social welfare function is needed\n# Merits\n- Wider than the Pareto criterion\n- Can be used for tax, tariff, public projects\n- Base of **cost-benefit analysis**\n- Avoids interpersonal comparison of utility (in theory)\n- Supports efficiency and growth arguments\n# Demerits\n- **Hypothetical compensation:** if not paid, losers remain worse off\n- **Value judgement** is hidden: assumes a rupee is of equal worth to rich and poor\n- **Scitovsky paradox** shows inconsistency\n- Ignores income distribution\n- Uses money as the measure of welfare, a cardinal view\n- Assumes given prices and tastes\n- Difficult to measure gains and losses\n# Importance\n- Gave a practical test for policy makers\n> Conclusion: Compensation tests widen the Pareto test, but they cannot escape value judgements, so they are only partly superior.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "How is a cartel formed in an oligopoly market? Explain the concept of market-sharing cartel.", "y": "2019, 2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Oligopoly:** few sellers, interdependence, uncertainty about rivals\n- **Cartel:** formal agreement among firms to fix price, output or share the market\n- **Aim:** to avoid competition, act like a monopolist, earn maximum joint profit\n- **Examples:** OPEC, diamond cartel (De Beers)\n# Why Cartels are Formed\n- To reduce uncertainty about rivals\n- To avoid price wars\n- To earn monopoly profit jointly\n- To restrict entry of new firms\n- To bargain with buyers and suppliers\n# Types of Cartel\n- **Perfect (centralised) cartel:** one central body decides all\n- **Market-sharing cartel:** firms agree on price and divide the market\n# Perfect (Centralised) Cartel\n- **Central agency** is set up by the member firms\n- Agency **adds up the marginal costs** of all firms to get industry MC\n- **Industry MR** is derived from market demand curve\n- Equilibrium output where **industry MC = industry MR**\n[[Industry: MC = MR ; Price from demand curve]]\n- Total output is then distributed among firms so that **MC of each firm = industry MR**\n[[MC1 = MC2 = ... = MCn = MR]]\n- **Profit** is also pooled and then shared (by agreement, not necessarily by cost)\n- Low-cost firms produce more\n# Market-Sharing Cartel\n- Firms agree on **a common price,** no central body\n- Share the market by:\n- **Non-price competition:** advertising, quality, service\n- **Quota system:** fixed output share by sales or capacity\n- **Geographical division:** each firm gets an area\n- Each firm sells at the agreed price and its profit depends on its own cost\n- Low-cost firms gain more\n# Instability of Cartels\n- **Incentive to cheat:** a firm can increase output secretly\n- **New entrants** may undercut\n- **Difference in cost** and demand among firms\n- **Legal restrictions** (anti-trust, MRTP, Competition Act, 2002)\n- Government control and changes in demand\n- Disagreement about quota and share\n# Conclusion\n> A cartel reduces competition and acts like a monopoly. It is in the firms' interest but harmful for consumers, and is often unstable.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "What is monopolistic competition? Discuss Chamberlin's model with free entry of firms.", "y": "2018, 2020, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **E. H. Chamberlin** (Theory of Monopolistic Competition, 1933) and **Joan Robinson** (Imperfect Competition, 1933)\n- **Meaning:** market with many sellers, differentiated products, free entry\n- **Mix of monopoly and competition**\n- **Examples:** toothpaste, soaps, restaurants, clothes\n# Features\n- **Large number of sellers** (but not as many as in perfect competition)\n- **Product differentiation:** by brand, quality, packing, location, service\n- **Free entry and exit** in the long run\n- **Selling costs:** advertising and sales promotion\n- **Downward sloping demand curve:** elastic, firm has limited price control\n- **Non-price competition**\n- **Independent decision** of firms (no mutual dependence)\n- **Imperfect knowledge** of consumers\n# Short Run Equilibrium\n- Firm equates **MC = MR**\n- Price is above AC, so it earns **supernormal profit** (or may suffer loss)\n[[SR: MC = MR ; AR above AC]]\n# Long Run Equilibrium\n- Supernormal profit attracts **new firms**\n- Demand curve of each firm shifts to the left and becomes more elastic\n- Equilibrium where **AR curve is tangent to LAC**\n[[LR: MC = MR and AR = AC]]\n- Only **normal profit** is earned\n- If there are losses, firms leave and the opposite happens\n# Excess Capacity\n- Firm produces less than **minimum-AC output** (optimum scale)\n- The gap = **excess capacity**\n- **Price is above MC,** hence allocative inefficiency\n- Cost of product variety\n# Chamberlin's Group Equilibrium\n- **DD curve:** demand when all firms change price\n- **dd curve:** demand when only one firm changes price\n- Group equilibrium needs P = AC for the average firm\n# Selling Costs\n- Shift demand to the right, make it less elastic\n- U-shaped selling cost curve\n# Criticism\n- Group concept unrealistic (uniform demand and cost assumed)\n- Product differentiation makes group heterogeneous (Kaldor)\n- Excess capacity not wasteful if variety is valued (Demsetz)\n- Ignores interdependence in small groups\n- Free entry in the long run is not always true\n# Importance\n- Realistic market form in many sectors\n- Explains advertising and branding\n> Conclusion: Monopolistic competition gives normal profits, excess capacity and variety.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Explain Cournot's duopoly model. How is equilibrium achieved through reaction curves?", "y": "2016, 2017, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Augustin Cournot** (French mathematician), 1838, first oligopoly model\n- **Duopoly:** two sellers of a homogeneous product\n- **Aim:** to find the equilibrium output of the two sellers\n- **Example used:** two firms selling mineral water from springs\n# Assumptions\n- Two firms, **identical product,** same market\n- **Zero cost** of production (or equal costs)\n- **Linear demand curve** for the market\n- Each firm decides its **output, not price**\n- Each firm assumes that **rival's output remains constant** (zero conjectural variation)\n- Firms take decisions simultaneously\n- No entry of other firms\n# Working of the Model\n- Firm A first acts as monopolist and produces **OQ/2** of the market OQ\n- Firm B assumes A's output is fixed and takes half of the remaining: **OQ/4**\n- A now sees B's output and reduces its output to **3/8**\n- B adjusts: output rises (**5/16**)\n- This process of adjustment continues, A's output falls and B's rises\n- Both firms reach equilibrium\n# Equilibrium Output\n[[A = OQ/3 ; B = OQ/3 ; industry = 2OQ/3]]\n- Each firm gets one-third of the market, one-third is unserved\n- Price is higher than competitive price, lower than monopoly price\n# Reaction Curves\n- **Reaction curve of A (RA):** shows A's best output for each output of B\n- Slopes **downwards,** higher B output means lower A output\n- **Reaction curve of B (RB)** is likewise drawn\n- **Cournot equilibrium** is at the intersection of RA and RB\n- At this point neither firm wants to change output\n# Stability\n- If the firms are away from equilibrium, adjustment brings them back\n- So it is **stable equilibrium**\n# General Case\n- With n firms, each produces 1/(n+1) of the market\n- Industry output = n/(n+1) of the competitive output\n- As n rises, result nears perfect competition\n# Criticism\n- Naive behaviour: assumes the rival never changes output\n- **Ignores interdependence** and price strategy (Bertrand)\n- Assumes no cost, no entry, homogeneous product\n- Cannot explain price leadership or collusion\n- Static, one-period model\n# Importance\n- First formal model of oligopoly, base for game theory (Nash)\n> Conclusion: Cournot's model is simple and historic, but its behaviour assumption is unrealistic.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Explain Kinked Demand Curve theory of oligopoly.", "y": "2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Paul Sweezy** (USA) and **Hall and Hitch** (UK), 1939\n- **Aim:** to explain **price rigidity** in oligopoly\n- **Observation:** prices in oligopoly stay stable for long periods\n- **Idea:** demand curve of the firm has a kink at the existing price\n# Assumptions\n- **Few sellers,** product may be differentiated\n- If a firm **cuts price,** rivals also cut it, to retain customers\n- If a firm **raises price,** rivals do not follow\n- Cost conditions are given\n- The existing price is accepted by all\n# The Kinked Demand Curve\n- **Above the kink:** demand is highly **elastic** (price rise loses customers)\n- **Below the kink:** demand is **inelastic** (price cut gives little gain)\n- The two segments join at the **kink**\n- Kink is at existing price P and output Q\n# Marginal Revenue Curve\n- MR curve has **two parts,** one for each segment of demand\n- There is a **discontinuity (gap) in MR** at the output corresponding to kink\n- Marginal cost curve can shift up or down within the gap\n[[Price and output remain unchanged if MC moves within the MR gap]]\n# Result\n- **Price rigidity** even when cost changes (within a limit)\n- If MC rises sharply beyond the gap, price changes\n- Firms try to compete by non-price methods: advertising, quality, service\n# Merits\n- Explains price stability in many oligopoly markets\n- Simple and realistic reaction pattern\n# Criticism\n- **Does not explain how the original price is determined**\n- Not a theory of price, only of rigidity\n- **Stigler** found evidence against: oligopoly prices do change\n- Rivals may follow price rises in a boom\n- Ignores collusion and leadership\n# Importance\n- Useful in explaining why firms do not engage in price wars\n> Conclusion: Kinked demand curve explains stickiness of price, not its level.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit III - Consumption", "q": "Explain the Permanent Income Hypothesis and compare it with the Absolute Income hypothesis.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Milton Friedman,** A Theory of the Consumption Function (1957)\n- **Aim:** to solve the **puzzle** between the short-run and long-run consumption function\n- **Puzzle:** Cross-section data and short run show APC falls, long-run data (Kuznets) show APC constant\n# Main Idea\n- Consumption depends on **permanent income,** not current income\n- **Permanent income:** the average expected income over a long period (lifetime)\n- Consumers plan their spending on the basis of long-term income\n[[Cp = k . Yp]]\n# Components of Measured Income\n- **Y = Yp + Yt**\n- **Yp:** permanent income\n- **Yt:** transitory income (windfall gain or loss)\n- **Consumption = Cp + Ct** (permanent and transitory consumption)\n- Correlation of Yt with Yp is zero, Ct with Cp is zero, Yt with Ct is zero\n# The Factor k\n- Proportion of permanent income consumed\n- Depends on **rate of interest**\n- Depends on **ratio of wealth to income (w)**\n- Depends on **age and tastes (u)**\n- Not on the level of income\n# Short Run\n- A rise in current income is mostly transitory, so it is mostly saved\n- So **short-run MPC is low** and APC falls in the short run\n# Long Run\n- Permanent income changes, so consumption changes in proportion\n- **APC = MPC = k** in the long run\n- The long-run consumption function is a straight line from the origin\n# Cross-section Analysis\n- High-income groups have positive transitory income, so APC looks lower\n- Low-income groups have negative transitory income, so APC looks higher\n# Comparison with Keynes\n- **Keynes:** current income, APC falls with income\n- **Friedman:** permanent income, APC constant in long run\n- **Keynes:** short-run view. **Friedman:** long-run planning\n# Criticism\n- Permanent income cannot be measured directly\n- Assumes consumers have perfect foresight\n- Assumption of zero correlation is debatable\n- Ignores credit and liquidity limits\n- Rational planning is not true for poor\n# Importance\n- Policy effect: temporary tax cuts have small effect on consumption\n- Basis of the life cycle theory (Modigliani)\n> Conclusion: Friedman reconciled the evidence, showing that consumption depends on long-term income.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit III - Consumption", "q": "What are the limitations of Keynesian consumption function? Has Duesenberry given a better approach?", "y": "2017, 2022", "a": "# Keynes's Consumption Function\n- **C = a + bY,** with a as autonomous consumption and b as MPC\n- **Psychological law:** consumption rises with income, but by less than the rise in income\n- **MPC < 1, APC falls** as income rises\n# Limitations of the Keynesian Function\n- **Only current absolute income** is considered\n- **Kuznets (1946):** long-run data show APC constant, against Keynes\n- **Cross-section data** support Keynes but time series do not\n- **Wealth effect ignored** (Pigou, Modigliani)\n- **Interest rate** influence ignored\n- **Past income and habits** ignored\n- **Demonstration effect** and social factors ignored\n- **Expectations and uncertainty** ignored\n- **Distribution of income** is ignored\n- **Credit availability** and price level not considered\n- **Short-run and long-run difference** is not explained\n- **Age structure** of population, urbanisation ignored\n# Post-Keynesian Theories\n- **Duesenberry:** relative income hypothesis\n- **Friedman:** permanent income\n- **Modigliani:** life cycle\n# Duesenberry's Relative Income Hypothesis\n- **Consumption depends on relative income** (income compared with others) and **highest past income**\n- **Demonstration effect:** people imitate those of higher income\n- **Ratchet effect:** consumption does not fall easily when income falls\n- The consumption function is irreversible\n[[Ct/Yt = a + b (Yt/Y0)]]\n# Short Run and Long Run\n- **Short run:** when income falls, consumption falls less, so APC rises\n- **Long run:** with steady growth in income, APC is constant\n- Explains both cross-section and time-series data\n# Criticism of Duesenberry\n- Based on imitation, difficult to measure\n- Ignores planning, rational choice\n- Lack of micro foundations\n- Ratchet effect is not always found\n# Merits\n- Explains the difference in short and long run\n- Social psychology as part of economics\n> Conclusion: Duesenberry's approach is more realistic than Keynes, though not free of weaknesses.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit IV - Money and IS-LM", "q": "What do you mean by IS-LM curve? Discuss how equilibrium is established in IS-LM model.", "y": "2016, 2022, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Hicks (1937)** and **Hansen** presented the IS-LM model\n- **Aim:** to show the simultaneous equilibrium of the **goods market and money market**\n- It combines **Keynesian and classical views**\n- Determines **equilibrium income (Y) and interest rate (r)**\n# IS Curve (Goods Market)\n- **IS:** combination of r and Y at which **Investment = Saving**\n- **Slope:** downward sloping\n- **Reason:** lower r leads to higher investment, higher income, so Y rises as r falls\n[[I(r) = S(Y)  or  Y = C + I + G]]\n- **Slope depends on** interest elasticity of investment and multiplier\n- Flatter IS if investment is elastic or MPC is high\n# Derivation of IS\n- Investment curve, saving curve and 45-degree line are used\n- At each r, find Y for which I = S\n- Join the points to get IS\n# Shift of IS\n- **Rise in G or fall in taxes** shifts IS right\n- **Rise in autonomous investment** or exports shifts IS right\n- **Fall in G** shifts IS left\n# LM Curve (Money Market)\n- **LM:** combination of r and Y at which **demand for money = supply of money**\n- **Slope:** upward sloping\n- **Reason:** higher Y raises transaction demand, so r must rise to keep money balance\n[[M/P = L(Y, r)]]\n- Three zones: **Keynesian (horizontal),** intermediate, **classical (vertical)**\n# Derivation of LM\n- L = L1 (transaction, precautionary) + L2 (speculative)\n- With fixed M, a rise in Y raises L1, leaving less for L2, so r rises\n# Shift of LM\n- **Rise in money supply** shifts LM right\n- **Rise in price level** shifts LM left\n- **Rise in liquidity preference** shifts LM left\n# Equilibrium\n- At the **point of intersection of IS and LM** both markets are in equilibrium\n- Income is Y* and interest rate is r*\n- **Disequilibrium:** excess saving or excess money supply pushes it back\n# Policy Analysis\n- **Fiscal policy:** expansion shifts IS right, Y and r rise\n- **Crowding out:** higher r reduces private investment\n- **Monetary policy:** expansion shifts LM right, Y rises and r falls\n- **Liquidity trap:** LM horizontal, monetary policy ineffective\n- **Investment trap:** IS vertical, monetary policy ineffective\n# Criticism\n- Static, no price level or inflation\n- Ignores supply side and expectations\n- Money supply is exogenous (assumption)\n- No role of banking or credit\n# Importance\n- Standard tool for macro policy\n> Conclusion: IS-LM shows how real and monetary factors jointly decide income and interest.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit V - Growth and Cycles", "q": "Write a note on Hicks' trade cycle theory. Is it an improvement on Samuelson's theory?", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **J. R. Hicks,** A Contribution to the Theory of the Trade Cycle (1950)\n- **Trade cycle:** wave-like fluctuation of income, output and employment\n- **Built on:** multiplier-accelerator interaction of Samuelson, with a **ceiling and floor**\n# Basic Elements\n- **Autonomous investment (A):** independent of income, grows at a steady rate\n- **Induced investment (I):** depends on change in income (accelerator)\n- **Multiplier:** raises income from investment\n- **Accelerator (v):** investment depends on change in output\n- **Lag:** consumption lags income\n- **Ceiling:** full employment limit\n- **Floor:** lower limit (disinvestment is limited)\n# Phases of the Cycle\n- **Upswing (boom):** autonomous investment raises income, accelerator makes induced investment, multiplier raises income more\n- **Cumulative rise** in income with an explosive path\n- **Ceiling reached:** income cannot rise beyond full employment output, so the boom ends\n- **Downswing (recession):** income falls, induced investment falls to zero\n- **Depression:** income falls along the explosive path till the floor\n- **Floor:** disinvestment is limited by replacement of worn capital, so fall stops\n- **Recovery:** autonomous investment brings income up again\n# Role of Ceiling and Floor\n- Ceiling is set by **resources (full employment)**\n- Floor is set by **autonomous investment and minimum consumption**\n- They turn explosive movement into **bounded cycles**\n# Comparison with Samuelson\n- Samuelson gives damped, explosive and steady paths\n- Explosive path has no turning points in Samuelson\n- Hicks adds a **ceiling and floor**, so the cycle repeats\n# Merits\n- Gives **turning points** of the cycle\n- Combines multiplier, accelerator and growth\n- Realistic cycle of finite amplitude\n# Criticism\n- Ceiling and floor are mechanical and rigid\n- **Accelerator is assumed constant** (unrealistic)\n- Floor assumes full cut in induced investment\n- Ignores money, price changes and expectations\n- Autonomous investment growth is assumed\n> Conclusion: Hicks's model is an improvement on Samuelson's, but it is a mechanical explanation.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Discuss the doctrine of Reciprocal Demand with the help of offer curves.", "y": "2016, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **J. S. Mill** (Principles of Political Economy, 1848) gave the theory of reciprocal demand\n- **Problem:** Ricardo's theory shows only the limits of trade, not the actual terms of trade\n- **Mill's answer:** the terms of trade are set by the **strength and elasticity of each country's demand for the other's product**\n- **Developed by:** Marshall and Edgeworth using **offer curves**\n# Assumptions\n- Two countries, two commodities\n- Free trade, no transport cost\n- Constant cost, full employment\n- Perfect competition, barter exchange\n- Labour is mobile within, not between countries\n# Limits of Terms of Trade (Ricardo-Mill)\n- **Domestic cost ratio of each country** fixes the limits\n- Example: India: 1 cloth = 2 wheat. Britain: 1 cloth = 1 wheat\n- Terms of trade lie **between 1 and 2 wheat per cloth**\n- Outside these limits, trade does not take place\n# Mill's Principle\n- Within the limits, terms are fixed by **reciprocal demand**\n- **Law of equation of international demand:** terms of trade adjust so that value of exports equals value of imports\n- **Greater demand of A for B's good,** worse the terms for A\n- **Gains are greater for** the country whose demand is less intense (less elastic)\n# Offer Curves (Marshall-Edgeworth)\n- **Offer curve:** shows how much of its export a country is ready to give for each quantity of import\n- It is drawn from the origin, curved\n- **Slope of the ray from origin:** terms of trade\n- **Shape depends on elasticity** of reciprocal demand\n# Equilibrium\n- **Intersection of the two offer curves** is the equilibrium\n- The terms of trade at this point equate quantity offered and quantity wanted\n- **At other terms of trade,** one country offers more than the other wants, so terms change\n# Effects of Change in Demand\n- **Rise in country A's demand for B's goods:** A's offer curve shifts, TOT turns against A\n- **Rise in productivity of A:** offer curve of A shifts, TOT can turn against A\n- **Tariff:** shifts the offer curve of the imposing country, can improve its TOT\n# Stability\n- Equilibrium is stable if the offer curves cut in the right way (Marshall-Lerner)\n# Merits\n- Explains the actual terms of trade\n- Brings demand side into trade theory\n- Offer curve is a useful tool\n# Criticism\n- Ignores cost side and factor endowments (Ohlin)\n- Two country, two good model is unreal\n- Constant costs assumed\n- Does not explain the causes of trade\n- Static and ignores capital movement\n> Conclusion: Mill's doctrine completes Ricardo's theory by fixing the terms of trade through reciprocal demand.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit III - Terms of Trade", "q": "What do you mean by terms of trade? Explain its various kinds and importance.", "y": "2016, 2017, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Terms of trade (TOT):** the rate at which one country's exports exchange for its imports\n- **Importance:** shows the share of gains from trade\n- **Concepts:** Taussig, Viner, Dorrance, Prebisch\n# Kinds of Terms of Trade\n- **1. Net barter TOT (commodity TOT):** ratio of export price index to import price index\n[[Net barter TOT = (Px / Pm) x 100]]\n- Rise means improvement, fall means deterioration\n- **2. Gross barter TOT (Taussig):** ratio of quantity of imports to quantity of exports\n[[Gross barter TOT = (Qm / Qx) x 100]]\n- **3. Income TOT (Dorrance):** net barter TOT multiplied by export volume index\n[[Income TOT = (Px / Pm) x Qx]]\n- Shows import capacity from exports\n- **4. Single factoral TOT (Viner):** net barter multiplied by productivity index of exports\n- **5. Double factoral TOT (Viner):** includes productivity of the foreign country's factors also\n- **6. Real cost TOT (Viner):** takes into account real cost (disutility) of labour\n- **7. Utility TOT (Viner):** includes utility of imports and disutility of exports\n# Determinants of TOT\n- Reciprocal demand and elasticity\n- Productivity changes and technology\n- Tariffs, quotas\n- Exchange rate changes\n- Inflation and growth\n- Terms depend on demand for exports and imports\n# Prebisch-Singer Hypothesis\n- **Terms of trade of developing countries decline** in the long run (primary vs manufactured goods)\n- Reasons: low income elasticity of demand for primary goods, monopoly in industrial countries\n- Policy: import substitution, diversification\n# Importance\n- Index of benefits of trade\n- Guides trade policy and tariffs\n- Gives a base for BOP analysis\n- Helps in comparing the growth impact\n- Indicates welfare of a country\n# Limitations\n- Net barter ignores quantity and quality changes\n- Index number problems\n- Does not show total welfare\n- Ignores services and capital flows\n# India's TOT\n- Fluctuations due to oil import prices and export of manufactures\n> Conclusion: TOT is a useful but incomplete indicator of the gain from trade.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Critically evaluate the Purchasing Power Parity theory of exchange rate.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Gustav Cassel** (Swedish economist) gave PPP in **1918** (earlier ideas by Ricardo, Wheatley)\n- **Idea:** exchange rate between two currencies is determined by their relative **purchasing power**\n- **Law of one price** is the base\n# Absolute PPP\n- Exchange rate equals ratio of price levels\n[[R = Pa / Pb]]\n- Example: basket costs Rs 800 in India and $10 in USA, so R = Rs 80 per $\n# Relative PPP\n- Change in exchange rate equals change in relative price levels\n[[R1 = R0 x (Pa1/Pa0) / (Pb1/Pb0)]]\n- If domestic inflation is higher, its currency depreciates\n# Assumptions\n- Free trade, no transport cost, no tariffs\n- Perfect competition, perfect information\n- Price indices are comparable\n- Flexible exchange rate\n- Price level determines exchange rate (not vice versa)\n# Uses\n- Gives the long-run equilibrium exchange rate\n- Used to fix the **new parity** after the war or devaluation (after WWI)\n- Comparison of living standards between countries (GDP at PPP)\n- Test of currency overvaluation (Big Mac index)\n# Criticism\n- **Ignores capital flows,** which affect exchange rates\n- **Non-traded goods** (services, land) are not equalised\n- **Transport costs, tariffs, quotas** break the law of one price\n- **Price index problem:** different goods, weights and quality\n- **Reverse causation:** exchange rate also changes prices\n- **Taste and demand differences**\n- **Expectations and speculation** affect exchange rate\n- **Balassa-Samuelson effect:** price levels are higher in rich countries\n- **Empirical evidence:** weak in short run, better in long run\n- **Government intervention** in exchange markets\n# Merits\n- Simple and clear\n- Useful in long-term analysis\n- Highlights role of inflation\n- Basis for exchange rate policy\n> Conclusion: PPP is a useful long-run guide but cannot fully explain exchange rate behaviour.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "What do you mean by Balance of Payments? Discuss the Absorption approach to correct disequilibrium.", "y": "2016, 2018, 2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Balance of Payments (BOP):** systematic record of all economic transactions between residents of a country and the rest of the world in one year\n- **Based on double-entry book-keeping:** credit (receipts) and debit (payments)\n- **BOP always balances** in accounting sense, but may be in disequilibrium\n# Components of BOP\n- **Current account:** visible trade (goods), invisible trade (services), investment income, unilateral transfers\n- **Capital account:** FDI, portfolio investment, external loans, banking capital\n- **Official reserve account:** changes in foreign exchange reserves, gold, SDR\n- **Errors and omissions:** balancing item\n# Balance of Trade vs BOP\n- BOT: only goods. BOP: all transactions\n- BOT is a part of the current account\n# Equilibrium and Disequilibrium\n- **Autonomous items** (above the line) vs **accommodating items** (below the line)\n- Deficit when autonomous payments exceed autonomous receipts\n# Absorption Approach (Sidney Alexander, 1952)\n- **Aim:** to analyse the effect of devaluation on BOP using income and absorption\n- **Absorption (A):** total domestic expenditure = C + I + G\n- **Identity:** Y = A + (X - M)\n[[B = Y - A]]\n- B = trade balance. Improvement needs **Y to rise more than A** or A to fall\n# Working of the Approach\n- Devaluation raises exports, lowers imports\n- If there are **idle resources,** output Y rises, and B improves if A rises less than Y\n- If there is **full employment,** Y cannot rise, so absorption must be cut\n- Need **expenditure-reducing policy:** tight fiscal and monetary policy\n# Direct Effects on Absorption\n- **Real balance (cash balance) effect:** rise in prices reduces real balances, so spending falls\n- **Money illusion:** people do not adjust fully\n- **Redistribution of income:** to profit earners with a higher saving rate\n- **Terms of trade effect:** worsening of TOT reduces real income\n- **Government revenue effect:** tax revenue rises\n# Conclusions of the Approach\n- Devaluation works if Y rises and A is controlled\n- **Policy mix:** devaluation plus deflation\n# Criticism\n- Ignores the relative price (elasticity) effects\n- Assumes autonomous absorption is independent of devaluation\n- Cannot separate income and absorption effects clearly\n- Does not explain capital flows\n- Marginal propensity to absorb is hard to measure\n# Importance\n- Brought macro analysis into BOP theory\n- Basis for IMF stabilisation programmes\n> Conclusion: The absorption approach explains that devaluation can succeed only with control over domestic spending.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "What do you understand by Agricultural Price Policy? What are its objectives and achievements?", "y": "2018, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Agricultural price policy:** government policy to fix farm prices so that farmers get **remunerative prices** and consumers get **fair prices**\n- **Need:** farm prices are unstable (weather, perishability, inelastic demand)\n- **Started:** 1965-66 with the Green Revolution and food shortage\n- **Agricultural Prices Commission** set up 1965, renamed **CACP** in 1985\n# Institutions\n- **CACP:** recommends MSP to the government\n- **FCI (1965):** procures grain, maintains buffer stock\n- **NAFED:** procures pulses and oilseeds\n- **PDS:** distributes grain at subsidised prices\n# Objectives\n- Give **remunerative price** to farmers, protect from price crash\n- Encourage **adoption of new technology** and higher output\n- Ensure **food security** and **buffer stock**\n- Keep **consumer prices** reasonable\n- Maintain **price parity** between agriculture and industry (terms of trade)\n- Change **cropping pattern** (pulses, oilseeds)\n- Reduce price fluctuation\n# Instruments\n- **Minimum Support Price (MSP):** guaranteed floor price for 22 crops (and FRP for sugarcane)\n- **Procurement price:** price at which government buys, above MSP in some states\n- **Issue price:** price at which PDS sells to consumers\n- **Buffer stock:** stocks to stabilise prices\n- **Statutory minimum price** for sugarcane (now FRP)\n- **Import-export policy** for stabilising price\n# Basis of MSP\n- **A2+FL cost** and **C2 cost** concepts\n- **Swaminathan Commission (2006):** MSP at least **50 percent above C2 cost**\n- **Budget 2018-19:** MSP at least 1.5 times of cost (A2+FL)\n# Achievements\n- **Self-sufficiency** in wheat and rice\n- **Higher production** and farm income security\n- **Buffer stock** and food security\n- **Control over price fluctuation**\n- Spread of HYV technology\n# Shortcomings\n- **Benefit limited to few crops** (wheat, rice) and regions (Punjab, Haryana, MP)\n- **Shanta Kumar Committee (2015):** only about 6 percent of farmers sell at MSP\n- **Pulses and oilseeds** are neglected\n- **Large stocks,** storage cost, wastage\n- **Fiscal burden** of food subsidy\n- **Distorts cropping pattern,** water-guzzling crops\n- **Middlemen and weak procurement** in many states\n- Low awareness among small farmers\n# Reforms and Recent Steps\n- **PM-AASHA (2018):** price support, price deficiency payment\n- **Bhavantar Bhugtan Yojana (MP)**\n- **e-NAM (2016):** national agricultural market\n- **Farm laws 2020** (repealed 2021)\n- Expansion of procurement for pulses and oilseeds\n> Conclusion: Price policy protected farmers and ensured food security, but it needs diversification and better coverage.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit I - Pre-classical", "q": "Critically examine the economic ideas of the Physiocrats.", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Physiocracy:** from Greek physis (nature) and kratos (rule) - rule of nature\n- **Founder:** Francois Quesnay (1694-1774), court physician of Louis XV\n- **Followers:** Mirabeau, Turgot, Dupont de Nemours (coined the word \"physiocratie\"), Mercier de la Riviere, Gournay\n- **Period:** mid-18th century France\n- **Background:** reaction against **Mercantilism** and Colbertism, decline of French agriculture, heavy taxes on farmers\n- **Key works:** Tableau Economique (1758), Maxims of Economic Government, Turgot's Reflections on the Formation and Distribution of Wealth (1766)\n# Main Ideas\n- **1. Natural order:** economic life is governed by natural laws set by God\n- **2. Land is the only source of wealth:** nature (land) alone gives surplus\n- **3. Agriculture alone is productive:** industry and trade only transform wealth\n- **4. Produit net (net product):** output of agriculture minus cost of production; the surplus goes as rent to landlords\n- **5. Three classes of society:**\n- **Productive class:** farmers and agricultural labourers\n- **Proprietary class:** landlords, king, church\n- **Sterile class:** artisans, merchants, manufacturers\n- **6. Circular flow of income:** money and goods move among the three classes\n- **7. Single tax (impot unique):** a single tax on landlords' net product\n- **8. Laissez faire, laissez passer:** freedom of trade, no state interference\n- **9. Private property and individual liberty** as natural rights\n- **10. Free internal trade in grain** and end of guild restrictions\n- **11. High price of agricultural products** to help farmers' capital formation\n- **12. Capital (avances):** original, annual and ground advances\n- **13. Role of the state:** protect life, property and contracts, enforce the natural order\n- **14. Enlightened despotism:** a strong, educated ruler to enforce natural law\n- **15. Against Mercantilism:** wealth is not gold and silver, but land produce\n# Tableau Economique (1758)\n- **A zig-zag diagram** showing circulation of income among classes\n- **Farmers** pay rent to landlords, buy goods from artisans\n- **Landlords** spend on agricultural products and manufactured goods\n- **Artisans** buy raw material and food from farmers\n- **Money returns** to farmers, ready for the next year\n- **Equilibrium** if flow is repeated each year\n- **First macro model** of the economy\n# Criticism\n- **Wrong to call industry and trade sterile**\n- **Land only source of value,** ignoring labour and capital\n- **Single tax is impractical** and heavy on landlords\n- **Based on French conditions,** narrow view\n- **Natural order** is assumed without proof\n- **Ignores role of foreign trade**\n- **Ignores division of labour** (Smith)\n- **Unrealistic distinction** of classes\n- **Political:** enlightened despotism contradicts liberty\n# Contributions\n- **First systematic school** of economics\n- **Idea of surplus,** basis of Ricardo and Marx\n- **Circular flow** and input-output analysis (Leontief)\n- **Laissez-faire** philosophy, free trade\n- **Influence on Adam Smith,** Wealth of Nations (1776)\n- **Single tax:** Henry George later\n- **Marx called Quesnay** the father of modern political economy\n> Conclusion: Physiocrats erred about industry, but they gave economics a scientific start and the idea of a self-regulating economy.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit I - Classical", "q": "Describe Malthus' ideas on population and over-production (general glut).", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Thomas Robert Malthus (1766-1834):** English clergyman, professor at East India College (Haileybury)\n- **Famous works:** **Essay on the Principle of Population (1798)**, 2nd edition 1803, **Principles of Political Economy (1820)**\n- **Other works:** Inquiry into Rent (1815), Observations on Corn Laws (1814)\n- **Context:** population growth, French Revolution, Poor Laws, Godwin's utopia\n# Theory of Population\n- **Population increases in geometric ratio** (1, 2, 4, 8, 16) every 25 years\n- **Food supply increases in arithmetic ratio** (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\n- **Law of diminishing returns** on land supports this view\n- **Population is limited by means of subsistence**\n- **Population tends to press** against food supply\n- **Gap leads to poverty,** vice and misery\n# Checks to Population\n- **Preventive checks (foresight):**\n- **Moral restraint:** late marriage and celibacy (added in 2nd edition)\n- **Vice:** birth control, prostitution (condemned by Malthus)\n- **Positive checks (nature):**\n- **Famine, disease, epidemics, war**\n- **Misery** that raises death rate\n- **Result:** population adjusts to food supply\n# Application of the Theory\n- **Opposed English Poor Laws:** relief raises births and worsens poverty\n- **Opposed Godwin and Condorcet's** utopian ideas\n- **Subsistence theory of wages** was supported\n# Other Contributions\n- **Theory of rent (1815):** rent arises from fertility and scarcity (published with Ricardo and West)\n- **Theory of value:** demand and supply\n- **Support for Corn Laws:** protection to agriculture\n- **Theory of general glut:** possibility of over-production\n- **Against Say's law:** supply does not create its own demand\n- **Effective demand** concept (Keynes praised Malthus)\n- **Under-consumption** explanation of crises\n- **Unproductive consumers:** landlords, church, state servants should spend\n- **Public works** to maintain demand\n- **Controversy with Ricardo** on gluts\n# Criticism of Population Theory\n- **Technology raised food supply** (green revolution)\n- **Birth rate falls** with income and education (demographic transition)\n- **Food imports** and trade ignored\n- **Rates are not fixed** geometric and arithmetic\n- **Ignores family planning** and contraception\n- **Poor Law view** is harsh and unfair to the poor\n- **Ignores social causes** of poverty\n# Importance\n- **Influenced Darwin** and Wallace (struggle for existence)\n- **Influenced Ricardo,** wage fund\n- **Anticipated Keynes** on effective demand\n- **Basis of modern population economics** and the concept of Malthusian trap\n- **Neo-Malthusians:** limits to growth, environment\n> Conclusion: Many predictions failed, but his warnings on resources and his idea of effective demand remain valuable.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit I - Welfare", "q": "Critically analyse the marginalist approach to the theory of the firm.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Marginalist theory of the firm:** firm is a rational unit aiming at **profit maximisation**\n- **Basis:** neo-classical economics, marginal analysis\n- **Decision rule:** equate marginal cost with marginal revenue\n# Assumptions\n- Single product firm, owner-manager (entrepreneur)\n- **Profit maximisation** is the only goal\n- **Perfect knowledge** of demand and cost\n- Rational behaviour and given objectives\n- Short run (fixed plant) and long run\n- Known cost and revenue curves\n# Equilibrium Conditions\n- **First order condition:** MC = MR\n[[MC = MR]]\n- **Second order condition:** MC cuts MR from below\n- Profit = TR minus TC is maximum at this output\n- Under perfect competition **P = MR = MC**\n- Under monopoly **P is above MC**\n# Short Run and Long Run\n- Short run: firm may earn supernormal profit, normal profit or loss, shut down if P < AVC\n- Long run: in perfect competition, P = LAC = LMC\n# Hall and Hitch (1939) Criticism\n- Surveyed **38 firms** in Oxford\n- Firms do not know MC and MR curves\n- They follow **full-cost pricing** (average cost plus mark-up)\n- Prices are stable, not changed by small change in demand\n# Other Criticisms\n- **Baumol:** firms maximise **sales revenue** subject to minimum profit\n- **Marris:** firms maximise **growth rate**\n- **Williamson:** managers maximise **utility** (staff, perquisites)\n- **Simon:** firms are **satisficers,** not maximisers\n- **Cyert and March:** behavioural theory, multiple goals\n- **Berle and Means:** separation of ownership and control\n- Uncertainty and imperfect information\n- Interdependence in oligopoly is ignored\n- Difficulty in measuring MC and MR\n# Defence of Marginalism\n- **Machlup:** firms do not calculate MC and MR but behave as if they do\n- **Friedman:** judge theory by predictions, not assumptions\n- **Competition** forces firms to maximise profits (survival)\n- Gives a simple and powerful basis for analysis\n# Importance\n- Foundation of price theory, supply, factor pricing\n> Conclusion: Marginalism is a good base model, but it needs behavioural and managerial theories to complete it.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Explain price leadership and the dominant firm model of price leadership.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Price leadership:** one firm in an oligopoly sets the price and others follow\n- It is a **tacit (informal) understanding,** no formal agreement\n- **Aim:** to avoid price war and reduce uncertainty\n- **Examples:** steel, cement, cigarettes, oil in the past\n# Types of Price Leadership\n- **Dominant firm (low cost) leadership:** the largest firm leads\n- **Barometric leadership:** a firm that predicts the market well leads (not the largest)\n- **Collusive leadership:** leader is chosen under an agreement\n- **Exploitative leadership:** a strong firm forces others to follow\n# Dominant Firm Model: Assumptions\n- One **large firm** and many **small firms**\n- **Homogeneous** product\n- **Small firms are price takers,** they follow the leader price\n- The large firm knows the market demand and the supply of small firms\n- Large firm has **lower costs**\n# Working of the Model\n- **Market demand curve (DD)** is known\n- **Supply of small firms (sum of their MC curves)** at each price is found\n- **Leader's demand = market demand minus small firms' supply**\n- Leader's demand curve is the **residual demand curve**\n- Leader derives **MR** from this residual demand\n- Leader sets output where **MC = MR**\n[[Leader: MC(L) = MR(L)]]\n- This gives leader's price **P***\n- Small firms take P* and produce where **their MC = P***\n# Result\n- **Total output = leader's output + small firms' output**\n- It equals market demand at P*\n- Leader has a large share, small firms have the rest\n# Merits\n- Explains stable pricing in oligopoly\n- Avoids price war\n# Demerits\n- Leader loses share in the long run (small firms grow)\n- **Entry of new firms** affects stability\n- Assumes small firms always follow\n- Barometric leadership is hard to identify\n- Ignores product differentiation\n# Evaluation\n- Legal restrictions (Competition Act) can affect it\n> Conclusion: Price leadership is a practical way to avoid price war while keeping the market stable.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Discuss the nature of oligopoly. Examine Bertrand model of duopoly and say why it is better than Cournot model.", "y": "2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Oligopoly:** market with a few sellers, each with a significant share\n- **Types:** pure (homogeneous, e.g. cement) and differentiated (e.g. cars)\n- **Duopoly:** special case of two sellers\n# Nature (Features) of Oligopoly\n- **Few sellers,** big in size\n- **Interdependence:** each firm considers rivals' reactions\n- **Barriers to entry:** capital, patents, scale\n- **Price rigidity:** prices are sticky\n- **Non-price competition:** advertising, quality\n- **Indeterminate demand curve:** reactions of rivals unknown\n- **Group behaviour:** collusion or competition\n- **Uncertainty** about rivals' policy\n- **No single theory** of oligopoly\n# Bertrand Model (1883)\n- **Joseph Bertrand** criticised Cournot\n- **Decision variable is price,** not output\n- **Assumptions:** two firms, identical product and same cost, firms take rival's **price** as given, consumers buy from the cheaper seller\n# Working\n- Firm A sets monopoly price\n- B sets slightly lower price and captures the whole market\n- A then undercuts B\n- Price cutting continues till **P = MC**\n[[Equilibrium: P = MC ; profit = 0]]\n- No firm will lower price below MC\n# Result\n- **Price equals the competitive price,** profits are zero\n- Two firms are enough to get a competitive result (Bertrand paradox)\n# Comparison with Cournot\n- **Cournot:** output is the variable, positive profits\n- **Bertrand:** price is the variable, zero profit\n- Cournot: reaction curves slope down. Bertrand: reaction curves slope up\n# Is Bertrand Better?\n- **Yes:** firms in practice announce prices, so price is a realistic variable\n- **No:** ignores capacity limits (Edgeworth), product differentiation\n- With capacity limits, there is no pure strategy equilibrium\n- Result of P = MC is not seen in the real world\n# Criticism\n- Assumes undercutting without limit\n- Ignores collusion and dynamic behaviour\n- Product homogeneity is assumed\n> Conclusion: Bertrand is a useful improvement on Cournot, but both are partial models of oligopoly.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit V - Growth and Cycles", "q": "Explain Harrod growth model: types of growth rates (warranted, actual, natural).", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **R. F. Harrod,** An Essay in Dynamic Theory (1939)\n- **Aim:** to find the growth rate for steady full-capacity growth\n- **Extension of Keynes** to the long run (Keynes: short run)\n- **Domar** gave a similar model, hence the **Harrod-Domar model**\n# Assumptions\n- Closed economy, no government\n- Saving is a **fixed proportion (s) of income**\n- **Capital-output ratio** is constant\n- No depreciation, no technical change\n- Full employment initially\n- Labour and capital are in fixed proportion\n# Basic Equation\n- **Saving = Investment** for equilibrium\n[[G x C = s]]\n- G = rate of growth, C = capital-output ratio, s = propensity to save\n# Three Growth Rates\n- **Actual rate (G):** G = s / Ca (Ca = actual capital-output ratio)\n- **Warranted rate (Gw):** growth rate at which entrepreneurs are satisfied, capacity is fully used\n[[Gw = s / Cr]]\n- **Natural rate (Gn):** maximum growth permitted by **labour force and technical progress**\n# Relationship among the Rates\n- **G = Gw:** steady growth, equilibrium\n- **G > Gw:** actual demand exceeds capacity, investment rises, **cumulative boom**\n- **G < Gw:** excess capacity, investment falls, **cumulative depression**\n- **Gw > Gn:** unemployment and stagnation (secular)\n- **Gw < Gn:** full-employment boom, rising prices\n# Knife-Edge Problem\n- Equilibrium growth is **unstable**\n- Any deviation of G from Gw increases (cumulative)\n- Like balancing on a knife's edge\n- Need **G = Gw = Gn** for full-employment steady growth, which is rare\n# Policy Implications\n- **Raise saving rate** to increase growth\n- Reduce capital-output ratio by technology\n- Adjust Gw and Gn through policy\n- **Government planning** to maintain balance\n# Criticism\n- Fixed capital-output ratio and saving rate are unreal\n- Fixed factor proportions, no substitution (Solow)\n- Closed economy, no state\n- Ignores price changes, financial factors\n- Knife-edge result is a model feature, not reality\n# Importance\n- Basis of the planning model in developing countries (India Second Plan)\n- Starting point for modern growth theory\n> Conclusion: The Harrod model highlights the instability of growth, but its rigid assumptions limit practical use.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit V - Growth and Cycles", "q": "Explain Samuelson theory of trade cycle.", "y": "2017", "a": "# Introduction\n- **P. A. Samuelson (1939):** Interactions Between the Multiplier Analysis and the Principle of Acceleration\n- **Idea:** interaction of multiplier and accelerator causes income cycles\n- **Features:** mathematical model with difference equations\n# Basic Concepts\n- **Multiplier:** change in income from investment, depends on MPC (c)\n- **Accelerator (v):** induced investment depends on change in consumption\n- **Lag:** consumption depends on the previous period's income\n# Model\n[[Yt = Ct + It + Gt]]\n[[Ct = c Y(t-1)]]\n[[It = v (Ct - C(t-1))]]\n- Government spending G is constant\n- Combine into a second-order difference equation\n# Working\n- A rise in G raises income (multiplier)\n- Higher income raises consumption\n- Higher consumption raises induced investment (accelerator)\n- That raises income again\n- When consumption growth slows, investment falls, income falls\n# Four Possible Paths\n- **Damped non-cyclical:** income moves smoothly to a new level\n- **Damped cyclical:** cycles die out gradually\n- **Explosive cyclical:** cycles become larger\n- **Explosive non-cyclical:** income rises or falls steadily\n# Which Path Depends on c and v\n- **Low c, low v:** damped path\n- **High v:** explosive path\n- **Cycles occur** when v is large relative to c\n- **c and v values** give four regions in a diagram\n# Merits\n- Shows cycles can be **internally generated**\n- Combines two important concepts\n- Gives clear numerical analysis\n# Criticism\n- Fixed c and v are unrealistic\n- **No ceiling or floor,** so explosive cycle never ends (Hicks improved this)\n- Ignores money, prices, expectations\n- No actual turning points\n- Accelerator works only with spare capacity\n# Importance\n- Starting point for cycle theories (Hicks, Goodwin)\n> Conclusion: Samuelson's model explains how cycles arise from internal forces, but it is a limited and mechanical explanation.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit IV - Money and IS-LM", "q": "What is general equilibrium? Describe any one model of general equilibrium (Walras).", "y": "2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Leon Walras** (Elements of Pure Economics, 1874)\n- **Partial equilibrium (Marshall):** one market, others constant\n- **General equilibrium:** all markets in equilibrium **simultaneously**\n- **Reason:** markets are interdependent\n# Basic Idea\n- **Demand for a good** depends on the prices of all goods and factors\n- **Supply** depends on all prices\n- Equilibrium is a set of prices at which **all markets clear**\n# Walrasian Model\n- Economy with **households and firms**\n- **Consumers** maximise utility, **firms** maximise profits\n- **Four types of markets:** products, factors, capital, money\n- **Numeraire:** one commodity as the unit for expressing prices\n- Number of equations is equal to number of unknowns\n# Equations\n- n goods, n-1 independent prices\n- **Demand = supply** for each good\n- **Budget constraint** of each household\n- **Zero profit** condition for each firm (in the long run)\n# Walras' Law\n- **Sum of values of excess demands in all markets is zero**\n[[Sum Pi x Ei = 0]]\n- So if n-1 markets are in equilibrium, the nth is also in equilibrium\n- Money market is in the model (the numeraire)\n# Tatonnement Process\n- **Auctioneer** announces a price\n- Excess demand leads to a rise in price, excess supply leads to a fall\n- No trade till equilibrium is reached\n- Process continues till excess demand is zero\n# Existence, Uniqueness, Stability\n- **Existence:** Arrow and Debreu (1954) proved it with fixed-point theorems\n- **Uniqueness:** need conditions on demand (gross substitutes)\n- **Stability:** equilibrium should be reached by adjustment\n# Welfare Aspects\n- **First welfare theorem:** competitive equilibrium is Pareto optimal\n- **Second welfare theorem:** any Pareto optimum can be reached by redistribution\n# Criticism\n- Perfect competition and perfect information are unrealistic\n- Static, no time or uncertainty\n- Auctioneer is imaginary\n- Ignores externalities, public goods, monopoly\n- Money plays a minor role\n- Very high abstraction\n# Importance\n- Foundation of modern theory, welfare economics, international trade and macro microfoundations\n> Conclusion: General equilibrium offers a powerful framework, though based on strong assumptions.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit III - Terms of Trade", "q": "What do you mean by gains from trade? Gains from exchange and gains from specialisation.", "y": "2016, 2018, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Gains from trade:** benefits that a country gets from trade compared to no trade (autarky)\n- **Discussed by:** Smith, Ricardo, Mill, Marshall, Haberler\n# Sources of Gains\n- **Gains from exchange:** consumers get goods at a cost lower than domestic cost\n- **Gains from specialisation:** world output rises as countries produce where comparative advantage lies\n- **Both together** make the total gain\n# Gains from Exchange\n- Terms of trade differ from the domestic price ratio\n- A country exports a good and gets more imports per unit than it can produce at home\n- Production is **fixed** at the pre-trade point, consumption moves to a higher indifference curve\n- Example: India 1 cloth = 1 wheat, world 1 cloth = 2 wheat, so India gains one wheat per cloth\n# Gains from Specialisation\n- Country specialises in the good with the **lower opportunity cost**\n- **PPC production point moves** to the specialisation point\n- Output of the world is larger\n- Gains arise from comparative advantage\n# Ricardian Example\n- Labour hours: England: cloth 100, wine 120. Portugal: cloth 90, wine 80\n- Portugal has absolute advantage in both\n- England has comparative advantage in cloth, Portugal in wine\n- Both gain by specialisation\n# Division of Gains\n- **Depends on terms of trade**\n- TOT closer to the other country's domestic ratio means more gain for you\n- **Mill:** depends on reciprocal demand\n- A small country usually gets a larger share of the gain\n# Measurement of Gains\n- Gains in terms of indifference curves (community)\n- Increase in real income\n# Dynamic Gains\n- **Economies of scale** from a larger market\n- **Transfer of technology** and ideas\n- **Competition** raises efficiency\n- **Investment and growth**\n- **Wider choice,** better quality\n# Limitations (Arguments Against)\n- Distribution of gains unequal\n- **Terms of trade** may worsen for developing countries (Prebisch)\n- Dependence on exports of primary goods\n- Infant industry may suffer\n- Domestic unemployment due to imports\n> Conclusion: Trade raises welfare for both countries, but the division of gains depends on terms of trade.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Discuss the merits and demerits of fixed and flexible exchange rates.", "y": "2016, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Exchange rate:** price of one currency in terms of another\n- **Fixed exchange rate:** rate is fixed by the government or central bank (gold standard, Bretton Woods 1944-71)\n- **Flexible exchange rate:** rate is determined by **demand and supply** in the forex market\n- **Intermediate systems:** managed float, crawling peg, currency board\n# Merits of Fixed Exchange Rate\n- **Stability and certainty,** helps trade and investment\n- **Encourages long-term capital flows**\n- **Checks speculation** and instability\n- **Anti-inflationary discipline** on government\n- **Easy planning** for trade and prices\n- **International cooperation** (IMF system)\n# Demerits of Fixed Exchange Rate\n- **Needs large reserves** for defence of the rate\n- **Loss of independent monetary policy** (trilemma)\n- **Rigid:** disequilibrium persists, rate becomes overvalued or undervalued\n- **Black market** and exchange control\n- **Sudden devaluation** causes shock\n- **Imports inflation or deflation** from partner countries\n- Speculative attacks on currency (1992 EMS crisis)\n# Merits of Flexible Exchange Rate\n- **Automatic BOP adjustment**\n- **No need for large reserves**\n- **Policy independence** in monetary and fiscal policy\n- **Shock absorber:** currency changes absorb external shocks\n- **No exchange control,** free trade\n- **Simple, market-driven**\n# Demerits of Flexible Exchange Rate\n- **Uncertainty** for traders and investors\n- **Speculation and volatility**\n- **May cause inflation** (depreciation raises import cost)\n- **Hampers foreign investment**\n- **Destabilising** for small developing countries\n- **Competitive depreciation** possible\n# Mundell's Trilemma\n- A country cannot have fixed exchange rate, free capital flow and independent monetary policy together\n# Middle Path\n- **Managed float:** market determines, central bank intervenes\n- **Crawling peg:** small regular changes\n- India follows managed float since 1993\n> Conclusion: Neither system is perfect; most countries today use managed flexibility.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Explain devaluation, its objectives and the Marshall-Lerner condition.", "y": "2018, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Devaluation:** official lowering of the value of the currency in terms of foreign currency (fixed exchange rate system)\n- **Depreciation:** fall due to market forces (flexible rate)\n- **Revaluation:** opposite of devaluation\n# Objectives\n- **Correct BOP deficit**\n- **Increase exports** and discourage imports\n- **Promote employment** and domestic industry\n- **Increase foreign exchange reserves**\n- **Remove overvaluation** of currency\n- **Reduce trade deficit**\n# Working\n- Exports become cheaper in foreign currency, so foreign demand rises\n- Imports become costlier, so import demand falls\n- Trade balance improves if demand is elastic\n# Marshall-Lerner Condition\n[[Ex + Em > 1]]\n- Devaluation improves BOP if **sum of elasticities of demand for exports and imports is greater than one**\n- If sum is equal to one, no change. If less than one, BOP worsens\n# Robinson-Metzler (Bickerdike) Condition\n- Includes supply elasticities also\n# J-Curve Effect\n- **Short run:** trade deficit worsens (contracts fixed, quantities do not adjust)\n- **Long run:** deficit falls as quantities adjust\n- The curve looks like the letter J\n# Favourable Conditions\n- Elastic demand and supply\n- Spare capacity\n- Others do not retaliate\n- Domestic prices do not rise\n# Limitations\n- **Inflation** from costlier imports\n- **Retaliation** (competitive devaluation)\n- Low elasticities in developing countries\n- **Debt burden** rises (foreign loans)\n- Supply constraints\n- Uncertainty and loss of confidence\n# Absorption View\n- Devaluation works only if domestic absorption is cut\n# India's Experience\n- **1949:** after sterling devaluation\n- **1966:** devaluation by about 57 percent, unsuccessful\n- **1991:** two-step downward adjustment of about 18-19 percent as part of reforms\n> Conclusion: Devaluation can correct BOP deficit only under favourable elasticity and supporting policies.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "Discuss functions, objectives and achievements of IMF. How has India benefitted?", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **IMF:** International Monetary Fund\n- **Established:** Bretton Woods Conference, 1944 (came into being 1945, began operations 1947)\n- **Headquarters:** Washington DC\n- **Members:** about 190 countries\n- **Sister organisation:** World Bank\n# Objectives (Article I)\n- **Promote international monetary cooperation**\n- **Facilitate expansion of world trade** and balanced growth\n- **Promote exchange rate stability** and orderly arrangements\n- **Establish a multilateral payments system,** remove exchange restrictions\n- **Give temporary financial help** to countries in BOP difficulty\n- **Shorten and reduce** the degree of BOP disequilibrium\n# Functions\n- **Financial function:** lending to members through quotas, facilities\n- **Regulatory function:** code of conduct on exchange rate policies\n- **Surveillance:** Article IV consultation, monitoring of economies\n- **Technical assistance** and training\n- **Creation of reserve asset:** SDR (1969)\n- **Research and data:** World Economic Outlook, statistics\n# Resources\n- **Quota subscriptions** of members (basis of voting, borrowing)\n- **Borrowing** (GAB, NAB)\n- **SDR allocation**\n- **Gold sales**\n# Lending Facilities\n- **Stand-By Arrangement (SBA)**\n- **Extended Fund Facility (EFF)**\n- **Flexible Credit Line**\n- **Rapid Financing Instrument**\n- **Poverty Reduction and Growth Trust** (concessional)\n# Achievements\n- Helped many countries in BOP crises\n- Stability in exchange rates for a long time (till 1971)\n- Created SDR as an international reserve asset\n- Helped in the debt crises of the 1980s, Asian crisis and 2008-09 crisis\n- Supported the transition economies\n- Standard of economic data and research\n# Criticism\n- **Conditionality** is harsh (austerity), hurts the poor\n- **Dominated by rich countries** (voting by quota)\n- Failed in the East Asian crisis (1997)\n- One-size-fits-all policy\n- Failed to prevent the breakdown of Bretton Woods\n# India and the IMF\n- Founder member\n- Borrowed in 1981 (EFF, about SDR 5 billion) and in 1991 (BOP crisis)\n- No borrowing in recent decades\n- India seeks greater quota and voice\n> Conclusion: The IMF plays a vital role in global stability, but needs governance reform and flexible conditions.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "Distinguish between Foreign Direct Investment and Portfolio Investment.", "y": "2018, 2022", "a": "# Meaning\n- **FDI:** investment by a foreign entity in a business with **lasting interest and control** (generally 10 percent or more equity)\n- **FPI:** investment in **shares, bonds and securities** for returns, **without control** (less than 10 percent)\n# Differences\n- **Control:** FDI gives management control, FPI does not\n- **Time horizon:** FDI is long-term, FPI is short-term\n- **Nature of capital:** FDI is physical (plants, machines) plus technology; FPI is financial\n- **Stability:** FDI is stable; FPI is volatile (hot money)\n- **Risk:** FDI risk is borne by the investor in the business; FPI can exit quickly\n- **Impact on growth:** FDI raises output, employment and technology; FPI affects stock markets and liquidity\n- **Exit:** FDI is difficult to withdraw; FPI easy to sell\n- **Return:** FDI gets profit and dividends; FPI gets capital gains and dividends\n- **Technology transfer:** FDI yes; FPI no\n- **Regulation in India:** FDI by FEMA and FDI policy (automatic route and approval route); FPI by SEBI (FPI Regulations)\n- **Role of host government:** FDI sector caps; FPI investment limits (aggregate)\n# Merits of FDI\n- Capital inflow and technology\n- Employment, exports and competition\n- Management skills and global linkages\n- Stable source of foreign exchange\n# Demerits of FDI\n- Profit repatriation, outflow of dividends\n- Dominance of multinational companies, harms domestic industry\n- Environmental and cultural concerns\n- Political influence\n# Merits of FPI\n- Liquidity in markets, lower cost of capital\n- Deepening of capital markets\n- Quick supply of foreign capital\n# Demerits of FPI\n- **Sudden outflow** can cause a currency or stock market crash (1997 Asian crisis, 2013 taper tantrum)\n- Exchange rate volatility\n- Speculation and no real addition to capacity\n# India\n- FDI in sectors through automatic route, caps in some (defence, insurance)\n- FPI replaced FII (2014 SEBI norms)\n> Conclusion: FDI is preferred for development, FPI is useful but needs careful monitoring.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Discuss opportunity cost theory of Haberler.", "y": "2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Gottfried Haberler** (The Theory of International Trade, 1933)\n- **Background:** Ricardo's comparative cost theory used the **labour theory of value,** which is defective\n- **Haberler's contribution:** restated comparative advantage in terms of **opportunity cost**\n- **Opportunity cost:** quantity of a good given up to produce one more unit of another\n# Assumptions\n- Two countries, two goods, two factors\n- Perfect competition, full employment\n- Free trade, no transport cost\n- Factors are mobile within, immobile between countries\n- Production possibility curve (PPC) is given\n- Technology is given\n# Production Possibility Curve\n- **PPC shows** the maximum combination of two goods with given resources\n- **Slope of PPC** at any point = marginal rate of transformation (MRT)\n- **MRT = opportunity cost** of one good in terms of another\n# Cases\n- **Constant opportunity cost:** straight line PPC (Ricardo's case)\n- **Increasing opportunity cost:** concave PPC (realistic)\n- **Decreasing cost:** convex (unusual)\n# Comparative Advantage\n- **A country has comparative advantage** in the good whose opportunity cost is lower\n- It exports that good and imports the other\n- Example: India: 1 cloth = 2 wheat. Britain: 1 cloth = 1 wheat. India has an advantage in wheat, Britain in cloth\n# Equilibrium with Trade\n- **Price ratio** (terms of trade) is between the two domestic opportunity cost ratios\n- Production is where **MRT = price ratio**\n- Consumption is where **indifference curve is tangent to price line**\n- Country gains by moving to a higher indifference curve\n# Gains from Trade\n- Exchange gains and specialisation gains\n- Both countries gain\n# Merits\n- Free from labour theory of value\n- Includes all factors of production\n- Realistic: increasing costs\n- Uses PPC and indifference curve, more general\n- Explains complete and partial specialisation\n# Criticism\n- Does not explain why opportunity costs differ (needs Heckscher-Ohlin)\n- Static and ignores economies of scale\n- Assumes perfect competition\n- Ignores transport and tariffs\n- Based on a two-country model\n> Conclusion: Haberler generalised Ricardo's theory by using opportunity cost, giving a stronger theoretical base.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "What are Regional Trade Agreements? Discuss their effects.", "y": "2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **RTA:** agreement among a group of countries to reduce or remove barriers to trade within the group\n- **Regionalism vs multilateralism:** WTO allows RTAs under **GATT Article XXIV**\n- **Examples:** EU, USMCA (formerly NAFTA), ASEAN, SAFTA, Mercosur\n# Stages (Forms) of Integration\n- **Preferential Trade Area:** lower tariffs among members (India-Afghanistan)\n- **Free Trade Area:** no tariffs inside, each keeps its own external tariff (SAFTA, NAFTA)\n- **Customs Union:** free trade inside plus **common external tariff** (Mercosur)\n- **Common Market:** free movement of labour and capital also (EEC earlier)\n- **Economic Union:** common economic policy (EU)\n- **Monetary Union:** common currency (Eurozone)\n# Static Effects (Viner)\n- **Trade creation:** shift from high-cost domestic producer to low-cost partner, welfare rises\n- **Trade diversion:** shift from low-cost outsider to high-cost partner, welfare falls\n- **Net effect** depends on the relative size\n# Dynamic Effects\n- **Economies of scale** in a larger market\n- **More competition,** efficiency\n- **Investment** inflow, FDI\n- **Technology transfer**\n- **Growth** and employment\n# Merits\n- Expands the market and trade\n- Political and strategic cooperation\n- Faster tariff reduction than WTO\n- Attracts FDI\n# Demerits\n- **Discrimination** against non-members\n- **Trade diversion** and inefficiency\n- Weakens **multilateral system** (spaghetti bowl effect: overlapping rules)\n- Loss of sovereignty\n- Unequal gains for small members\n- Revenue loss\n# India's RTAs\n- SAFTA, India-ASEAN FTA\n- CEPA with Japan, South Korea, UAE (2022)\n- India-Australia ECTA (2022)\n- India stayed out of **RCEP** (2019)\n# Evaluation\n- RTAs are a second-best option; their gain depends on trade creation\n> Conclusion: RTAs can raise welfare if trade creation dominates, but they can also harm global free trade.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "Discuss the National Agriculture Policy and causes of low agricultural growth.", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Agriculture:** employs about 45 percent of workers, contributes about 15-18 percent of GDP\n- **National Agriculture Policy, 2000:** first comprehensive policy\n- **Target:** growth rate of **more than 4 percent per annum**\n- **National Policy for Farmers 2007:** focus on farmers' welfare (after Swaminathan Commission)\n# Objectives of the Policy 2000\n- Achieve **over 4 percent annual growth**\n- **Growth based on efficient use of resources,** conserve soil, water, biodiversity\n- **Equitable growth** across regions and farmers\n- **Demand-driven** and technology-oriented agriculture\n- **Sustainable** development\n- Compete in **global markets** (WTO)\n# Main Features\n- **Rural infrastructure:** roads, markets, storage, power\n- **Land and water use,** soil conservation, watershed\n- **Credit:** KCC, institutional credit\n- **Risk management:** crop insurance\n- **Agro-processing** and value addition\n- **Price protection:** MSP, market intervention\n- **Research and extension:** ICAR, KVKs\n- **Cooperatives and Panchayats** role\n- **Export promotion** of agricultural products\n- **Bio-technology** and organic farming\n- **Participation of women and tribal farmers**\n# Causes of Low Agricultural Growth\n- **Monsoon dependence;** only about half of net sown area is irrigated\n- **Small and fragmented holdings** (average size about 1.1 hectare)\n- **Low productivity** compared to world standards\n- **Low public investment** in irrigation, roads, storage\n- **Inadequate credit,** moneylender dependence\n- **Weak marketing,** middlemen, price fluctuation\n- **Incomplete land reforms,** tenancy\n- **Use of old technology,** low seed replacement\n- **Soil degradation,** overuse of fertilisers, water table fall\n- **Climate change** risks\n- **Poor extension services**\n# Measures Taken\n- **PMKSY** (irrigation), **PMFBY** (insurance, 2016)\n- **Soil Health Card (2015)**\n- **e-NAM,** FPOs\n- **PM-KISAN (2019):** Rs 6,000 per year income support\n- **Agriculture Infrastructure Fund (2020)**\n- **Doubling Farmers' Income** target by 2022 (Dalwai Committee)\n- **KCC,** MSP hike, micro irrigation\n# Suggestions\n- Increase investment, irrigation, market reforms\n- Diversification to horticulture, livestock, fisheries\n- Technology, extension and farmer training\n- Land consolidation, FPOs and contract farming\n> Conclusion: Growth in agriculture needs investment, technology and market reform together.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit II - Industry", "q": "Discuss the major components of Industrial Policy development (1956, 1991).", "y": "2016, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Industrial policy:** government's rules and strategy for industrial growth\n- **Aim:** build industrial base, self-reliance, employment, balanced regional growth\n# Industrial Policy Resolution 1948\n- **First industrial policy,** after independence\n- **Mixed economy** accepted\n- **Four categories:** state monopoly (arms, atomic energy, railways); mixed (coal, iron and steel, shipbuilding, mineral oils); government-regulated private sector (18 key industries); and the rest left to private sector\n- Importance to **cottage and small industries**\n- **Foreign capital** allowed with regulation\n# Industrial Policy Resolution 1956 (Economic Constitution)\n- **Socialist pattern of society** (Avadi 1955)\n- **Schedule A:** 17 industries, exclusive state responsibility\n- **Schedule B:** 12 industries, progressively state-owned, private supplements\n- **Schedule C:** rest, private sector\n- Emphasis on **heavy industry** (Mahalanobis model)\n- **Small scale and cottage industry** protection\n- **Reduction of regional inequalities**\n- Prevention of monopoly\n# Laws of the Control Period\n- **Industries (Development and Regulation) Act 1951:** licensing\n- **MRTP Act 1969:** control on monopolies\n- **FERA 1973:** control on foreign exchange\n# Policies of 1970s and 1980s\n- **1973:** big houses allowed in core and heavy industry, joint sector\n- **1977 (Janata):** emphasis on small and tiny sector, **District Industries Centres**\n- **1980:** promotion of competition, modernisation\n- **1985-88:** partial liberalisation, **MRTP limit raised to Rs 100 crore, broad-banding, MODVAT**\n# New Industrial Policy 1991\n- **Abolition of industrial licensing,** except for 18 industries\n- **Public sector reserved industries cut** to 8 (now very few)\n- **MRTP limits removed,** only unfair trade practice\n- **FDI:** automatic approval up to 51 percent in 34 high priority industries\n- **Foreign technology agreements:** automatic permission\n- **Disinvestment** of PSUs\n- **Sick units:** BIFR, policy on revival\n- **Small sector** incentives\n# Later Developments\n- **Make in India (2014),** Start-up India (2016)\n- **MSME Development Act 2006**\n- **National Manufacturing Policy 2011:** 25 percent share of manufacturing in GDP\n- **PLI schemes (2020)**\n- **Atmanirbhar Bharat,** corporate tax cut\n- **GST** (2017), IBC (2016)\n# Evaluation\n- **Merits:** competition, growth, exports, quality, investment, IT boom\n- **Demerits:** jobless growth, weak MSMEs, regional imbalance, share of manufacturing stagnant at about 15 percent\n> Conclusion: Policy moved from control to liberalisation, but manufacturing and employment still need push.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit III - Poverty and Population", "q": "Define poverty. Explain the strategy adopted by India to remove poverty.", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Poverty:** inability to meet minimum basic needs (food, clothing, shelter, health, education)\n- **Absolute poverty:** below a **poverty line** (minimum consumption)\n- **Relative poverty:** compared with other groups\n- **Multidimensional poverty:** deprivation in health, education, living standard\n# Measurement in India\n- **Poverty line:** based on calorie norm (2400 rural, 2100 urban)\n- **Head Count Ratio (HCR):** share of population below the line\n- **Alagh (1979), Lakdawala (1993), Tendulkar (2009), Rangarajan (2014)** committees\n- **Tendulkar:** 21.9 percent in 2011-12 (Rangarajan estimate 29.5 percent)\n- **NITI Aayog MPI:** about 15 percent in 2019-21 (down from about 25 percent in 2015-16)\n# Causes of Poverty\n- **Low growth** in the past, population pressure\n- **Unemployment and underemployment**\n- **Inequality** in income and assets\n- **Illiteracy,** poor health\n- **Low agricultural productivity,** landlessness\n- **Inflation,** regional imbalance\n- **Social factors:** caste, gender\n- **Weak implementation** of schemes\n# Strategy to Remove Poverty\n- **Growth strategy:** higher GDP growth, agriculture, industry, services\n- **Land reforms:** abolition of zamindari, tenancy, ceiling\n- **Self-employment:** IRDP (1978), SGSY (1999), **NRLM (2011)**, PM Mudra\n- **Wage employment:** NREP, JRY, **MGNREGA 2005:** 100 days of work\n- **Food security:** **PDS, NFSA 2013,** mid-day meal\n- **Social security:** NSAP (old-age pension), PM Jeevan Jyoti\n- **Housing:** Indira Awas, **PMAY**\n- **Basic needs:** Jal Jeevan Mission, Ujjwala (2016), Saubhagya\n- **Financial inclusion:** **Jan Dhan Yojana (2014),** DBT\n- **Education and health:** Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan, National Health Mission, Ayushman Bharat\n- **Skill development:** Skill India, DDU-GKY\n- **Urban poverty:** SJSRY, DAY-NULM\n- **Empowerment:** SHGs, Panchayati Raj\n# Evaluation\n- Poverty fell sharply since 1991, but regional gap persists (Bihar, UP, Jharkhand)\n- **Leakages** and weak targeting\n- Overlap of schemes\n- Need for quality jobs\n> Conclusion: Growth with employment, human development and effective delivery is the lasting solution.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit III - Poverty and Population", "q": "Write a note on development of population policy in India.", "y": "2016, 2017", "a": "# Introduction\n- **India's population:** over 140 crore, most populous country (2023)\n- **First country** to launch a family planning programme (1952)\n- **Aim of policy:** reduce fertility, improve health and stabilise population\n# Stages of Development\n- **1952:** National Family Planning Programme launched\n- **1950s-60s:** clinic approach, then extension approach, Lippes loop\n- **1966:** Ministry of Health and Family Planning, targets\n- **1976: first National Population Policy** (Emergency)\n- **1977:** renamed **Family Welfare Programme** (voluntary approach)\n- **1983:** National Health Policy, replacement level goals\n- **1993-94:** Expert group (Swaminathan Committee) for new policy\n- **1996:** target-free approach, **Reproductive and Child Health (RCH)**\n- **2000: National Population Policy**\n- **2005:** National Rural Health Mission\n- **2017:** National Health Policy\n# NPP 1976\n- **Raised age of marriage:** 18 for girls, 21 for boys\n- **Incentives** and disincentives, freeze on Lok Sabha seats\n- **Sterilisation** drive, which became coercive\n- **Backlash** after Emergency\n# NPP 2000: Objectives\n- **Immediate:** meet unmet needs for contraception, health infrastructure and personnel\n- **Medium term:** bring TFR to **replacement level (2.1) by 2010**\n- **Long term:** **stabilise population by 2045**\n# NPP 2000: Targets\n- **IMR** below 30 per 1000\n- **MMR** below 100 per lakh\n- **Universal immunisation**\n- **100 percent registration** of births, deaths, marriages, pregnancy\n- **Delay marriage** to 20 for girls\n- **Universal access** to information and counselling\n- **Promote small family norm**\n- **Prevent and control** AIDS\n- **12 strategic themes**\n# Recent Steps\n- **Mission Parivar Vikas** (2016), injectable contraceptive (Antara)\n- **Two-child norm** in some state panchayats\n- **Janani Suraksha Yojana**\n# Evaluation\n- **TFR is about 2.0** (NFHS-5, 2019-21), near replacement\n- **Regional variation:** Kerala, Tamil Nadu low; Bihar, UP high\n- **Population momentum** keeps numbers rising\n- **Sex ratio** imbalance\n- Weak health services in some states\n> Conclusion: Voluntary approach combined with education, health and women's empowerment gives the best result.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit IV - Environment and Energy", "q": "Outline National Environment Policy of India.", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **National Environment Policy, 2006 (NEP):** comprehensive policy of the Ministry of Environment and Forests\n- **Aim:** **sustainable development,** balance between growth and ecology\n- **Earlier:** National Forest Policy 1988, National Conservation Strategy 1992\n# Objectives\n- **Conservation of critical environmental resources**\n- **Intra-generational equity:** livelihood for the poor\n- **Inter-generational equity**\n- **Integration** of environment into development policy\n- **Efficient use** of resources\n- **Good environmental governance**\n- **Enhance resources** for conservation\n# Guiding Principles\n- **Polluter pays principle**\n- **Precautionary principle**\n- **Public trust doctrine**\n- **Economic efficiency**\n- **Entities with comparative advantage**\n- **Equity**\n- **Legality** and decentralisation\n- **Integration**\n# Legal and Institutional Framework\n- **Wildlife (Protection) Act 1972**\n- **Water Act 1974,** Cess Act 1977\n- **Forest (Conservation) Act 1980**\n- **Air Act 1981**\n- **Environment (Protection) Act 1986**\n- **Biodiversity Act 2002**\n- **Forest Rights Act 2006**\n- **National Green Tribunal Act 2010**\n- **EIA notification 2006**\n- **CPCB and SPCBs**\n- **Article 48A and 51A(g):** state and citizens' duty\n# Climate Policy\n- **NAPCC 2008** with eight missions: Solar, Enhanced Energy Efficiency, Sustainable Habitat, Water, Himalayan Ecosystem, Green India, Sustainable Agriculture, Strategic Knowledge\n- **Paris Agreement 2015,** India's NDCs\n- **COP26 (2021):** 500 GW non-fossil capacity by 2030, **net zero by 2070**\n- **International Solar Alliance (2015)**\n# Key Issue Areas\n- **Land degradation,** forests and wildlife\n- **Biodiversity,** wetlands, coastal areas\n- **Freshwater,** air and noise pollution\n- **Waste management,** hazardous waste\n- **Mining,** urbanisation\n# Programmes\n- **Swachh Bharat Mission**\n- **Namami Gange**\n- **National Clean Air Programme (2019)**\n- **Project Tiger, Project Elephant**\n- **Compensatory Afforestation (CAMPA)**\n# Problems\n- Weak enforcement, pollution of rivers and cities\n- Conflict between development and ecology\n- Deforestation, plastic waste, climate risks\n- Poor coordination among agencies\n> Conclusion: India has a strong framework, but its enforcement and community participation need improvement.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit IV - Environment and Energy", "q": "Why is energy policy essential? Outline India energy policy.", "y": "2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Energy:** basic input for growth, industry, agriculture, transport and households\n- **India:** world's third-largest energy consumer\n- **Energy policy:** ensure secure, affordable and clean energy\n# Why Energy Policy is Essential\n- **Energy security:** India imports about 85 percent of its crude oil\n- **Growth needs:** high demand with rising GDP\n- **Universal access:** electricity and clean cooking for all\n- **Climate commitments:** reduce emissions\n- **Price stability** and subsidy management\n- **Efficient use** of limited resources\n- **Technology** and investment planning\n- **Environment protection**\n- **Reduce import bill** and trade deficit\n# Integrated Energy Policy 2006 (Kirit Parikh Committee)\n- Meet the energy needs of all sectors at **lowest cost**\n- **Energy security,** efficiency, sustainability\n- **Competitive markets,** pricing reforms\n- Targeted subsidy\n- Promotion of **energy efficiency** and R and D\n# Elements of the Policy\n- **Coal:** largest source (about half of commercial energy), clean coal, gasification\n- **Oil and gas:** NELP and HELP (exploration), **strategic petroleum reserves,** city gas, LNG imports\n- **Electricity:** **Electricity Act 2003,** open access, **UDAY (2015),** Saubhagya (2017)\n- **Renewable energy:** **National Solar Mission (2010),** wind, hydro, biomass\n- **Target:** **500 GW non-fossil capacity by 2030**\n- **Nuclear energy:** three-stage programme, Indo-US civil nuclear deal (2008)\n- **Energy efficiency:** Bureau of Energy Efficiency, **star rating, UJALA (LED)**\n- **Biofuels:** ethanol blending (target 20 percent), National Policy on Biofuels\n- **Green hydrogen:** National Green Hydrogen Mission\n- **PM-KUSUM:** solar pumps\n- **International Solar Alliance** (2015)\n- **Electric vehicles:** FAME scheme\n# Problems\n- **Dependence on coal and imported oil**\n- **Losses of electricity distribution companies (discoms)**\n- **Land and finance** for renewables\n- **Grid integration** of renewable sources\n- **Subsidies** and pricing distortions\n- **Pollution** from fossil fuels\n# Suggestions\n- Increase domestic exploration, renewables and storage\n- Reform discoms, smart grids\n- Efficiency and demand management\n- Diversify energy imports\n> Conclusion: India needs a balanced energy mix, with more renewable energy for secure and clean growth.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit V - External Sector", "q": "Discuss main features of India foreign trade policy.", "y": "2018, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Foreign Trade Policy (FTP):** policy on imports and exports of goods and services\n- **Authority:** **DGFT,** Ministry of Commerce, under the **Foreign Trade (Development and Regulation) Act 1992**\n- **Earlier:** Export-Import Policy (1992-97 onwards), renamed FTP in 2009\n- **FTP 2015-20:** MEIS, SEIS\n- **FTP 2023:** effective **1 April 2023**, no fixed end date (dynamic)\n# Objectives\n- **Increase exports** (target **2 trillion dollars by 2030**)\n- **Trade facilitation** and ease of doing business\n- **Self-reliance (Atmanirbhar Bharat)**\n- **Diversify products and markets**\n- **Boost employment**\n# Main Features of FTP 2023\n- **Four pillars:** incentive to remission, export promotion through collaboration, ease of doing business, emerging areas\n- **RoDTEP and RoSCTL:** remission of duties and taxes on exports\n- **Towns of Export Excellence:** four new towns added (Faridabad, Mirzapur, Moradabad, Varanasi)\n- **Districts as Export Hubs** initiative\n- **Merchanting trade** allowed\n- **Amnesty scheme** for pending authorisations\n- **Common digital platform** for certificate of origin\n- **Recognised exporter status** (Star Export Houses)\n- **E-commerce exports** promotion\n- **SCOMET policy** streamlined\n- **Dispute resolution:** one-time settlement\n# Export Promotion Schemes\n- **EPCG:** import of capital goods at zero or low duty with export obligation\n- **Advance Authorisation:** duty-free import of inputs for exports\n- **Duty Drawback**\n- **SEZ (2005), EOU, EPZ**\n- **Market Access Initiative,** TIES\n- **Interest equalisation**\n# Import Policy\n- **Most items freely importable;** few restricted, prohibited (negative list)\n- **Import licensing** for specific goods\n- **Tariff and non-tariff measures,** anti-dumping duty\n# Services\n- **IT and ITeS:** SEIS earlier, STPI, services from India\n# Evaluation\n- **Exports rose** (merchandise and services exceeded about 750 billion dollars in 2022-23)\n- **Persistent trade deficit** (oil, gold, electronics imports)\n- **Concentration** in few products and markets\n- **Non-tariff barriers abroad**\n- **WTO compliance issues** (export subsidies)\n# Suggestions\n- Quality, logistics, scale and technology, FTAs\n> Conclusion: FTP supports exports and ease of trade, but competitiveness is the key to sustained success.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit V - External Sector", "q": "Capital account convertibility: arguments for and against.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Capital account convertibility (CAC):** freedom to convert local currency into foreign currency for **capital transactions** (investment, loans)\n- **Current account convertibility:** for trade and services (India since 1994, Article VIII IMF)\n- **India's position:** **partial** CAC\n# Tarapore Committee (1997)\n- **Chairman:** S. S. Tarapore (RBI)\n- **Three-phase roadmap** (1997-2000)\n- **Pre-conditions:**\n- **Fiscal deficit** to GDP at 3.5 percent\n- **Inflation** at 3-5 percent\n- **CRR** at 3 percent\n- **Gross NPAs** at 5 percent\n- **Monitoring exchange rate band** and adequate reserves\n# Second Tarapore Committee (2006)\n- Five-year phased roadmap, liberalise limits on **ECBs, outward investment, portfolio flows**\n# Arguments in Favour\n- **Free flow of capital** and lower cost of capital\n- **More investment** and growth\n- **Diversification** of portfolio and risk\n- **Integration** with world markets\n- **Discipline** on government policies\n- **Efficient allocation** of resources\n- **Attracts NRI funds**\n# Arguments Against\n- **Volatility and sudden outflow (hot money):** East Asian crisis 1997\n- **Exchange rate instability**\n- **Loss of monetary policy independence** (Mundell trilemma)\n- **Weak financial system** of developing countries\n- **Capital flight** and black money\n- **Asset bubbles,** inflation\n- **Risk for small economies**\n# India's Experience\n- **India escaped** the 1997 Asian crisis (partly due to CAC controls)\n- **FEMA 1999** replaced FERA\n- **FDI** liberalised, **FPI** with limits\n- **ECBs** under ceilings, **LRS** (Liberalised Remittance Scheme) for individuals\n# Present Status\n- Capital account is **partially convertible,** with controls on short-term flows\n- Gradual approach continues\n# Suggestions\n- Strengthen banks, reduce fiscal deficit, build reserves\n- Move step by step, keep safeguards\n> Conclusion: CAC brings benefits only if macro economy and financial institutions are strong enough.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "What is food security? Outline India food security policy.", "y": "2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Food security (FAO):** all people at all times have **physical, social and economic access** to sufficient, safe and nutritious food\n- **Four dimensions:** availability, access, utilisation, stability\n- **Importance:** poverty, health, social stability\n# Components\n- **Availability:** production, stocks, imports\n- **Access:** income, prices, PDS\n- **Absorption:** health, sanitation, nutrition\n- **Stability:** no seasonal or climatic shocks\n# Policy Instruments in India\n- **Production policy:** green revolution, irrigation, MSP\n- **Buffer stock:** held by **FCI** (1965), storage by CWC\n- **Procurement** at MSP\n- **Public Distribution System (PDS)** through fair price shops\n- **Targeted PDS (1997)**\n- **Antyodaya Anna Yojana (2000)**\n- **Mid-day meal** (now **PM POSHAN**), **ICDS** (Anganwadi)\n- **Employment programmes:** MGNREGA\n# National Food Security Act 2013\n- **Legal right** to food\n- **Coverage:** up to **75 percent rural and 50 percent urban population**\n- **Priority households:** **5 kg per person per month** at Rs 3 (rice), Rs 2 (wheat), Re 1 (coarse grains)\n- **Antyodaya households:** **35 kg per household per month**\n- **Maternity benefit:** Rs 6,000 for pregnant women\n- **Nutrition** for children aged 6 months to 14 years\n- **Eldest woman** as head of household for ration card\n- **Grievance redressal** mechanism, state food commissions\n# PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana\n- **Free grain** (5 kg per person) to NFSA beneficiaries, extended for five years from 2024\n# Problems\n- **Leakage and diversion** in PDS\n- **Focus on rice and wheat,** poor nutrition (protein, micronutrients)\n- **Storage losses,** poor godowns\n- **Regional imbalance** in procurement\n- **Exclusion and inclusion errors**\n- **Climate risk,** price rise\n- Hunger and malnutrition remain (Global Hunger Index rank)\n# Reforms\n- **Digitised ration cards,** Aadhaar linking\n- **One Nation One Ration Card**\n- **DBT** and food coupons\n- **Fortified rice**\n- **Diversify** to pulses and millets\n- **Modern storage,** end-to-end computerisation\n> Conclusion: Food security needs production, storage, distribution and nutrition to work together.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit II - Classical and Socialist", "q": "Describe in brief the economic ideas of Karl Marx.", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Karl Marx (1818-83):** German philosopher, economist, revolutionary\n- **Collaborator:** Friedrich Engels\n- **Works:** Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts (1844), Communist Manifesto (1848), Critique of Political Economy (1859), **Das Kapital Vol. 1 (1867)**, Vols 2 and 3 (published by Engels, 1885, 1894)\n- **Influences:** Hegel (dialectics), Feuerbach (materialism), classical economists, French socialists\n# Dialectical and Historical Materialism\n- **Dialectics:** thesis, antithesis, synthesis (change through contradiction)\n- **Materialism:** matter and economic conditions come first\n- **Base and superstructure:** mode of production decides law, politics, religion\n- **Stages of history:** primitive communism, slavery, feudalism, capitalism, socialism, communism\n- **Class struggle:** history is a history of class struggle\n# Labour Theory of Value\n- **Value** is determined by **socially necessary labour time**\n- **Use value** and **exchange value**\n- **Commodity fetishism:** social relations appear as relations between things\n# Surplus Value\n- **Labour power** is a commodity, bought at subsistence wage\n- **Surplus value:** value produced by labour beyond wages, taken by capitalist\n- **Capital:** constant (C, machines) and variable (V, labour)\n- **Rate of surplus value (exploitation) = S/V**\n- **Organic composition of capital = C/V**\n- **Absolute surplus value:** longer working day\n- **Relative surplus value:** higher productivity\n# Capital Accumulation and Capitalist Process\n- **M-C-M':** money to commodity to more money\n- **Accumulation** by reinvestment of surplus\n- **Concentration and centralisation** of capital (big fish eat small)\n- **Industrial reserve army:** unemployed workers keep wages low\n- **Increasing misery** of the proletariat\n# Falling Rate of Profit\n- **Rate of profit = S/(C+V)**\n- **C/V rises** with mechanisation, so profit rate falls\n- **Counter tendencies:** exploitation, cheaper capital goods\n# Crisis Theory\n- **Over-production and under-consumption**\n- **Disproportionality** between sectors\n- **Periodic crises** worsen over time\n# Reproduction Schemes (Vol. 2)\n- **Department I** (capital goods) and **Department II** (consumer goods)\n- **Simple and expanded reproduction**\n# Other Ideas\n- **Alienation of labour** (from product, process, nature, others)\n- **Theory of rent and money**\n- **Transformation problem** (values to prices of production, Vol. 3)\n- **State** as instrument of ruling class\n- **Revolution** by the proletariat and **dictatorship of the proletariat**\n- **Withering away of the state** under communism, \"from each according to ability, to each according to need\"\n# Criticism\n- **Labour theory of value** is defective, ignores utility and capital\n- **Transformation problem** is unsolved\n- **Predictions failed:** wages rose, middle class grew, no collapse in advanced countries\n- **Neglects entrepreneur,** technology and risk\n- **Over-emphasis** on economic factors\n- **Communist experiments** failed (USSR collapse)\n# Contributions and Influence\n- **Critique of capitalism,** analysis of class and exploitation\n- **Basis of socialist and communist movements** (Lenin, Mao)\n- **Influence on economic history,** sociology, development theory\n- **Keynes, Schumpeter** acknowledged insights on cycles\n> Conclusion: Marx's predictions failed in part, but his analysis of capital, class and crisis remains influential.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit I - Ancient and Indian", "q": "Describe the economic ideas of Kautilya.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Kautilya (Chanakya, Vishnugupta):** minister of Chandragupta Maurya, 4th century BC\n- **Work:** **Arthashastra,** 15 books (adhikaranas), 150 chapters, 180 topics\n- **Artha:** wealth, livelihood and **earth** inhabited by people\n- **Arthashastra:** science of acquiring and protecting the earth\n- **Four vidyas:** Anvikshiki (philosophy), Trayi (Vedas), **Varta (economics)**, Dandaniti (politics)\n- **Varta:** agriculture, cattle rearing, trade\n# Role of the State in Economy\n- **State actively controls** economic life\n- **King's duty:** welfare of subjects (\"in happiness of the subjects lies the king's happiness\")\n- **State owns** mines, forests, large industries\n- **Wealth is the root** of dharma and kama\n- **Superintendents (Adhyakshas)** for each department: agriculture (Sitadhyaksha), trade (Panyadhyaksha), mining (Akaradhyaksha), treasury, weights and measures, forests, ports, textile, shipping\n# Agriculture\n- **Crown lands (Sita)** and private lands\n- **Irrigation:** state-run, water tax\n- **Settlement of new villages,** exemption of tax for new settlers\n- **Protection** of farmers, famine relief\n- **Cattle and forest** management\n# Trade, Industry and Prices\n- **Internal trade:** markets regulated, **weights and measures** standardised\n- **Fair prices,** profit limits (5 percent for local goods, 10 percent for foreign goods)\n- **Punishment for adulteration,** hoarding\n- **Guilds (Shrenis)** recognised\n- **Foreign trade:** protection of merchants, trade routes, duties on imports and exports\n- **State-owned industries:** mines, salt, weaving, armaments\n- **Wages:** fixed by state, fair wages, payment on time\n- **Labour:** protection of women and slaves, minimum wage principle\n# Public Finance\n- **Kosha (treasury)** is the most important limb; army depends on treasury\n- **Land revenue:** one-sixth share (bhaga) of produce\n- **Other taxes:** customs, sales tax, tolls, licence fees, fines\n- **Principle of taxation:** king should collect like a bee collects honey, without hurting the flower; **tax according to ability**\n- **Emergency taxes** in war or crisis\n- **Expenditure:** army, officials, public works, welfare\n- **Budget:** accounts and audit by officials\n# Administration and Law\n- **Saptanga:** swami, amatya, janapada, durga, kosha, danda, mitra\n- **Espionage system** to prevent corruption\n- **Corruption control:** 40 ways of embezzlement listed\n- **Mandala theory:** foreign policy among neighbours\n- **Shadgunya:** six measures (sandhi, vigraha, asana, yana, samshraya, dvaidhibhava)\n# Welfare State Ideas\n- **Famine relief,** storage of grain\n- **Care for orphans,** aged, widows, disabled\n- **Social security,** protection of consumers\n# Evaluation\n- **Merits:** comprehensive, realistic, early mixed economy ideas, public finance, welfare\n- **Demerits:** state control is heavy, harsh punishments, slavery accepted, caste elements\n> Conclusion: Kautilya's Arthashastra is the earliest systematic treatise on economics and governance, and many ideas are still relevant.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Explain the economic ideas of M. K. Gandhi.", "y": "2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **M. K. Gandhi (1869-1948):** Father of the Nation, moral and spiritual leader\n- **Works:** Hind Swaraj (1909), Constructive Programme, Young India, Harijan, Navajivan\n- **Influences:** Ruskin (Unto This Last), Tolstoy, Thoreau, Gita, Jainism\n- **Approach:** ethical economics, \"Economics that hurts the moral well-being is immoral\"\n# Basic Principles\n- **Truth and non-violence (ahimsa)** as the base\n- **Man comes before money** (human-centred)\n- **Simple living and high thinking**\n- **Aparigraha (non-possession)** and limitation of wants\n- **\"Earth provides enough for everyone's need, not for everyone's greed\"**\n- **Dignity of labour**\n# Swadeshi\n- **Use local goods,** serve the nearest neighbours\n- **Boycott of foreign cloth,** Khadi as a symbol\n- **Self-reliance** of village and nation\n- **Charkha** as source of employment and self-respect\n# Decentralisation and Village Economy\n- **Village republics (Gram Swaraj):** self-sufficient, with panchayats\n- **Cottage and village industries:** All India Village Industries Association (1934)\n- **Production by masses,** not mass production\n- **Rural employment,** remove poverty and migration\n- **Small units** instead of big concentration\n# Machinery\n- **Not against machines,** but against the craze for machinery\n- **Machines should help,** not displace labour\n- **Opposition to heavy industry** in a labour-surplus country\n# Trusteeship\n- **Rich are trustees** of wealth for the society\n- **Wealth belongs to society,** not to individual\n- **Non-violent way** of reducing inequality\n- **Voluntary renunciation** and fair wages, not confiscation\n- **Reply to Marxian class struggle**\n# Bread Labour\n- **Everyone should do manual work** for livelihood\n- **Removes parasitism** and class divisions\n# Sarvodaya\n- **Welfare of all** (not the greatest good of the greatest number)\n- **Basis:** Ruskin's Unto This Last\n# Education and Social Ideas\n- **Nai Talim (1937):** education through craft\n- **Prohibition** and removal of untouchability\n- **Women's role** in economic life\n- **Public finance:** low taxes, simple state, cheap administration, salt tax opposed\n# Agriculture\n- **Cooperative farming,** land to the tiller\n- **Bhoodan** and Gramdan (by Vinoba, Gandhian)\n- **Cow protection,** organic manure\n# Gandhian Plan (1944)\n- **Shriman Narayan's plan:** stress on agriculture and village industries\n# Criticism\n- **Impractical** in modern industrial economy\n- **Underestimates technology** and economies of scale\n- **Trusteeship** depends on voluntary goodwill, no legal force\n- **Cottage industry** cannot meet mass demand and growth needs\n- **Rejection of consumption** conflicts with growth\n- **Neglect of urban and industrial** development\n# Relevance Today\n- **Sustainable development,** climate and ecology concerns\n- **Decentralised planning:** Panchayati Raj (73rd Amendment)\n- **Rural employment:** MGNREGA, khadi and village industries\n- **Self-reliance:** Atmanirbhar Bharat\n- **Inequality** and ethical business\n> Conclusion: Gandhian economics is idealistic but gives a moral and sustainable alternative to unlimited consumption.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "What is Neo-classical economics? Explain its contributions.", "y": "2017, 2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Neo-classical economics:** school of economics from about 1870s to 1930s, after the **marginal revolution**\n- **Main contributors:** **Jevons (England), Menger (Austria), Walras (Switzerland)** and later **Marshall, Clark, Wicksteed, Edgeworth, Pigou, Wicksell, Fisher, Pareto**\n- **Also known as:** marginalist school\n- **Replaced** classical cost-of-production and labour theory of value with utility and margin\n# Basic Features\n- **Marginal analysis:** decisions are taken at the margin\n- **Subjective value:** value depends on utility and scarcity\n- **Rational economic man:** maximises utility or profit\n- **Methodology:** abstract, deductive, mathematical (graphs, calculus)\n- **Equilibrium analysis:** markets tend toward balance of demand and supply\n- **Micro-economic focus:** individual units, prices and allocation\n- **Ceteris paribus** assumption\n- **Perfect competition** as basic model\n- **Laissez-faire:** self-regulating market\n# Major Contributions\n- **Marginal utility theory:** diminishing marginal utility, equi-marginal principle (Jevons, Menger, Walras)\n- **Theory of demand and supply:** price as an outcome of both blades of the scissors (Marshall)\n- **Consumer's surplus** (Marshall)\n- **Elasticity of demand** (Marshall)\n- **Time element:** market period, short and long run\n- **General equilibrium** (Walras, Pareto)\n- **Marginal productivity theory** of distribution (J. B. Clark)\n- **Theory of rent and quasi-rent**\n- **Theory of interest:** Austrian capital theory (Bohm-Bawerk), Fisher's time preference, Wicksell\n- **Quantity theory of money:** Fisher's equation of exchange, Cambridge cash balance\n- **Welfare economics:** Pareto optimality, Pigou's economics of welfare\n- **Theory of the firm:** costs, representative firm, economies of scale\n- **Indifference curve approach** (Edgeworth, Pareto, later Hicks)\n# Austrian School\n- **Menger:** subjective value, opportunity cost (Wieser)\n- **Bohm-Bawerk:** capital and interest, time preference\n# Policy View\n- **Free markets,** minimum state, Say's law\n- **Full employment** as natural result\n- **Sound money,** gold standard\n# Criticism\n- **Unrealistic assumptions:** perfect competition, rationality, perfect knowledge\n- **Ignores unemployment** and business cycles (Keynes)\n- **Static analysis,** ignores dynamics and institutions (Veblen, historical school)\n- **Neglects income distribution**\n- **Mathematics** hides reality\n- **Ignores social and moral** factors\n- **Failure** in the Great Depression\n# Importance\n- **Foundation of micro economics** and modern price theory\n- **Basis for welfare and public finance**\n- **Tools used by Keynesian, monetarist** and modern schools\n> Conclusion: Neo-classical economics built the analytic tools of modern economics, though its limits were exposed by the Great Depression.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "Discuss the contribution of Marshall to economic thought.", "y": "2017, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Alfred Marshall (1842-1924):** Professor at Cambridge, founder of the **Cambridge School**\n- **Works:** **Principles of Economics (1890)**, Economics of Industry (1879, with Mary Paley), Industry and Trade (1919), Money, Credit and Commerce (1923)\n- **Definition:** economics is a study of mankind in the ordinary business of life\n- **Position:** synthesis of classical (cost) and marginal (utility) ideas\n# Methodology\n- **Economics as science and art**\n- **Partial equilibrium analysis:** one market with others constant (ceteris paribus)\n- **Use of mathematics** only in footnotes\n- **Realistic and historical** approach\n- **Economic chivalry** and ethical concern\n# Demand and Utility\n- **Marginal utility** and **diminishing marginal utility**\n- **Equi-marginal principle**\n- **Marginal utility of money** assumed constant\n- **Law of demand** and demand curve\n- **Elasticity of demand** (price, measure)\n- **Consumer's surplus:** difference between willingness to pay and price paid\n# Supply and Cost\n- **Cost of production** and **real cost** (efforts and sacrifices)\n- **Increasing, constant, diminishing returns**\n- **Internal and external economies**\n- **Representative firm**\n- **Law of increasing returns in industry**\n# Price Determination\n- **Demand and supply** as two blades of scissors\n- **Marshallian cross**\n- **Time element:** market period (supply fixed), short run (variable factors), long run (all variable), secular period\n- **Normal price:** long-run equilibrium price\n- **Stability of equilibrium:** Marshallian and Walrasian stability\n# Distribution\n- **Marginal productivity** theory for factors\n- **Wages:** marginal productivity, efficiency wages\n- **Rent:** Ricardian differential rent applied to all factors\n- **Quasi-rent:** short-run surplus on fixed factors\n- **Interest:** reward for waiting (abstinence)\n- **Profit:** reward of entrepreneur, normal profit as supply price of organisation\n# Money\n- **Cambridge cash balance equation:** M = kPY\n- **Quantity theory** with purchasing power\n- **Gold standard,** symmetallism proposal\n# Welfare Ideas\n- **Economic welfare** measured by money\n- **Consumer's surplus** and tax on diminishing returns industries, subsidy to increasing returns industries\n- **Concern for poverty,** social reform\n# Criticism\n- **Partial equilibrium** is unrealistic\n- **Ignores unemployment** and trade cycles (Keynes)\n- **Static,** ignores dynamics\n- **Representative firm** is vague\n- **Constant marginal utility of money** is unreal\n- **Cardinal utility** criticised (Hicks and Allen)\n- **Ignores imperfect competition** (Sraffa, Robinson, Chamberlin)\n# Importance\n- **Principles** dominated economics for about 50 years\n- **Created Cambridge tradition** (Pigou, Keynes, Robertson)\n- **Founder of modern microeconomics** and teaching of economics\n> Conclusion: Marshall synthesised classical and marginal ideas into a durable system that shaped modern economics.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit IV - Modern", "q": "How is Keynesian theory of employment different from the classical theory?", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Classical economists:** Adam Smith, Ricardo, Mill, J. B. Say, Marshall, Pigou\n- **J. M. Keynes:** General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936)\n- **Background:** Great Depression of 1930s, long unemployment, failure of classical remedies\n# Classical Theory of Employment\n- **Say's law:** supply creates its own demand\n- **Full employment** is normal, only temporary or voluntary unemployment\n- **Flexible wages and prices** restore full employment\n- **Saving equals investment** through interest rate\n- **Money is neutral:** only price level changes (quantity theory)\n- **Laissez-faire:** government should not interfere\n- **Balanced budget,** sound finance\n- **Micro approach:** analysis of individual markets\n# Keynesian Theory of Employment\n- **Effective demand** determines output and employment (aggregate demand = aggregate supply)\n- **Under-employment equilibrium** is possible\n- **Involuntary unemployment** exists\n- **Wages and prices are rigid** downward\n- **Consumption function:** MPC less than 1, income determines consumption\n- **Saving and investment** equalised by changes in income (not interest)\n- **Marginal efficiency of capital (MEC)** and interest rate determine investment\n- **Liquidity preference theory:** interest is the price for parting with liquidity\n- **Multiplier:** changes in investment cause larger changes in income\n- **Money matters:** affects output through interest rate\n- **Role of the state:** active fiscal policy, deficit financing, public works\n- **Paradox of thrift:** more saving can reduce income\n- **Short run view:** \"in the long run we are all dead\"\n- **Macro approach:** study of aggregates\n# Main Differences\n- **Say's law:** accepted vs rejected\n- **Employment:** full employment vs under-employment equilibrium\n- **Unemployment:** voluntary vs involuntary\n- **Wage cut:** helps recovery vs reduces demand\n- **Interest rate:** price of saving vs price of liquidity\n- **Saving and investment:** equated by interest vs equated by income\n- **Money:** neutral vs non-neutral\n- **Government role:** none vs active\n- **Analysis:** micro, long run vs macro, short run\n- **Budget:** balanced vs deficit financing in depression\n# Criticism of Keynes\n- **Neglects supply side** and long run\n- **Pigou effect:** real balance effect restores full employment in theory\n- **Rigid wage assumption** is questioned\n- **Ignores inflation** and expectations (monetarists, Friedman)\n- **Deficit financing** causes inflation, crowding out\n- **Stagflation** of the 1970s\n- **Applies mainly to developed economies**\n# Importance\n- **Birth of macro economics**\n- **Basis for government stabilisation policy,** welfare state\n- **Led to national income accounting**\n> Conclusion: Keynes replaced the classical view of automatic full employment with the idea of demand management by the state.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit IV - Modern", "q": "Evaluate contributions of Wicksell and Pigou.", "y": "2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Knut Wicksell (1851-1926):** Swedish economist, founder of the **Stockholm School**\n- **A. C. Pigou (1877-1959):** Cambridge economist, successor of Marshall\n- **Both:** neo-classical economists who extended Marshall and Walras\n# Contributions of Wicksell: Monetary Theory\n- **Interest and Prices (1898)**\n- **Natural rate of interest:** rate at which saving equals investment, equal to marginal productivity of capital\n- **Market (money) rate:** rate set by banks\n- **Cumulative process:** if market rate is below natural rate, investment exceeds saving, prices rise cumulatively\n- **Price stability** needs market rate equal to natural rate\n- **Criticism of quantity theory:** credit and interest matter\n- **Influence on Keynes** (Treatise on Money), Myrdal, Ohlin, Lindahl\n# Contributions of Wicksell: Other Areas\n- **Value, Capital and Rent (1893):** marginal productivity theory\n- **Product exhaustion:** adding-up of factor shares (Euler's theorem)\n- **Wicksell effect:** problem of measuring capital\n- **Wicksell's Lectures on Political Economy (1901-06)**\n- **Public finance (1896):** benefit principle, **unanimity rule**, just taxation\n- **Population theory:** neo-Malthusian, supported birth control\n# Contributions of Pigou: Welfare Economics\n- **Wealth and Welfare (1912), Economics of Welfare (1920)**\n- **Welfare:** national dividend (real income) as measure\n- **Welfare increases** if national dividend rises, distribution becomes more equal, fluctuations reduce\n- **Marginal social and private net product:** divergence due to externalities\n- **Pigouvian tax and subsidy:** to correct externalities\n- **Transfers from rich to poor** raise welfare (diminishing marginal utility of income)\n- **Public finance:** principle of least sacrifice\n# Contributions of Pigou: Other Areas\n- **Theory of Unemployment (1933):** wages and unemployment (classical, wage elasticity)\n- **Industrial Fluctuations (1927):** psychological theory of cycles\n- **Pigou effect (1943):** real balance effect\n- **Cambridge cash balance** equation\n- **Industrial relations,** wage determination\n# Criticism\n- **Wicksell:** natural rate is difficult to measure, no clear role of expectations\n- **Pigou:** assumes cardinal and comparable utility (Robbins)\n- **Pigou:** Coase argued that bargaining can solve externalities\n- **Pigou:** classical view of unemployment criticised by Keynes\n- **Wicksell:** monetary theory incomplete\n# Importance\n- **Wicksell:** links classical and Keynesian monetary theory, started Swedish school\n- **Pigou:** founder of welfare economics and externality theory\n> Conclusion: Both extended neo-classical theory and prepared the ground for modern macro and welfare economics.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Critically estimate J. K. Mehta theory of wantlessness.", "y": "2018, 2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **J. K. Mehta (1900-80):** Indian economist, professor at Allahabad University\n- **Works:** Advanced Economic Theory, Economics of Growth, Studies in Economics, Elementary Economics\n- **Approach:** Eastern (Indian) philosophy, influenced by Gandhi and Buddhist ideas\n- **Contribution:** new definition of economics as science of **wantlessness**\n# Concept of Wantlessness\n- **Economics:** science of **wantlessness,** not of want satisfaction\n- **Wants and pain:** unsatisfied wants cause pain\n- **Wants are unlimited,** satisfaction is never complete\n- **Happiness** comes from reducing wants, not satisfying them\n- **Aim of economic activity:** minimise wants and restore peace\n- **Optimum:** state of wantlessness\n# Western vs Mehta's View\n- **Western (Marshall, Robbins):** maximise satisfaction, increase wants and efforts\n- **Mehta:** minimise wants, reduce efforts\n- **Western:** equi-marginal principle, more is better\n- **Mehta:** self-control, satisfaction by wantlessness\n- **Welfare:** from inner peace and moral growth, not from consumption\n# Main Ideas\n- **Wants and means:** a gap between wants and means causes pain\n- **Priority of moral values**\n- **Consumption:** moderate, ethical\n- **Reduction of wants** is the cheapest way to raise happiness\n- **Equilibrium of a consumer:** where pain from want is minimum\n- **Economic analysis** aimed at a happy and peaceful society\n- **Application to planning:** emphasis on saving and austerity in poor country\n# Merits\n- **Original Indian contribution,** attempt to integrate philosophy and economics\n- **Highlights limits** of consumerism\n- **Relevant for sustainability** and environment\n- **Moral and welfare dimension** of economics\n- **Consistent with Gandhian** thought\n# Criticism\n- **Unrealistic** in a market economy based on rising wants\n- **Reduction of wants** may reduce demand, growth and employment\n- **Hurts incentive** to work, innovate and save\n- **Poor people need more goods,** not fewer wants\n- **Subjective and philosophical,** hard to measure\n- **Confuses positive and normative** economics (Robbins)\n- **Ignores role of wants** as motive of progress\n- **Not applicable** as a basis for government policy\n# Relevance\n- **Sustainable consumption,** minimalism and environmental concerns\n- **Happiness economics** (Bhutan GNH)\n- **Debate on consumerism** and wellbeing\n> Conclusion: Mehta's wantlessness is a thoughtful ethical idea, but it is difficult to use as a guide for development policy.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit II - Classical and Socialist", "q": "Critically examine economic ideas of Saint-Simon. Assess the contribution of Proudhon.", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Utopian socialism:** early socialist thought (Saint-Simon, Fourier, Owen, Proudhon) based on persuasion, not revolution\n- **Name \"utopian\"** given by Marx and Engels\n- **Saint-Simon (1760-1825):** French count and thinker\n- **Proudhon (1809-65):** French printer, journalist, anarchist thinker\n# Saint-Simon: Works\n- **L'Industrie (1816-18),** The Organiser (1819-20)\n- **Systeme Industriel (1821),** Catechisme des Industriels (1823-24)\n- **Nouveau Christianisme (1825)**\n# Saint-Simon: Main Ideas\n- **Industrial society:** rule of producers (workers, farmers, merchants, scientists) over idle classes\n- **Two classes:** industriels (useful) and oisifs (idle: nobles, rentiers, priests)\n- **Role of experts:** technocrats, bankers and scientists should plan the economy\n- **Planned economy** and public works (canals, railways)\n- **Abolition of inheritance:** property to go back to the state\n- **Credit and banks** to guide investment\n- **Slogan:** \"to each according to his capacity, to each capacity according to its works\"\n- **Aim:** improving the condition of the poorest class\n- **New Christianity:** moral base of reform, brotherhood\n- **Stages of history:** progress through science and industry\n- **Saint-Simonians** (Enfantin, Bazard) extended the ideas\n# Saint-Simon: Criticism\n- **Elitist:** rule by experts, no worker participation\n- **Unclear plan** and religious mysticism\n- **Ignores class conflict** between capital and labour\n- **Not practical**\n# Proudhon: Works\n- **What is Property? (1840)**\n- **System of Economic Contradictions (Philosophy of Poverty, 1846)**\n- **General Idea of the Revolution (1851)**\n# Proudhon: Main Ideas\n- **\"Property is theft\"** (property in the sense of unearned income, rent and interest)\n- **\"Property is liberty\"** (small possession by work) in a later work\n- **Mutualism:** exchange based on equal labour value\n- **People's Bank (1849):** free credit, labour notes\n- **Abolition of interest and rent**\n- **Anarchism:** society without state, federation of free communes\n- **Federalism** and decentralised units\n- **Opposed communism** and strong state\n# Proudhon: Criticism\n- **Marx's reply:** Poverty of Philosophy (1847)\n- **Mutualism** is a petty bourgeois view\n- **People's Bank failed**\n- **Contradictory views** on property\n# Contributions\n- **Early critique of capitalism,** exploitation by capital\n- **Ideas of planning,** cooperation, credit\n- **Influence on socialism,** syndicalism, anarchism, cooperative movement\n> Conclusion: Their ideas were visionary but lacked a practical path to change, which Marx called utopian.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Discuss main economic ideas of Nehru and compare Nehruism with Gandhism.", "y": "2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Jawaharlal Nehru (1889-1964):** first Prime Minister of India (1947-64), architect of planning\n- **Works:** Discovery of India, Glimpses of World History, Autobiography\n- **Influences:** Fabian socialism, Marxism, Gandhi\n- **Chairman:** National Planning Committee (1938)\n# Main Economic Ideas\n- **1. Socialistic pattern of society:** democratic socialism (Avadi resolution 1955)\n- **2. Mixed economy:** public and private sectors together\n- **3. Economic planning:** Planning Commission (1950), Five Year Plans\n- **4. Heavy industry:** Second Plan (Mahalanobis model); steel plants (Bhilai, Rourkela, Durgapur)\n- **5. Public sector** on the \"commanding heights\" of the economy\n- **6. Industrial Policy Resolution 1956:** Schedules A, B, C\n- **7. Self-reliance:** import substitution, protection of domestic industry\n- **8. Science and technology:** \"scientific temper,\" IITs, CSIR, atomic energy\n- **9. Dams and irrigation:** \"temples of modern India\" (Bhakra-Nangal)\n- **10. Land reforms:** abolition of zamindari\n- **11. Community development (1952)** and Panchayati Raj (1959)\n- **12. Cooperatives** and village industries (supporting)\n- **13. Social justice:** removal of poverty and inequality\n- **14. Non-alignment:** aid from both blocs\n- **15. Foreign capital:** accepted with regulation\n- **16. Education and health** as national investments\n# Nehru and Gandhi: Differences (Nehruism vs Gandhism)\n- **Industry:** Nehru - large and heavy; Gandhi - cottage and village\n- **Machine:** Nehru welcomed modern technology; Gandhi was cautious\n- **State:** Nehru - strong planning state; Gandhi - decentralised, minimal state\n- **Wants:** Nehru - raise standard of living; Gandhi - limit wants\n- **Socialism:** Nehru - state socialism; Gandhi - trusteeship\n- **Approach:** Nehru - scientific and modern; Gandhi - moral and spiritual\n- **Urbanisation:** Nehru accepted; Gandhi - village centred\n# Similarities\n- **Both wanted** removal of poverty and inequality\n- **Both emphasised** self-reliance\n- **Both cared** for rural welfare, democracy and peace\n# Achievements of Nehru's Model\n- **Industrial base** in steel, power, machine tools\n- **Scientific and technical institutions**\n- **Self-reliance** in many sectors\n- **Stable growth** for planned economy (about 3.5-4 percent)\n# Criticism\n- **Slow growth** (Hindu rate of growth by Raj Krishna)\n- **Licence-permit raj,** inefficiency of public sector\n- **Neglect of agriculture** and primary education\n- **Import substitution** led to high-cost industries\n- **Balance of payments crisis** by 1991\n# Relevance\n- **Reforms of 1991** corrected excesses, but public institutions, science and planning legacy continue\n> Conclusion: Nehru's model built the industrial and scientific base of India, though its controls later needed reform.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "Explain economic ideas of Walras (general equilibrium).", "y": "2017", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Leon Walras (1834-1910):** French economist, professor at Lausanne (Lausanne School)\n- **Works:** **Elements of Pure Economics (1874-77),** Studies in Social Economics, Studies in Applied Economics\n- **One of the founders** of the marginal revolution (with Jevons and Menger)\n- **Followers:** Pareto, Cassel, Hicks, Arrow, Debreu\n# Marginal Utility Theory\n- **Rarete:** scarcity relative to utility (his term for marginal utility)\n- **Value** depends on scarcity and utility\n- **Equi-marginal principle:** ratio of marginal utility to price is equal for all goods\n- **Exchange equilibrium:** ratio of marginal utilities equals price ratio\n# General Equilibrium Model\n- **All markets** in the economy are interdependent\n- **Simultaneous equations** for demand, supply, price\n- **Four types of markets:** products, factor services, capital goods (savings), money\n- **Numeraire:** a good used as the unit of account\n- **Number of equations equals number of unknowns**\n- **Perfect competition,** perfect information, no transaction cost\n- **Equilibrium in exchange:** consumers maximise utility\n- **Equilibrium in production:** firms earn zero profit, price equals average cost\n- **Fixed technical coefficients** (production)\n# Walras' Law\n- **Sum of values of excess demands in all markets is zero**\n- **If n-1 markets are in equilibrium, the nth is also in equilibrium**\n# Tatonnement (Groping)\n- **Auctioneer** calls out prices\n- **Excess demand** raises price, excess supply lowers price\n- **No trade till equilibrium**\n- **Stability** of equilibrium by this process\n# Theory of Capital and Interest\n- **Capital goods** earn net income\n- **Saving and investment** equal in equilibrium\n- **Rate of interest** from the capital market\n# Theory of Money\n- **Demand for money** (cash balance) and quantity of money\n- **Money as a numeraire** and medium\n# Distribution\n- **Marginal productivity theory:** factor prices equal marginal productivity\n- **Wages, rent and interest** determined in factor markets\n# Social Economics\n- **Nationalisation of land:** rent to be taken by the state to finance expenditure (instead of taxes)\n- **Free competition** in other fields\n- **Cooperatives and just distribution**\n# Criticism\n- **Unrealistic assumptions:** perfect competition and information\n- **Static,** no time, uncertainty or expectations\n- **Existence and uniqueness** issues (solved later by Arrow-Debreu)\n- **Tatonnement is imaginary:** actual trades occur at false prices\n- **Ignores money,** role of banks\n- **Over-mathematical,** no practical use\n- **Fixed coefficients** are unrealistic\n# Importance\n- **Foundation of general equilibrium theory,** welfare economics, input-output analysis (Leontief), CGE models\n- **Mathematical economics** developed through him\n> Conclusion: Walras gave the mathematical framework for the study of interdependence in the whole economy.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit IV - Modern", "q": "What is the Cobweb Model? Explain its types.", "y": "2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Cobweb model:** a dynamic model of price and quantity in markets with a **production lag**\n- **Developed by:** Henry Schultz, Ricci, Tinbergen (1930); named by **Nicholas Kaldor (1934)**; Mordecai Ezekiel (1938)\n- **Applies to:** agricultural and livestock markets (wheat, potatoes, hogs), shipbuilding\n- **Idea:** supply responds to the last period's price\n# Assumptions\n- **Production lag:** one period between decision and output\n- **Supply** depends on previous period's price: S(t) = f(P(t-1))\n- **Demand** depends on current price: D(t) = f(P(t))\n- **Perfect competition,** price takers\n- **Naive (static) expectations:** producers expect today's price to continue\n- **Market clears** each period\n- **Perishable goods,** no storage\n[[Equilibrium each period: D(t) = S(t)]]\n# Working\n- **High price today** induces large planting for next period\n- **Next period supply is large,** price falls sharply\n- **Low price** leads to small planting, so later supply is small\n- **Price rises again,** cycle repeats\n- **Diagram** looks like a spider's web\n# Three Types\n- **Convergent (stable) cobweb:** supply curve is steeper (less elastic) than demand, price converges to equilibrium\n- **Divergent (explosive) cobweb:** supply is flatter (more elastic) than demand, fluctuations widen\n- **Continuous (perpetual) cobweb:** slopes of equal magnitude, fluctuations remain constant\n[[Stable if elasticity of supply < elasticity of demand (in absolute value)]]\n# Mathematical Form\n- **Difference equation** of first order\n- **Solution:** P(t) = equilibrium price + (P0 - equilibrium price) x (-b/d)^t\n- **Stability** depends on ratio of slopes of supply and demand\n# Economic Meaning\n- **Explains regular cycles** in agriculture (hog cycle)\n- **Shows importance of expectations**\n- **Highlights the role of time lags**\n# Policy Implications\n- **Price stabilisation:** buffer stock, minimum support price\n- **Forward and futures markets**\n- **Crop insurance,** information on acreage\n- **Government forecasts** of supply and prices\n# Criticism\n- **Naive expectations** unrealistic; farmers learn (adaptive expectations, **Muth's rational expectations 1961**)\n- **Ignores storage and inventory**\n- **Assumes competitive market** and fixed lag\n- **Only partial equilibrium**\n- **Policy of the government** and trade is ignored\n> Conclusion: The cobweb model explains why prices of agricultural goods fluctuate, and why expectations and time lags matter.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "Explain Cobb-Douglas production function.", "y": "2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Cobb-Douglas production function:** developed by **Charles W. Cobb (mathematician)** and **Paul H. Douglas (economist)** in **1928**\n- **Based on:** US manufacturing data, 1899-1922\n- **Earlier idea:** Wicksell and Knut\n- **Use:** shows relation between output and inputs (labour and capital)\n# Mathematical Form\n[[Q = A . L^a . K^b]]\n- **Q:** output, **L:** labour, **K:** capital\n- **A:** technology (efficiency) parameter\n- **a and b:** output elasticities of labour and capital\n- **Linear homogeneous form:** when a + b = 1, then Q = A L^a K^(1-a)\n# Properties\n- **Returns to scale:** a + b = 1 constant, a + b > 1 increasing, a + b < 1 decreasing\n- **Marginal products:** positive and diminishing for each factor\n- **Elasticity of substitution = 1**\n- **Marginal product of labour** MPL = a (Q/L), of capital MPK = b (Q/K)\n- **Isoquants** are convex to origin and never touch axes\n- **Constant factor shares:** share of labour = a, share of capital = b\n- **Euler's theorem:** total output is exhausted when factors are paid their marginal products (if a + b = 1)\n- **Log-linear form:** log Q = log A + a log L + b log K (easy to estimate by regression)\n# Findings of Cobb and Douglas\n- **Labour elasticity about 0.75,** capital about 0.25\n- **Constant returns to scale** for US manufacturing\n# Uses\n- **Estimating factor shares**\n- **Growth accounting:** Solow residual (technical progress)\n- **Returns to scale** measurement\n- **Production planning** and cost minimisation\n- **Macro growth models**\n- **Empirical studies** of industries and countries\n# Criticism\n- **Elasticity of substitution always one** (unrealistic)\n- **Constant factor shares** do not always hold\n- **Assumes perfect competition** and full use of factors\n- **Capital measurement** problem (Cambridge controversy)\n- **Ignores technical change** other than neutral shift in A\n- **Multicollinearity** and aggregation problems\n- **Not suited** for industries with fixed proportions\n# Extensions\n- **CES function (Arrow, Chenery, Minhas, Solow, 1961):** variable elasticity of substitution\n- **Translog function** (Christensen, Jorgenson, Lau)\n> Conclusion: The Cobb-Douglas function is simple and widely used, though its assumptions are restrictive.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Write the outline of economic ideas of R. C. Dutt.", "y": "2017", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Romesh Chunder Dutt (1848-1909):** Indian civil servant (ICS 1871), historian, nationalist economist\n- **President:** Indian National Congress, Lucknow session (1899)\n- **Works:** **Economic History of India (2 vols): Under Early British Rule (1901), In the Victorian Age (1903)**; Famines in India (1900); Open Letters to Lord Curzon (1900); India in the Victorian Age (1904)\n- **Approach:** nationalist, used official statistics\n# Main Economic Ideas\n- **1. Drain of wealth:** wealth of India flowed to Britain without return\n- **2. Home charges:** pensions, interest and salaries paid in England\n- **3. Decline of Indian industries:** handicrafts and cotton industry ruined by British policy\n- **4. De-industrialisation:** workers pushed to agriculture\n- **5. Heavy land revenue:** high and uncertain demand impoverished farmers\n- **6. Famines:** caused by heavy assessment, not only by drought\n- **7. Permanent Settlement:** supported extension of the permanent settlement to protect cultivators\n- **8. Limit on land revenue:** fixing land tax (one-third of produce in his demand, letters to Curzon)\n- **9. Tariff and trade policy:** unfair free trade, one-sided duties, no protection to Indian industries\n- **10. Poverty of India:** a result of foreign rule, over-taxation and wasteful expenditure\n- **11. Excessive military expenditure** and war costs charged to India\n- **12. Public works:** irrigation neglected compared with railways, expensive railway guarantees\n- **13. Salt tax** and burden on the poor\n- **14. Indian participation** in higher services and administration\n- **15. Revival of village industry** and Swadeshi encouraged\n# Economic History\n- **Revealed** the growth and fall of Indian crafts\n- **Gave data** on trade, revenue and expenditure\n- **Described** Mughal and company rule contrast\n# Comparison with Naoroji\n- **Naoroji:** drain theory with estimates\n- **Dutt:** historical proof, emphasis on land revenue, famines, and remedies\n# Contributions\n- **First economic history of British India**\n- **Factual base** to the nationalist critique\n- **Inspired the Swadeshi movement** and later economists\n- **Practical suggestions** for relief of farmers\n# Criticism\n- **Biased against British rule,** ignored benefits (railways, law, administration)\n- **Support for permanent settlement** is questionable\n- **Statistics** limited and sometimes disputed\n- **Cultural and political factors** neglected\n> Conclusion: R. C. Dutt gave a factual base to the economic critique of colonial rule and shaped Indian economic thought.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Pigou Effect.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Meaning:** fall in the price level raises the **real value of wealth**, so spending rises\n- **Given by:** A. C. Pigou (1943), as a reply to Keynes\n- **Also called:** real balance effect (Patinkin)\n# Working\n- In depression, prices and wages fall\n- Real value of **money balances and bonds** rises\n- People feel richer, so **consumption rises**\n- Rise in demand raises output and employment\n- Process continues till **full employment** is restored\n[[Fall in P -> rise in M/P -> rise in C -> rise in Y]]\n# Importance\n- Shows that **under-employment equilibrium is not permanent** if prices are flexible\n- Restores the classical result in theory\n- Supports the role of monetary wealth in consumption\n# Criticism\n- **Wages and prices are rigid** downward\n- **Expectation of further fall** delays spending\n- **Debtors are hurt,** as their real debt rises (Fisher's debt deflation)\n- **Real balance** part held by outside money is small\n- Very slow process, so government action is better\n> Conclusion: The Pigou effect is correct in theory, but weak in practice.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Demonstration Effect.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Meaning:** people **imitate the consumption pattern** of richer groups or countries\n- **Given by:** James Duesenberry (1949); the term was also used by Nurkse for international comparison\n# Features\n- Consumption depends on **neighbours' consumption**\n- Lower income groups copy higher groups\n- Developing countries copy rich countries\n- Spread by media, advertising, travel and trade\n# Effects\n- **Raises propensity to consume,** lowers saving\n- **Reduces capital formation** and investment\n- Raises import of luxury goods, pressure on **balance of payments**\n- **Explains why APC is constant** in the long run\n- Social envy and inequality\n# Policy\n- Tax on luxury goods, encouraging saving, local products\n# Criticism\n- Difficult to measure\n- Underestimates rational choice\n- Not all imitation is harmful (improves standards)\n> Conclusion: Demonstration effect shows that social influence shapes consumption, especially in developing countries.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Selling Costs.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Meaning:** expenses made to **create or raise demand** for a product\n- **Concept of:** E. H. Chamberlin, in monopolistic competition\n- **Examples:** advertising, salesmen, samples, display, discounts, free gifts\n# Production Cost vs Selling Cost\n- **Production cost:** meet an existing demand\n- **Selling cost:** change (shift) the demand\n- Production cost raises output, selling cost raises sales\n# Effects on Demand\n- **Shift demand curve to the right**\n- Make demand **less elastic** (brand loyalty)\n- Product differentiation is increased\n# Selling Cost Curve\n- **U-shaped average selling cost curve**\n- Initially cost per unit falls, then rises\n# Equilibrium\n- Firm spends on selling until **marginal selling cost = marginal revenue** from extra sales\n[[Total cost = Production cost + Selling cost]]\n- Optimum selling expenditure maximises profit\n# Criticism\n- Wasteful in many cases, raises price\n- Can create entry barriers\n- Informational value (advert tells about product)\n> Conclusion: Selling costs are special to imperfect competition, and they have both useful and wasteful sides.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Reaction Curve.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Meaning:** shows how a firm changes its **best output** as the rival's output changes\n- **Used in:** Cournot duopoly model, also Stackelberg model\n- **Concept:** interdependence of firms in oligopoly\n# Features\n- **Reaction curve of A (RA):** A's profit-maximising output for each output of B\n- **Reaction curve of B (RB)** is similarly drawn\n- It is **downward sloping** in the Cournot model (outputs are strategic substitutes)\n- Rise in B's output leads A to cut output\n# Derivation\n- Take iso-profit curves of the firm for each output of the rival\n- Join the **peak points** of the iso-profit curves\n- That gives the reaction curve\n# Equilibrium\n- **Intersection of RA and RB** gives **Cournot (Nash) equilibrium**\n[[RA = RB at equilibrium]]\n- Neither firm wants to change its output\n# Stability\n- Equilibrium is stable if RA is steeper than RB\n# Types\n- **Cournot:** downward sloping reaction curves (quantity competition)\n- **Bertrand:** upward sloping reaction curves (price competition)\n# Importance\n- Basic tool of oligopoly and game theory\n> Conclusion: Reaction curves help to understand equilibrium in mutual dependence.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Distinguish between internal and international trade. Explain the absolute advantage theory of trade.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Internal (domestic) trade:** trade between regions of the same country\n- **International trade:** trade between different countries\n- **Classical view (Ricardo, Mill):** a separate theory is needed for international trade\n- **Ohlin's view:** no fundamental difference, only a difference of degree\n# Differences\n- **Mobility of factors:** high inside the country, low between countries\n- **Currency:** one currency inside; different currencies between countries, so exchange rate problem\n- **Trade barriers:** tariffs, quotas, licences between countries\n- **Government policy:** different laws and taxes in each country\n- **Language, culture, customs:** different\n- **Markets:** homogeneous market inside, segmented markets outside\n- **Risk:** higher in foreign trade (political, exchange)\n- **Transport and documentation cost:** higher in foreign trade\n- **Law of cost:** comparative cost theory applies more in foreign trade because factors are immobile\n- **Balance of payments:** arises only in foreign trade\n- **Cost differences:** large between countries\n- **Economic and political independence** of countries\n# Similarities\n- Both based on division of labour\n- Both aim at gains from exchange\n- Same basic laws of demand and supply\n# Absolute Advantage Theory (Adam Smith, 1776)\n- **Idea:** country should produce what it can produce at **lower absolute cost** and import the rest\n- **Division of labour** limited by the extent of the market\n- **Assumptions:** two countries, two goods, labour the only factor, constant cost, free trade, no transport cost\n- **Example:** India makes cloth in 10 hours and wheat in 20; UK makes cloth in 20 and wheat in 10\n- India should specialise in cloth, UK in wheat\n- Both gain by trade\n- **World output rises**\n# Criticism of Absolute Advantage\n- Cannot explain trade where one country has absolute advantage in all goods (solved by Ricardo)\n- Labour theory of value is unrealistic\n- Ignores demand side and cost of transport\n- Two-country, two-good model\n# Merits\n- Basis of free trade and division of labour doctrine\n- Opposed mercantilist ideas\n> Conclusion: International trade differs from internal trade in degree due to barriers, and Smith's theory explains only a limited part of it.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Explain the Heckscher-Ohlin theory of international trade.", "y": "2018, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Eli Heckscher** (1919) and **Bertil Ohlin** (Interregional and International Trade, 1933)\n- **Also called:** factor proportions or factor endowment theory\n- **Improvement on Ricardo:** explains why comparative costs differ\n# Main Idea\n- **Trade arises due to differences in factor endowments** among countries\n- **Country exports** goods that use its **abundant factor** intensively\n- **Country imports** goods that use its **scarce factor** intensively\n# Assumptions\n- Two countries, two goods, two factors (labour and capital) (2x2x2)\n- Same technology everywhere\n- Perfect competition, full employment\n- Constant returns to scale\n- Goods differ in factor intensity (no intensity reversal)\n- Factors mobile inside, immobile between countries\n- Identical tastes\n- No transport cost and no tariffs\n# Explanation\n- **Capital-abundant country (USA):** capital is cheap, exports capital-intensive goods\n- **Labour-abundant country (India):** labour is cheap, exports labour-intensive goods\n[[Factor endowment -> Factor price -> Commodity price -> Trade]]\n- **Two ways to define abundance:** physical (K/L ratio) and price (wage-rent ratio)\n# Factor Price Equalisation Theorem\n- **Samuelson:** free trade tends to equalise **relative and absolute factor prices** among countries\n- Trade is a substitute for factor movement\n# Related Theorems\n- **Stolper-Samuelson:** rise in price of a good raises the return of the factor used intensively\n- **Rybczynski:** rise in a factor's supply raises output of the good using it intensively\n# Leontief Paradox (1953)\n- US exports were **less capital-intensive** than imports\n- Against the expectation\n- **Explanations:** US labour is more productive, demand differences, natural resources, factor intensity reversal, trade barriers\n# Merits\n- Explains the **cause** of comparative advantage\n- General equilibrium approach\n- Explains effect on factor prices and income distribution\n# Criticism\n- **Same technology** is unrealistic\n- **Demand differences** ignored\n- **Economies of scale** and imperfect competition ignored (Krugman)\n- **Intra-industry trade** not explained\n- Leontief paradox\n- Static, ignores dynamic factors (technological gap, product cycle)\n- **Assumes factor immobility**\n> Conclusion: H-O theory explains the basic pattern of trade, but needs support from newer trade theories.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "What are the causes of disequilibrium in Balance of Payments? Explain measures to correct it.", "y": "2016, 2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **BOP disequilibrium:** autonomous receipts and payments are not equal\n- **Deficit:** payments more than receipts. **Surplus:** receipts more than payments\n- **Concern:** mostly deficit, particularly for developing countries\n# Causes of BOP Disequilibrium\n- **Cyclical:** boom or recession in the country or in trading partners\n- **Structural:** change in demand or supply pattern, loss of export markets, supply bottlenecks\n- **Secular (long-term):** rising import needs in development, falling exports\n- **Inflation:** domestic prices rise faster than abroad, exports fall\n- **Development needs:** import of capital goods, technology, oil\n- **Population pressure:** food and consumption imports\n- **Demonstration effect:** import of luxury goods\n- **Low export competitiveness:** poor quality, high cost\n- **Capital flows:** capital flight, withdrawal of foreign funds\n- **Political instability,** war, sanctions\n- **Natural calamities,** crop failure\n- **Overvalued exchange rate**\n- **Foreign debt burden,** interest payments\n- **Global shocks:** oil prices, crisis\n# Measures to Correct Disequilibrium\n# Monetary measures\n- **Credit control:** raise bank rate, CRR, to reduce inflation and imports\n- **Tight monetary policy** reduces demand\n# Fiscal measures\n- **Reduce government expenditure,** raise taxes\n- **Export incentives,** tax rebates\n# Exchange rate measures\n- **Devaluation or depreciation** to raise exports\n- **Multiple exchange rates**\n# Trade measures\n- **Export promotion:** subsidies, EPZ, SEZ, duty drawback\n- **Import control:** tariffs, quotas, licences\n- **Import substitution**\n# Direct controls\n- **Exchange control** and rationing of foreign exchange\n# Others\n- **Foreign loans and aid,** IMF assistance\n- **Attract FDI and NRI deposits**\n- **Use of reserves**\n- **Increase productivity,** quality of exports\n# Evaluation of Measures\n- Deflation hurts growth and employment\n- Devaluation depends on elasticities\n- Import control invites retaliation\n- Exchange control creates black market\n- Loans increase debt\n# India's Experience\n- 1991 crisis: devaluation, loan from IMF, trade reforms, FDI liberalisation\n> Conclusion: A mix of expenditure-reducing and expenditure-switching policies, with structural reforms, gives lasting correction.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Discuss the merits and demerits of floating exchange rate system.", "y": "2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Floating exchange rate:** rate determined by **market demand and supply** of currencies\n- **Clean float:** no intervention by the central bank\n- **Dirty (managed) float:** central bank intervenes sometimes\n- **System after 1973,** end of Bretton Woods\n# Determination of the Rate\n[[Equilibrium: Demand for forex = Supply of forex]]\n- **Demand for forex:** imports, foreign investment abroad, remittances\n- **Supply of forex:** exports, foreign investment inflow, remittances\n- **Currency appreciates** when demand for it rises\n- **Currency depreciates** when its supply rises\n# Merits\n- **Automatic adjustment** of BOP deficits\n- **No need for large foreign exchange reserves**\n- **Independent monetary and fiscal policy**\n- **Shock absorber** against external events\n- **No black market** or exchange control\n- **Efficient allocation** of resources by market signals\n- **Free from political pressure** to devalue\n- **Reduces speculative attacks** on a fixed peg\n# Demerits\n- **Uncertainty** hurts trade and investment\n- **Speculation** and large volatility\n- **May cause inflation** because depreciation raises import prices\n- **Reduces foreign investment**\n- **Hot money** flows\n- **Hurts small countries** with thin forex markets\n- **Competitive depreciation** (currency wars)\n- **Cost of hedging** for businesses\n- **Overshooting** of exchange rates\n# Mundell's Trilemma\n- Floating rate allows an independent monetary policy plus free capital flow\n# India\n- **Managed floating system** since 1993 (market-determined with RBI intervention)\n- RBI buys or sells dollars to limit volatility\n- Large forex reserves to guard against shocks\n# Suitability\n- Suits large open economies with developed financial markets\n- Needs strong institutions\n> Conclusion: The floating system gives flexibility and policy freedom, but needs managed intervention to avoid excess volatility.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit V - External Sector", "q": "What were the main reforms of the New Economic Policy 1991? Evaluate them.", "y": "2016, 2017, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **New Economic Policy (NEP) 1991:** **LPG reforms** (Liberalisation, Privatisation, Globalisation)\n- **Government:** P. V. Narasimha Rao, **Finance Minister Dr Manmohan Singh**\n- **Type:** stabilisation and structural adjustment\n# Background (Causes)\n- **BOP crisis:** forex reserves fell to about 1 billion dollars (two weeks of imports)\n- **High inflation** (about 17 percent), **fiscal deficit** about 8 percent of GDP\n- **Gulf War** oil price shock, fall in remittances\n- **Poor performance of public sector**\n- **Gold pledged** to raise foreign exchange, IMF loan\n- **Inefficiency** of the licence-permit raj\n# Liberalisation\n- **Industrial licensing abolished** except for few industries\n- **MRTP limits removed**\n- **Public sector reservation cut**\n- **Trade liberalisation:** import licensing removed, **tariffs reduced**\n- **Rupee devalued** (July 1991 by about 18-19 percent), then **market-determined rate (1993)**\n- **Current account convertibility** (1994)\n# Privatisation\n- **Disinvestment** of PSUs\n- **Rangarajan Committee,** Disinvestment Commission (1996)\n- **Entry of private sector** in telecom, aviation, power, banking\n# Globalisation\n- **FDI:** automatic route up to 51 percent, later higher\n- **Foreign technology agreements**\n- **FIIs** allowed (1992)\n- **Joined WTO (1995)**\n- **FEMA 1999** replaced FERA\n# Financial Sector Reforms\n- **Narasimham Committee (1991, 1998)**\n- **SLR and CRR cut,** interest rates deregulated\n- **Private and foreign banks** allowed\n- **Prudential norms:** capital adequacy, NPAs\n- **SEBI** statutory body (1992), capital market reforms\n# Fiscal and Tax Reforms\n- **Chelliah Committee:** lower tax rates, broad base\n- **VAT, service tax,** later **GST (2017)**\n- **FRBM Act 2003**\n- **Reduced subsidy**\n# Evaluation: Merits\n- **Faster growth** (6-7 percent), forex reserves above 600 billion dollars\n- **IT and services boom,** exports rise\n- **Poverty decline,** more consumer choice\n- **Better efficiency** and competition\n# Evaluation: Demerits\n- **Jobless growth,** informal sector\n- **Agriculture neglect,** farmers' distress\n- **Rising inequality,** regional gaps\n- **Weak social sector** spending\n- **Crisis risk** from global integration\n- **MSME stress**\n# Second-generation Reforms\n- Labour, land, power, judiciary, infrastructure, IBC, GST\n> Conclusion: The 1991 reforms transformed the economy, but inclusive and employment-rich growth remains a challenge.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "What is land reform? Discuss the measures and their evaluation.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Land reform:** changes in the ownership, operation and distribution of land to remove inequality and raise productivity\n- **Aim:** social justice and efficiency (**land to the tiller**)\n- **Base:** Article 39(b) and (c), land is a State subject (List II)\n- **Ninth Schedule** protects land laws\n# Objectives\n- **Remove exploitation** by intermediaries\n- **Give security to tenants**\n- **Reduce inequality** in land holding\n- **Increase agricultural productivity**\n- **Increase rural employment** and income\n- **Establish village democracy**\n# Measures\n- **1. Abolition of intermediaries:** zamindari, jagirdari, inamdari ended in the 1950s (UP Zamindari Abolition Act 1950); about 2 crore tenants became owners\n- **2. Tenancy reforms:**\n- **Regulation of rent** (one-fourth to one-fifth of produce)\n- **Security of tenure**\n- **Ownership rights to tenants** (Kerala, West Bengal)\n- **3. Ceiling on land holdings:**\n- First ceiling laws around 1961, national guidelines in 1972\n- **Surplus land distributed** to landless and SC/ST\n- **4. Consolidation of holdings:** Punjab pioneer, reduces fragmentation\n- **5. Bhoodan and Gramdan:** Vinoba Bhave (1951, Pochampalli)\n- **6. Cooperative farming**\n- **7. Land records:** computerisation (DILRMP)\n- **8. Operation Barga (1977, West Bengal):** registration of sharecroppers\n- **9. Kerala Land Reforms Act 1963, 1969**\n# Evaluation: Successes\n- **Zamindari abolished** all over India\n- **Tenancy gains** in West Bengal, Kerala\n- **Reduced the power of landlords**\n- **Land ownership** to millions of tenants\n# Evaluation: Failures\n- **Loopholes in ceiling laws** (benami transfers, exemptions)\n- **Little surplus land** declared and distributed\n- **Tenants evicted,** informal oral tenancy\n- **Poor land records,** litigation\n- **Lack of political will** and administrative apathy\n- **Consolidation incomplete** in many states\n- **Cooperative farming** failed\n# Impact\n- **Equity** improved partly, productivity higher with owners\n- But rural inequality persists\n# Recent Concerns\n- **Land acquisition Act 2013 (RFCTLARR),** land titling, leasing laws, women's land rights\n# Suggestions\n- Better records, legalise tenancy, strong implementation, land banks, women's titles\n> Conclusion: Land reform had partial success; better records and tenancy legalisation are needed.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "Explain the Green Revolution. What are its achievements and limitations?", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Green Revolution:** large increase in food-grain output due to **high yielding varieties (HYV), fertilisers, irrigation and technology**\n- **Started:** 1966-67 in India (after 1965 droughts, PL-480 dependence)\n- **Persons:** **Norman Borlaug** (Mexican wheat), **M. S. Swaminathan** (India), C. Subramaniam, **Father of Green Revolution in India**\n- **Rice:** IR-8 variety; **wheat:** Sonora 64, Lerma Rojo\n# Components of the New Strategy\n- **HYV seeds** (wheat, rice, maize, bajra, jowar)\n- **Chemical fertilisers** and pesticides\n- **Assured irrigation** (tube wells, canals)\n- **Mechanisation:** tractors, threshers\n- **Credit** and institutions\n- **MSP and procurement,** FCI (1965)\n- **Research and extension:** ICAR, agricultural universities (Pantnagar)\n- **Intensive Agricultural District Programme (IADP, 1960),** HYV Programme (1966)\n# Achievements\n- **Foodgrain production:** from about 51 million tonnes (1950-51) to over 300 million tonnes now\n- **Self-sufficiency** in wheat and rice, end of food imports\n- **Buffer stock** and food security\n- **Higher farmers' income** in Punjab, Haryana, Western UP\n- **Rural employment** and agro-industries\n- **Modernisation:** machinery, fertiliser industry\n- **Lower food prices** and better nutrition\n- **Spread effect** to other crops (Operation Flood, Oilseed mission)\n# Limitations\n- **Regional inequality:** only Punjab, Haryana, Western UP, Andhra, Tamil Nadu\n- **Crop inequality:** mostly wheat and rice; pulses, oilseeds, coarse grains neglected\n- **Class inequality:** big farmers gained more, small farmers lagged\n- **Environmental harm:** groundwater depletion, soil salinity, pests, chemical pollution\n- **Dependence** on costly inputs, subsidies\n- **Health issues** from pesticides\n- **Reduced crop diversity**\n- **Rise of debt** among small farmers\n- **Unemployment due to mechanisation**\n# Second Green Revolution\n- **Eastern India:** Bihar, Odisha, West Bengal, Assam\n- **Evergreen revolution (Swaminathan):** sustainable and productive\n- **Bio-technology,** precision farming\n- **Water use efficiency,** micro-irrigation\n- **Organic farming,** conservation of soil\n- **Crop diversification**\n# Suggestions\n- Balanced use of fertilisers, rainfed area focus, research for small farmers\n> Conclusion: Green Revolution was a great success in output, but sustainability and inclusion need attention.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit III - Poverty and Population", "q": "Discuss the problem of unemployment in India. What measures have been taken?", "y": "2017, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Unemployment:** situation where able and willing persons do not get work at the going wage\n- **India:** large labour force, abundant labour, scarce jobs\n- **Measured by:** NSSO and **PLFS** (Periodic Labour Force Survey) using usual status, weekly status, daily status\n# Types of Unemployment in India\n- **Disguised unemployment:** more people than needed in agriculture (zero marginal productivity)\n- **Open unemployment:** educated and urban\n- **Seasonal unemployment:** agriculture off-season\n- **Structural unemployment:** mismatch of skills and jobs\n- **Cyclical unemployment:** recession\n- **Frictional unemployment:** job switching\n- **Technological unemployment**\n- **Educated unemployment** and underemployment\n# Causes\n- **Rapid growth of population** and labour force\n- **Slow growth of jobs** (jobless growth)\n- **Over-dependence on agriculture**\n- **Slow industrial growth,** weak manufacturing\n- **Defective education system,** skill mismatch\n- **Capital-intensive technology**\n- **Low rate of capital formation**\n- **Decline of cottage industry**\n- **Regional imbalance**\n- **Informal sector** with low-quality jobs\n- **Inadequate social security**\n- **Rigid labour laws** (argued)\n# Effects\n- **Poverty,** inequality\n- **Wastage of human resources**\n- **Social unrest,** crime, brain drain\n- **Low demand** and growth\n- **Mental stress**\n# Measures Taken\n- **MGNREGA 2005:** 100 days guaranteed wage employment\n- **Skill India (2015),** **PMKVY**\n- **Start-up India (2016),** **Stand-up India**\n- **Mudra Yojana (2015)**\n- **PMEGP (2008):** self-employment\n- **NRLM (DAY-NRLM),** DDU-GKY\n- **Make in India,** PLI, MSME support\n- **Digital India** and services\n- **Labour codes**\n- **Infrastructure projects**\n- **Atmanirbhar Bharat Rozgar Yojana**\n# Suggestions\n- **Labour-intensive manufacturing** and MSME growth\n- **Skill development** linked with industry\n- **Education reform** (vocational)\n- **Rural non-farm employment,** agro-processing\n- **Boost to services,** tourism, construction\n- **Population control** and women's participation\n# Evaluation\n- Schemes provide relief, but quality job creation is low\n> Conclusion: Labour-intensive growth with skill development is the lasting solution to unemployment.", "s": "Economics"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Discuss the state of society in France on the eve of the Revolution of 1789. What were the reasons for growing discontent among the social classes?", "y": "2015, 2022, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **French Revolution (1789):** a revolution against the **Ancien Regime** (old order) which ended absolute monarchy and feudal privileges\n- **Slogan:** Liberty, Equality, Fraternity\n- **Society on the eve of 1789:** unequal, divided into three estates\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2022, 2023\n# Social Structure: The Three Estates\n## First Estate: The Clergy (about 1-2 percent)\n- **Owned about 10 percent of land,** exempt from taille (paid voluntary gift)\n- **Collected the tithe** from peasants\n- **Higher clergy (bishops, abbots):** rich, noble-born, lived in luxury\n- **Lower clergy (parish priests):** poor, close to the people\n## Second Estate: The Nobility (about 1.5-2 percent)\n- **Owned about 25-30 percent of land**\n- **Exempt from most taxes**\n- **Held high posts** in army, church, administration\n- **Feudal rights:** hunting rights, seigneurial dues, corvee, banalities\n- **Court nobility** (Versailles) and **provincial nobility** (poor, resentful)\n- **Nobility of the robe** (judges) and **sword**\n## Third Estate (about 97 percent)\n- **Bourgeoisie:** merchants, lawyers, doctors, officials, manufacturers; rich and educated, but no political power or social honour\n- **Peasants:** about 80 percent of population; paid taille, tithe, feudal dues; owned small land\n- **Urban workers and artisans (sans-culottes):** poor, bread price high\n- **Landless labourers and beggars**\n# Political Conditions\n- **Absolute monarchy:** Louis XVI (1774-92), \"L'etat c'est moi\" tradition\n- **Weak and indecisive king,** influence of Marie Antoinette (the Austrian)\n- **No representation:** Estates General not summoned since 1614\n- **Lettres de cachet,** Bastille symbol of arbitrary power\n- **Corrupt, irregular administration,** sale of offices, many different laws and customs\n- **Parlements** protected privileges\n# Economic Conditions\n- **Heavy taxes on the poor:** taille (direct), gabelle (salt), aides, corvee (labour), tithe\n- **Tax farming** (Farmers-General) and inequality\n- **Financial crisis:** huge debt from Seven Years' War, American War of Independence; interest took about half of the budget\n- **Court expenditure** at Versailles\n- **Failed ministers:** Turgot, Necker, Calonne, Brienne could not tax privileged classes\n- **Bad harvest 1788, severe winter,** bread price rise\n- **Eden Treaty (1786):** British goods hurt French industry; unemployment\n# Intellectual Causes\n- **Philosophers (Enlightenment):** Montesquieu (separation of powers), Voltaire (against church and intolerance), Rousseau (general will, sovereignty of the people), Diderot (Encyclopedie)\n- **Physiocrats** and economists\n- **American Revolution (1776)** inspired\n- **Rise of public opinion,** pamphlets, salons\n# Reasons for Discontent Among Classes\n## Bourgeoisie\n- **Excluded from high offices** and honour\n- **Taxes and trade restrictions** (internal customs)\n- **Wanted equality and constitutional government**\n## Peasants\n- **Feudal dues, tithe, taxes**\n- **Hunger and bad harvest**\n- **Hated seigneurial rights**\n## Urban Poor\n- **High bread prices** and unemployment\n## Lower Clergy\n- **Poverty,** resentment against bishops\n## Nobility\n- **Provincial nobility** jealous of court\n- **Aristocratic revolt (1787-88),** refused tax reforms (Assembly of Notables)\n# Immediate Events\n- **Estates General (5 May 1789)** and dispute on voting by head\n- **National Assembly (17 June)** and **Tennis Court Oath (20 June)**\n- **Fall of the Bastille (14 July 1789)**\n- **Great Fear** in countryside\n- **August Decrees (4 August)** ending feudalism\n- **Declaration of the Rights of Man and Citizen (26 August)**\n# Historians' Views\n- **Marxist (Lefebvre, Soboul):** class struggle, bourgeois revolution\n- **Tocqueville:** revolution came when conditions were improving; centralisation of state\n- **Revisionists (Cobban):** no clear class divide; role of politics and ideas\n# Conclusion\n> A society based on privilege, a bankrupt and absolutist state, the rising bourgeoisie and Enlightenment ideas together caused the Revolution; the financial crisis was the spark, but social inequality and political exclusion were the deeper causes.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Explain why the French Revolution, which began as a movement for liberty and equality, developed into the Reign of Terror.", "y": "2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **French Revolution (1789):** began with ideals of **liberty, equality and fraternity**\n- **Reign of Terror (September 1793 - July 1794):** period of mass executions under the **Committee of Public Safety** led by **Robespierre**\n- **Question:** how did the liberal revolution turn into dictatorship and terror?\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2022\n# Stages of the Radicalisation\n## National Assembly (1789-91)\n- **August Decrees,** Declaration of the Rights of Man, feudalism abolished\n- **Civil Constitution of the Clergy (1790):** split the nation; many priests refused the oath\n- **Flight to Varennes (June 1791):** Louis XVI's attempt to flee destroyed trust in the king\n## Legislative Assembly (1791-92)\n- **War with Austria (April 1792)** and Prussia; early defeats\n- **Brunswick Manifesto (July 1792)** threatened Paris\n- **Insurrection of 10 August 1792:** Tuileries stormed; monarchy suspended\n- **September Massacres (1792)**\n## National Convention (1792-95)\n- **Republic proclaimed (21 September 1792)**\n- **Trial and execution of Louis XVI (21 January 1793)**\n- **First Coalition against France**\n# Causes of the Terror\n## External War\n- **Threat of foreign invasion** (Austria, Prussia, Britain, Spain)\n- **Levee en masse (August 1793),** total war required control\n## Civil War and Counter-revolution\n- **Vendee revolt (March 1793)** of peasants, priests and royalists\n- **Federalist revolts** in Lyon, Marseille, Toulon\n- **Fear of traitors and spies**\n## Economic Crisis\n- **Assignat inflation,** shortages and high bread prices\n- **Sans-culottes demanded price controls;** Law of the Maximum (1793)\n## Pressure of the Radical Masses\n- **Paris sans-culottes and Jacobins** forced the Convention to radical steps\n- **Fall of the Girondins (June 1793)**\n## Ideology\n- **Rousseau's general will** and the idea of virtue\n- **Robespierre: \"Terror is nothing but justice, prompt, severe, inflexible\"**\n- **Cult of the Supreme Being**\n## Weakness of State Authority\n- **Need for emergency government**\n# Machinery of the Terror\n- **Committee of Public Safety (April 1793)** with Robespierre (from July 1793)\n- **Committee of General Security**\n- **Revolutionary Tribunal** (Paris)\n- **Law of Suspects (September 1793),** **Law of 22 Prairial (June 1794)**\n- **Representatives on mission** in the provinces (Carrier at Nantes)\n- **About 17,000 official executions,** and tens of thousands died in prisons or civil war\n- **Victims:** Marie Antoinette (October 1793), Girondins, Danton (April 1794), Hebert\n# End of the Terror\n- **Military victories** (Fleurus, June 1794) removed need\n- **Thermidorian Reaction (27 July 1794):** Robespierre arrested and guillotined\n- **Directory (1795)**\n# Historians' Views\n- **Circumstances thesis** (Aulard, Mathiez): the Terror was caused by war and civil war\n- **Furet:** ideology and logic of revolution\n- **Marxist:** class struggle\n# Conclusion\n> The Revolution turned to terror because of foreign war, civil war, economic crisis, mass pressure and ideological extremism; the leaders used terror to save the Republic, but it betrayed the very liberty it claimed to defend.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Discuss the economic condition of France before the Revolution. Was it the most important cause of the French Revolution?", "y": "2023", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "The Napoleonic Empire was doomed because of its inherent and self-defeating contradictions. Elucidate.", "y": "2015", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Napoleon (1769-1821):** Emperor of the French (1804-14, 1815); built a vast European empire\n- **Statement:** the empire fell because of **inherent, self-defeating contradictions**\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015\n# Contradictions Within the Empire\n## Revolution vs Despotism\n- **Napoleon spread revolutionary ideals** (equality, Code Napoleon), yet became an **autocrat,** controlled press and elections\n- **Contradiction** between liberty and dictatorship\n## Nationalism\n- **Napoleon's conquests awakened national feeling** against French rule: Spain, Germany, Russia, Italy, Tyrol\n- **Reforms in Prussia** (Stein, Hardenberg, Scharnhorst), Fichte's Addresses\n- **Peoples preferred their own rule** though under liberal laws\n## Dynastic Nepotism\n- **Placed brothers and relatives** on thrones (Joseph in Spain, Louis in Holland, Jerome in Westphalia)\n- **Contradicts the principle** of merit and republic\n## Constant War\n- **Empire depended on endless war;** conscription, high taxes\n- **Exhaustion of France** and its resources\n# Immediate Causes of Downfall\n## The Continental System (1806-12)\n- **Berlin Decrees (1806)** and Milan Decrees (1807) to ruin British trade\n- **Britain controlled the sea;** blockade harmed the continent more\n- **Smuggling,** unemployment, distress in Europe\n- **Russia withdrew (1810),** which led to the invasion of Russia\n## The Peninsular War (1808-14)\n- **\"The Spanish ulcer\":** Spain's national guerrilla resistance, British aid (Wellington)\n- **Drained** French armies\n## The Russian Campaign (1812)\n- **Grande Armee of about 6 lakh** entered Russia\n- **Scorched earth policy;** Moscow burned; retreat in winter\n- **Almost the whole army lost**\n## The War of Liberation (1813-14)\n- **Prussia, Austria, Russia, Britain, Sweden** united\n- **Battle of Leipzig (Battle of Nations, October 1813)**\n- **Paris fell (March 1814);** Napoleon abdicated and went to Elba\n## The Hundred Days (1815)\n- **Returned from Elba;** defeated at **Waterloo (18 June 1815)** by Wellington and Blucher\n- **Exiled to St Helena**\n# Napoleon's Mistakes\n- **Underestimated the power of nationalism and Britain's navy**\n- **Overextended his empire**\n- **Quarrel with the Pope;** Concordat gave no lasting support\n- **Neglect of the navy** (Trafalgar 1805)\n# Legacy\n- **Code Napoleon,** centralised administration, abolished feudalism in many places, legal equality\n- **Spread of nationalism and liberalism**\n- **Basis of later unification of Italy and Germany**\n# Conclusion\n> Napoleon's empire carried the seeds of its own destruction: despotism against liberty, conquest against nationalism, and an unending war economy; the Continental System, Spain and Russia merely hastened its end.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "What were the main features of the Industrial Revolution in Europe? Discuss its impact on European society.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Industrial Revolution:** change from hand production and farm economy to machine production and the factory system\n- **Began in Britain (about 1760-1840);** spread to Belgium, France, Germany, USA\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016, 2018\n# Why It Began in Britain\n- **Agricultural revolution:** enclosures, new crops, food surplus, labour freed\n- **Capital** from trade and colonies, banks\n- **Coal and iron** resources\n- **Colonies** supplied raw material and markets\n- **Political stability,** Parliament protected property\n- **Growing population;** navy and trade\n# Main Features: Inventions and Technology\n## Textiles\n- **Flying shuttle (Kay, 1733)**\n- **Spinning jenny (Hargreaves, 1764)**\n- **Water frame (Arkwright, 1769)**\n- **Mule (Crompton, 1779)**\n- **Power loom (Cartwright, 1785)**\n- **Cotton gin (Whitney, 1793)**\n## Steam Power\n- **Newcomen engine (1712),** **James Watt's improved steam engine (1769, 1776)**\n## Iron and Coal\n- **Abraham Darby:** coke smelting; **Cort:** puddling process (1784)\n- **Safety lamp (Davy)**\n## Transport and Communication\n- **Canals,** Macadam roads\n- **Stephenson's Rocket (1829),** Liverpool-Manchester railway (1830)\n- **Steamships,** telegraph (Morse, 1837)\n# Other Features\n- **Factory system** replaced the domestic system\n- **Capitalism:** profit-based, laissez-faire economy\n- **Division of labour,** mass production\n- **Banking and joint-stock companies**\n- **Urbanisation:** Manchester, Birmingham, Leeds\n# Impact on European Society\n## Social\n- **Two new classes:** industrial capitalists (bourgeoisie) and the proletariat (workers)\n- **Urbanisation and slums,** poor housing, disease\n- **Child and women labour,** long hours, low wages\n- **Decline of artisans** and handloom weavers; Luddites\n## Reforms\n- **Factory Acts (1833, 1847 Ten Hours Act)**\n- **Trade unions (Combination Acts repealed 1824-25)**\n- **Chartism (1838)**\n- **Poor Law 1834**\n## Economic\n- **Rapid growth,** more goods and wealth\n- **Imperialism:** search for raw materials and markets\n- **Free trade** policies\n## Political\n- **Rise of liberalism, democracy** (Reform Act of 1832)\n- **Socialism:** Robert Owen, Saint-Simon, Marx\n- **Nationalism** and railways helped unity\n## Intellectual\n- **Utilitarianism,** economic theories (Smith, Ricardo, Malthus)\n# Spread\n- **Belgium (1830s),** France (after 1815), Germany (Zollverein, 1834), USA, Russia, Japan (later)\n# Stages of Industrialisation\n- **First stage (1760-1830):** textiles, coal, iron, steam power, canals\n- **Second stage (1870-1914):** steel (Bessemer, Siemens), electricity, chemicals, oil, motor cars\n- **Spread:** Belgium first on the continent, then France and Germany\n# Economic Doctrines\n- **Adam Smith (1776),** David Ricardo, Malthus\n- **Laissez-faire,** free trade (Corn Laws repealed 1846)\n- **Joint stock companies** and banks\n# Protests and Reactions\n- **Luddites (1811-16):** machine breaking\n- **Peterloo (1819)**\n- **Chartism (1838-48):** Six Points\n- **Co-operative movement:** Robert Owen (New Lanark), Rochdale Pioneers (1844)\n- **Socialism and Marx**\n# Environment and Health\n- **Pollution,** coal smoke, polluted rivers, cholera epidemics\n- **Public Health Act (1848)**\n# Conclusion\n> The Industrial Revolution changed production, society, politics and ideas; it created wealth and a modern working class but also misery, leading to reform and socialist movements.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Describe the objectives, policies and settlements of the Congress of Vienna.", "y": "2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Congress of Vienna (September 1814 - June 1815):** meeting of European powers to settle Europe after the defeat of Napoleon\n- **Host:** Austria (Metternich)\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2019\n# Participants\n- **Austria:** Prince Metternich (leading figure)\n- **Russia:** Tsar Alexander I\n- **Britain:** Lord Castlereagh (later Wellington)\n- **Prussia:** Hardenberg and Humboldt\n- **France:** Talleyrand (skilled; got France a place)\n# Objectives and Principles\n## Legitimacy\n- **Restore the old ruling families** overthrown by the Revolution and Napoleon (Bourbons in France, Spain, Naples)\n## Balance of Power\n- **No power should dominate Europe;** France to be contained by strong neighbours\n## Compensation\n- **Winners to be rewarded;** losers to lose land\n## Reaction\n- **Check revolution,** liberalism and nationalism\n# Settlements\n## France\n- **Bourbons restored (Louis XVIII);** boundaries of 1790\n- **Second Treaty of Paris (1815)** after the Hundred Days: indemnity (700 million francs), occupation for five years, borders of 1790\n## Netherlands\n- **Belgium joined Holland** as the United Kingdom of the Netherlands (a barrier against France)\n## Prussia\n- **Rhineland, Westphalia,** part of Saxony, Swedish Pomerania\n## Austria\n- **Lombardy and Venetia,** Illyria, Tyrol, Salzburg\n## Russia\n- **Congress Poland** (with Tsar as King), Finland, Bessarabia\n## Britain\n- **Malta, Ceylon, Cape Colony, Heligoland,** Ionian Islands protectorate; naval supremacy\n## Sweden and Norway\n- **Norway given to Sweden** (Denmark lost)\n## Germany\n- **German Confederation of 39 states** presided over by Austria (Diet at Frankfurt)\n## Italy\n- **Divided:** Austrian control in the north, Papal States, Bourbons of Naples\n## Switzerland\n- **Neutrality guaranteed**\n# The Concert of Europe\n- **Holy Alliance (September 1815)** of Russia, Austria, Prussia\n- **Quadruple Alliance (November 1815)** (Britain, Austria, Russia, Prussia), later Quintuple (France, 1818)\n- **Congress system:** Aix-la-Chapelle (1818), Troppau (1820), Laibach (1821), Verona (1822)\n# Evaluation\n## Successes\n- **Peace for about 40 years** (no general war till the Crimean War)\n- **Balance of power** and diplomatic cooperation (Concert)\n- **Strong barriers around France**\n## Failures\n- **Ignored nationalism and liberalism** (Italy, Germany, Belgium, Poland)\n- **Artificial arrangements** (Belgium-Holland, Norway-Sweden)\n- **Reactionary:** revolutions of 1820, 1830 and 1848\n- **Metternich's repression**\n# Conclusion\n> The Congress of Vienna restored the old order and the balance of power and brought a long peace, but it neglected the new forces of nationalism and liberalism, which broke it down in the nineteenth century.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Critically examine the causes and effects of the revolutions of 1830 and 1848 in Europe.", "y": "2017, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Revolutions of 1830 and 1848:** two waves of liberal and national revolts against the **Vienna settlement (1815) and the Metternich system**\n- **1848:** \"the Springtime of the Peoples\"\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2017, 2022\n# Revolutions of 1830\n## Causes\n- **Reaction after 1815** (Holy Alliance, Metternich), repressive rule\n- **Liberal ideas** and nationalism from the French Revolution\n- **Economic distress** and bad harvests\n- **Charles X's ordinances of St Cloud (July 1830)** in France\n## Events\n- **France:** July Revolution (three glorious days); **Charles X fled;** **Louis Philippe** became the \"Citizen King\" (bourgeois monarchy)\n- **Belgium:** revolt against Dutch rule; independent (1830-31, guaranteed neutrality 1839)\n- **Poland:** revolt (1830-31) crushed by Russia\n- **Italy and Germany:** small risings in Modena, Parma, Papal States, and minor German states, put down by Austria\n- **Britain:** Reform Act of 1832\n## Effects\n- **Belgian independence** and a blow to the Vienna settlement\n- **Liberal bourgeois rule in France**\n- **Metternich system weakened,** but not broken\n# Revolutions of 1848\n## Causes\n- **Economic crisis:** potato blight (1845-47), bad harvests, unemployment\n- **Political repression** and demand for constitutions\n- **Nationalism:** Italians, Germans, Hungarians, Czechs\n- **Socialist ideas:** Louis Blanc, Marx (Communist Manifesto, 1848)\n- **Rise of working class** and middle-class dissatisfaction\n- **Failure of Louis Philippe's government (Guizot)**\n## Events\n- **France:** February Revolution, ban on banquets, **Louis Philippe abdicated;** **Second Republic;** National Workshops; **June Days;** Louis Napoleon elected President (December 1848)\n- **Austria:** Vienna revolt (March), **Metternich fled;** serfdom abolished; **Hungary (Kossuth);** Bohemia (Prague Slav Congress)\n- **Italy:** revolt in Lombardy, Venetia; **Charles Albert** fought Austria (defeated at Custoza and Novara)\n- **Germany:** **Frankfurt Parliament;** King of Prussia refused the crown (1849)\n# Causes of Failure of 1848\n- **Disunity among liberals, nationalists and socialists**\n- **Army remained loyal** to rulers\n- **Lack of mass support** in many areas\n- **Conflict between nationalities** (Slavs vs Germans vs Magyars)\n- **Russian help** to Austria in Hungary (1849)\n- **Fear of social revolution** among the middle class\n# Effects\n- **End of serfdom** in Austria and Hungary\n- **Constitutions** in Prussia (1850) and Piedmont (the Statuto)\n- **Napoleon III** in France (Second Empire, 1852)\n- **Marx and socialism** gained\n- **Lessons:** unification needed force (Bismarck, Cavour)\n- **Metternich system finished**\n# Conclusion\n> The revolutions of 1830 and 1848 failed in many places but broke the old order, gave Belgium independence and spread liberalism and nationalism, preparing the unification of Italy and Germany.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Trace the different stages of the unification of Germany. What was the role of Bismarck?", "y": "2016, 2017, 2019, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Unification of Germany (1871):** creation of one German Empire out of 39 states of the German Confederation\n- **Led by:** Prussia, under **Otto von Bismarck** and King William I\n- **Method:** wars and diplomacy, \"blood and iron\" (30 September 1862 speech)\n- **Importance:** changed the balance of power in Europe; a new strong state in the centre of Europe\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016, 2017, 2019, 2023\n# Background: Forces Behind Unification\n## Political Background\n- **Holy Roman Empire** (about 300 states) ended in 1806 by Napoleon\n- **Confederation of the Rhine (1806):** Napoleon reduced the states to about 39\n- **Congress of Vienna (1815):** German Confederation of 39 states under Austrian presidency (Metternich)\n- **Austria-Prussia rivalry** for leadership of Germany\n## Intellectual and Cultural Forces\n- **German nationalism:** Fichte (Addresses to the German Nation), Herder, Hegel, Grimm brothers, Arndt\n- **Romanticism** and common language and culture\n- **Burschenschaften** (student societies), Wartburg festival 1817\n## Economic Forces\n- **Zollverein (1834):** customs union led by Prussia, excluding Austria\n- **Railways, coal and iron** of the Ruhr and Saar, industrial growth of Prussia\n- **Economic unity** prepared political unity\n## Failure of 1848\n- **Revolutions of 1848** and **Frankfurt Parliament**\n- **Crown offered to Frederick William IV** of Prussia (1849), who refused it (\"a crown from the gutter\")\n- **Humiliation of Olmutz (1850):** Prussia gave up the Erfurt Union plan to Austria\n- **Lesson:** unity could not be achieved by liberal speeches\n# Bismarck and His Policy\n## Rise of Bismarck\n- **Appointed Minister-President (1862)** during the army reform crisis (King vs Liberal Landtag)\n- **Realpolitik:** policy based on realities and power, not ideals\n- **\"Not by speeches and majority resolutions but by iron and blood\"**\n- **Army reform:** Von Roon (War Minister), Von Moltke (Chief of General Staff), needle gun\n## Aims\n- **Unite Germany** under Prussia, exclude Austria\n- **Strengthen the monarchy** and conservative order\n# Stages of Unification\n## Stage 1: The Danish War (1864)\n- **Issue:** Schleswig-Holstein question (Danish succession, German population)\n- **Austria and Prussia** jointly defeated Denmark\n- **Treaty of Vienna (1864):** duchies handed over to Austria and Prussia\n- **Convention of Gastein (1865):** Prussia got Schleswig, Austria got Holstein\n- **Result:** Bismarck created a quarrel with Austria\n## Stage 2: The Austro-Prussian War (1866)\n- **Diplomatic preparation:** Russia kept friendly (help in the Polish revolt 1863); Napoleon III promised neutrality (Biarritz meeting 1865); alliance with Italy (April 1866, Venetia promised)\n- **Excuse:** dispute over Holstein\n- **Battle of Sadowa (Koniggratz), 3 July 1866:** Prussia's victory, a \"Seven Weeks' War\"\n- **Treaty of Prague (1866):** Austria was excluded from Germany, no heavy terms for Austria\n- **North German Confederation (1867):** 22 states north of the Main under Prussia\n- **Hanover, Hesse-Cassel, Nassau and Frankfurt** annexed\n## Stage 3: The Franco-Prussian War (1870-71)\n- **Cause:** Spanish throne dispute (Hohenzollern candidate Leopold)\n- **Ems Telegram (13 July 1870):** edited by Bismarck to anger France\n- **France declared war (19 July 1870)**\n- **South German states** joined Prussia due to national feeling\n- **Battle of Sedan (2 September 1870):** Napoleon III captured\n- **Siege of Paris,** fall of the Second Empire\n- **Treaty of Frankfurt (May 1871):** Alsace-Lorraine to Germany, indemnity of 5 billion francs\n- **Proclamation of the German Empire (18 January 1871)** at the Hall of Mirrors, Versailles; William I became Kaiser\n# Constitution of the New Empire\n- **Federal structure:** Reichstag (elected by male suffrage) and Bundesrat (princes' representatives)\n- **Emperor** (Prussian king) controlled army and foreign policy\n- **Chancellor** (Bismarck) responsible only to the Emperor\n# Causes of Success\n- **Strong Prussian army** and military reform\n- **Bismarck's diplomacy:** isolation of each enemy\n- **Economic strength** of Prussia and Zollverein\n- **Weak and divided opponents:** Austria's internal problems, France's isolation\n- **German national sentiment**\n- **Mistakes of Napoleon III**\n# Role of Bismarck: Evaluation\n- **Architect of unity:** planned wars and diplomacy\n- **Other views:** A. J. P. Taylor stresses that Bismarck used events as they came, not a master plan\n- **Liberal nationalists** and economic forces also helped\n- **Limits:** the unification excluded Austrian Germans (Kleindeutsch solution)\n# Consequences\n- **New power** in Europe, balance of power changed\n- **Bismarckian alliance system** (1873-90)\n- **Alsace-Lorraine** caused French hatred (a cause of the First World War)\n- **Rise of militarism**\n# Conclusion\n> Germany was unified by Prussian arms and Bismarck's diplomacy, helped by economic and national forces; it created a mighty empire which changed European politics.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Discuss the various stages of the unification of Italy. Assess the contributions of Mazzini, Cavour and Garibaldi.", "y": "2015, 2018, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Unification of Italy (Risorgimento = resurrection):** the process of uniting the many Italian states into one Kingdom of Italy (1861-1870)\n- **Leaders:** Mazzini (soul), Cavour (brain), Garibaldi (sword), Victor Emmanuel II (king)\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2018, 2022\n# Background\n## Condition after 1815\n- **Congress of Vienna divided Italy:** Lombardy and Venetia (Austria), Piedmont-Sardinia (Savoy), Papal States, Kingdom of the Two Sicilies (Bourbons), duchies of Parma, Modena, Tuscany\n- **Metternich:** \"Italy is a mere geographical expression\"\n- **Austria dominated** Italian affairs\n## Forces Helping Unity\n- **Napoleonic reforms:** laws, administration, nationalism\n- **Carbonari** secret societies; revolts of 1820-21 (Naples, Piedmont) and 1830-31 (Modena, Parma, Papal States) failed\n- **Literature and national feeling:** Dante, Manzoni, Verdi\n- **Economic:** railways, customs unions\n# Role of the Leaders\n## Mazzini (1805-72)\n- **Founded Young Italy (1831)** at Marseilles: republic, unity by the people\n- **\"Italy must make herself\" (Italia fara da se)**\n- **Inspired youth,** led the Roman Republic (1849)\n- **Weakness:** idealistic, no army or diplomacy\n## Garibaldi (1807-82)\n- **Soldier and patriot,** Red Shirts, defender of the Roman Republic (1849)\n- **Conquered Sicily and Naples (1860)** with the Thousand\n## Cavour (1810-61)\n- **Prime Minister of Piedmont (1852)**\n- **Modernised Piedmont:** economy, army, railways, free trade\n- **Diplomatic genius:** used foreign help for Italian cause\n- **Realist,** monarchist and liberal\n# Stages of Unification\n## Stage 1: Revolutions of 1848-49\n- **Charles Albert of Piedmont** declared war on Austria\n- **Defeated at Custoza (1848) and Novara (1849)**\n- **Roman Republic** (Mazzini, Garibaldi) crushed by French troops\n- **Lesson:** Piedmont leads, foreign help needed\n## Stage 2: Cavour and Crimean War\n- **Piedmont joined Crimean War (1855)** with Britain and France\n- **Congress of Paris (1856):** Cavour raised the Italian question\n## Stage 3: War of 1859\n- **Pact of Plombieres (July 1858)** with Napoleon III: help for Nice and Savoy\n- **War with Austria (1859):** victories at Magenta and Solferino\n- **Napoleon III made peace at Villafranca (July 1859):** Lombardy to Piedmont\n- **Plebiscites (1860):** Tuscany, Parma, Modena, Romagna joined Piedmont\n- **Treaty of Turin (1860):** Nice and Savoy to France\n## Stage 4: Garibaldi's Expedition (1860)\n- **Landed at Marsala (11 May 1860)** with 1,000 Red Shirts\n- **Conquered Sicily,** then Naples\n- **Cavour sent Piedmont army** through Papal States (to prevent a republic)\n- **Teano meeting (26 October 1860):** Garibaldi handed over the conquests to Victor Emmanuel\n- **Plebiscites** approved union\n## Stage 5: Kingdom of Italy (1861)\n- **Proclaimed 17 March 1861;** Victor Emmanuel II king\n- **Cavour died** in June 1861\n## Stage 6: Venetia (1866)\n- **Alliance with Prussia** in the Austro-Prussian War\n- **Venetia added** by the treaty of Vienna\n## Stage 7: Rome (1870)\n- **French troops withdrew** because of the Franco-Prussian War\n- **Italian army entered Rome (20 September 1870)**; capital shifted in 1871\n- **Law of Guarantees (1871);** conflict with Pope (the Roman Question)\n# Causes of Success\n- **Piedmont's leadership** and Cavour's diplomacy\n- **Foreign help:** France (1859), Prussia (1866)\n- **Patriotism** and Garibaldi's daring\n- **Weak Austria** and Bourbon rule\n# Incomplete Unification\n- **Italia Irredenta** (Trentino, Trieste) was added only after the First World War\n- **North-South gap**\n# Conclusion\n> Italian unification was achieved by the combined work of Mazzini's idealism, Cavour's diplomacy and Garibaldi's valour, backed by Piedmont's army and foreign help.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Discuss the Eastern Question from the Greek War of Independence (1821) to the Congress of Berlin (1878).", "y": "2016, 2022, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Eastern Question:** problem arising from the **decline of the Ottoman (Turkish) Empire** and the **rivalry of the European powers** over its territory and control of the Straits\n- **\"Sick man of Europe\"** (Tsar Nicholas I, 1853)\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016, 2022, 2023 (Berlin Congress)\n# Meaning and Causes of the Ottoman Decline\n- **Weak Sultans,** corrupt administration, military decay\n- **Subject Christian nations** (Greeks, Serbs, Bulgarians, Romanians) wanted freedom\n- **Nationalism** from the French Revolution\n- **Powers' interference** and Capitulations\n- **Treaty of Kuchuk Kainarji (1774):** Russia got the right to protect Orthodox Christians\n# Interests of the Powers\n## Russia\n- **Warm-water port** and control of the Straits (Bosphorus, Dardanelles)\n- **Pan-Slavism** and Orthodox religion\n## Britain\n- **Route to India** and Mediterranean trade\n- **Prevent Russian expansion;** support for Ottoman integrity (Palmerston, Disraeli)\n## Austria\n- **Expand into the Balkans,** fear of Slav nationalism\n## France\n- **Catholic interests,** holy places, Mediterranean influence\n# Stages of the Eastern Question\n## 1. Greek War of Independence (1821-29)\n- **Revolt of Greeks in 1821;** Ottoman brutality (Chios massacre)\n- **Philhellenism** (Byron)\n- **Battle of Navarino (1827):** Anglo-French-Russian fleet destroyed Turkish-Egyptian fleet\n- **Treaty of Adrianople (1829);** Greece independent by London Protocol (1830)\n## 2. Mehmet Ali Crisis (1831-41)\n- **Egypt's Mehmet Ali** attacked the Sultan\n- **Treaty of Unkiar Skelessi (1833):** Russia-Turkey alliance\n- **London Convention / Straits Convention (1841):** Straits closed to warships in peace\n## 3. Crimean War (1853-56)\n- **Dispute over holy places;** Nicholas I demanded protection of Orthodox subjects\n- **Britain and France joined Turkey**\n- **Treaty of Paris (1856):** Black Sea neutralised, Danubian principalities under European guarantee, Ottoman integrity guaranteed\n## 4. Balkan Crisis (1875-78)\n- **Revolt in Bosnia and Herzegovina (1875),** Bulgarian atrocities (1876), Gladstone's pamphlet\n- **Russo-Turkish War (1877-78)**\n- **Treaty of San Stefano (March 1878):** large Bulgaria (Russian influence)\n- **Britain and Austria objected**\n## 5. Congress of Berlin (1878)\n- **Presided by Bismarck** (honest broker)\n- **Big Bulgaria divided:** Bulgaria (autonomous), Eastern Rumelia, Macedonia to Turkey\n- **Serbia, Montenegro, Romania independent**\n- **Austria occupied Bosnia-Herzegovina**\n- **Britain got Cyprus**\n- **Russia kept Kars, Batum, Bessarabia**\n# Response of Britain and Russia\n- **Britain:** supported Turkey to check Russia (Crimean War, Disraeli at Berlin)\n- **Russia:** supported Balkan Christians, aggressive in 1877-78\n# Consequences\n- **Balkan nationalism** continued; Balkans became \"powder keg of Europe\"\n- **Russia felt cheated,** hostility to Austria and Germany\n- **Anglo-Russian rivalry,** later alliance system\n- **Ground for the First World War** (Bosnia crisis 1908, Balkan Wars 1912-13)\n# Conclusion\n> The Eastern Question was the central problem of nineteenth-century diplomacy; the powers' rivalry preserved the Ottoman Empire for a time but left the Balkan problem which led to the First World War.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Was the question of slavery the main cause of the American Civil War (1860-65)? Discuss.", "y": "2016, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **American Civil War (1861-65):** war between the **Union (North)** and the **Confederacy (South)** of eleven seceded states\n- **Question:** was slavery the main cause?\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016, 2023\n# Background: North and South\n- **North:** industrial, free labour, cities, immigrants, protective tariff\n- **South:** agricultural, plantations (cotton, tobacco), about 4 million slaves\n- **Cotton gin (1793)** made slavery profitable\n# Slavery as the Central Cause\n## Expansion of Slavery in the Territories\n- **Missouri Compromise (1820):** Missouri slave, Maine free, 36°30' line\n- **Compromise of 1850:** California free, **Fugitive Slave Act**\n- **Kansas-Nebraska Act (1854):** popular sovereignty; **Bleeding Kansas**\n- **Dred Scott decision (1857):** slaves not citizens, Missouri Compromise void\n## Abolitionism\n- **William Lloyd Garrison (The Liberator, 1831),** Frederick Douglass\n- **Harriet Beecher Stowe, Uncle Tom's Cabin (1852):** moved the North\n- **John Brown's raid at Harpers Ferry (1859)**\n## Politics\n- **Republican Party (1854)** opposed expansion of slavery\n- **Abraham Lincoln elected (November 1860)**\n- **South Carolina seceded (December 1860);** **Confederacy (February 1861),** Jefferson Davis\n- **Declarations of secession** put slavery first\n- **Alexander Stephens (\"Cornerstone speech\"):** slavery is the cornerstone of the Confederacy\n## Immediate Cause\n- **Fort Sumter attacked (12 April 1861)**\n# Other Causes\n## Economic Differences\n- **Industrial North vs agrarian South;** tariff issue (Tariff of 1828, 1832 nullification crisis)\n- **Railways, banks** divided the sections\n## States' Rights vs Federal Power\n- **Southerners:** states can nullify or secede (Calhoun)\n- **North:** Union is perpetual\n## Sectionalism and Culture\n- **Different social orders,** growing mistrust\n## Political Failure\n- **Compromises failed;** weak presidents (Buchanan)\n# Assessment\n- **Slavery was the core issue,** because tariffs, states' rights and territories were all connected to slavery\n- **States' rights** meant the right to protect slavery\n- **Lincoln's original aim:** to preserve the Union (letter to Greeley 1862), not to abolish slavery\n- **Emancipation Proclamation (1 January 1863)** made abolition a war aim\n# Course and Results\n- **Key battles:** Bull Run, Antietam, **Gettysburg (July 1863),** Vicksburg\n- **Lee surrendered at Appomattox (9 April 1865);** Lincoln assassinated\n- **13th Amendment (1865):** slavery abolished\n- **600,000+ dead,** Union saved\n- **Reconstruction,** 14th and 15th Amendments\n- **Rise of industrial capitalism** in the North\n# Conclusion\n> Slavery was the central cause of the American Civil War, linked with economic and constitutional issues; the quarrel over its expansion made the compromise impossible and led to secession and war.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Discuss the causes of the First World War. Was it inevitable?", "y": "2015", "a": "# Introduction\n- **First World War (1914-18):** global war between the **Allies (Triple Entente)** and the **Central Powers**\n- **Immediate cause:** assassination at Sarajevo (28 June 1914)\n- **Question:** was it inevitable?\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015\n# Long-term Causes\n## Alliance System\n- **Dual Alliance (1879):** Germany and Austria-Hungary\n- **Triple Alliance (1882):** with Italy\n- **Franco-Russian Alliance (1894)**\n- **Entente Cordiale (1904)** (Britain-France), **Anglo-Russian Entente (1907)** gave the **Triple Entente**\n- **Europe divided into two armed camps**\n## Militarism and Arms Race\n- **Large armies and conscription**\n- **Anglo-German naval rivalry:** Dreadnought (1906), Tirpitz's navy laws\n- **Military plans (Schlieffen Plan)** needed quick mobilisation\n## Imperialism and Economic Rivalry\n- **Scramble for colonies,** trade competition\n- **Moroccan crises (1905, 1911),** Berlin-Baghdad railway\n- **Germany's Weltpolitik** under Kaiser Wilhelm II\n## Nationalism\n- **French desire for revenge (Alsace-Lorraine)**\n- **Pan-Slavism** (Russia) and **Pan-Germanism**\n- **Serbian nationalism** against Austria-Hungary; national minorities in the Habsburg and Ottoman empires\n## Balkan Crises\n- **Bosnian Crisis (1908):** Austria annexed Bosnia\n- **Balkan Wars (1912-13)** raised Serbia's power\n## Secret Diplomacy and Press\n- **Secret treaties,** suspicion, aggressive newspapers\n# Immediate Cause\n- **Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand** by Gavrilo Princip (Black Hand/Young Bosnia), Sarajevo, 28 June 1914\n- **Blank cheque** from Germany (5 July)\n- **Austrian ultimatum to Serbia (23 July);** Serbia accepted most\n- **Austria declared war on Serbia (28 July)**\n- **Russia mobilised;** **Germany declared war on Russia (1 August),** France (3 August)\n- **Invasion of Belgium (Schlieffen Plan);** **Britain declared war (4 August)**\n# Was It Inevitable?\n## Yes (Structural)\n- **Alliances and arms race** made a major war likely\n- **Imperial and nationalist tensions**\n## No (Contingent)\n- **Crisis of July 1914** could have been settled by diplomacy\n- **Decisions of Vienna, Berlin, St Petersburg** and mistakes (Lloyd George: \"we all slid into war\")\n# Historians' Views\n- **Fritz Fischer:** Germany's aggressive war aims\n- **Versailles Article 231:** war guilt of Germany\n- **Christopher Clark (The Sleepwalkers):** shared responsibility\n# Conclusion\n> The First World War was the result of the alliance system, militarism, imperial rivalry and nationalism; the Sarajevo assassination was only the spark, and the war was not completely inevitable.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "Discuss the rise of the United States of America as a world power in the beginning of the twentieth century.", "y": "2015", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Rise of the USA as a world power:** from an isolated republic to a global power by 1900-1917\n- **Turning points:** industrial growth, Spanish-American War (1898), Panama Canal, role in the First World War\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015\n# Background Factors\n- **Vast territory and rich resources** (coal, iron, oil, cotton)\n- **Growing population and immigration**\n- **Geographical security** between two oceans\n- **Monroe Doctrine (1823):** Western Hemisphere closed to European colonisation\n- **Union victory in the Civil War** (1865) strengthened the nation\n# Economic Growth\n- **Rapid industrialisation after 1865:** steel (Carnegie), oil (Rockefeller), railways (transcontinental 1869)\n- **By 1900 the largest industrial output** in the world, surpassing Britain\n- **Big business and trusts;** capital for investment abroad\n- **Need for markets and raw materials** led to expansion\n# Imperial Expansion\n## Spanish-American War (1898)\n- **Cause:** Cuban revolt, sinking of the Maine (Feb 1898), yellow press\n- **Treaty of Paris (1898):** USA got **Philippines, Guam, Puerto Rico;** Cuba under US influence (Platt Amendment 1901)\n## Hawaii and Pacific\n- **Annexation of Hawaii (1898),** Samoa\n## China\n- **Open Door Policy (John Hay, 1899-1900):** equal trade rights in China\n# Policies of Theodore Roosevelt (1901-09)\n- **\"Speak softly and carry a big stick\"**\n- **Panama Canal:** support for Panama's independence (1903), Hay-Bunau-Varilla Treaty, canal opened 1914\n- **Roosevelt Corollary (1904):** the USA as an \"international police power\" in Latin America\n- **Mediated Russo-Japanese War (Treaty of Portsmouth 1905);** Nobel Peace Prize 1906\n- **Great White Fleet (1907-09)** showed naval power\n- **Algeciras Conference (1906)**\n# Taft and Wilson\n- **Taft's Dollar Diplomacy:** economic influence in Latin America and China\n- **Wilson's \"moral diplomacy,\"** interventions in Mexico (1914), Haiti and Dominican Republic\n# First World War\n- **Neutral at first,** supplier of arms and loans to the Allies\n- **Entry in 1917:** German unrestricted submarine warfare, Zimmermann telegram\n- **Wilson's Fourteen Points and the League** idea\n- **Became world's creditor;** emerged as a leading power\n# Why the USA Rose\n- **Resources, industry and capital**\n- **Navy** (Mahan's sea power ideas)\n- **Strong leaders** and national confidence\n- **European exhaustion**\n# Limits\n- **Isolationism after 1919** (Senate rejected the Treaty and League)\n# Conclusion\n> The USA became a world power through its industrial strength, imperial expansion after 1898, an active foreign policy under Roosevelt and its decisive role in the First World War.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "Examine critically the causes of the Opium Wars (1840-42) in China. To what extent did they open China to the West?", "y": "2015, 2017", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Opium Wars:** two wars between Britain and China (First, 1839-42; Second, 1856-60)\n- **Result:** opening of China to the West through unequal treaties\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2017\n# Background: The Canton System\n- **Trade only at Canton (1757)** through the Cohong merchants\n- **China's self-sufficiency;** Emperor's Celestial Empire view\n- **Britain wanted free trade;** buying tea, silk with silver\n- **Trade imbalance** (silver outflow from Britain)\n# Causes of the First Opium War\n## Opium Trade\n- **British (East India Company) sold Indian opium to China** to correct the imbalance\n- **Opium imports grew:** about 1,000 chests (1800) to about 40,000 (1838)\n- **Silver drained from China,** addiction, corruption\n- **Chinese ban** on opium (edicts)\n## Commercial and Political Causes\n- **End of Company's trade monopoly (1833)** and free-trade desire\n- **Chinese restrictions** and the Canton system, limited ports\n- **Diplomatic dispute:** China refused equal status and embassy (kowtow, Lord Napier 1834 failed)\n- **Legal issue:** Chinese refusal to surrender Britons to British courts (extraterritoriality)\n## Immediate Cause\n- **Commissioner Lin Zexu (1839)** seized and destroyed about 20,000 chests of opium\n- **Incidents** at Kowloon; Britain sent a naval expedition\n# First Opium War (1839-42)\n- **British naval and gunnery superiority;** Chinese forces outdated\n- **Treaty of Nanking (29 August 1842):**\n- **Hong Kong ceded** to Britain\n- **Five treaty ports opened:** Canton, Amoy, Foochow, Ningpo, Shanghai\n- **Indemnity** (21 million dollars)\n- **Cohong monopoly abolished**\n- **Equal official correspondence**\n- **Supplementary Treaty of the Bogue (1843):** extraterritoriality, most-favoured nation clause, fixed tariffs\n- **Similar treaties** with USA (Wanghia 1844) and France (Whampoa 1844)\n# Second Opium War (Arrow War, 1856-60)\n- **Arrow incident (1856)** and murder of a French missionary\n- **Treaty of Tientsin (1858)** and **Convention of Peking (1860):** more ports, opium legalised, Christian missions, foreign legation in Peking, Kowloon to Britain\n# To What Extent Did It Open China?\n- **Treaty ports and foreign settlements,** extraterritoriality\n- **Loss of tariff autonomy,** fixed duties\n- **Foreign missionaries** and trade penetrated inside\n- **Spheres of influence** later (1890s)\n- **Weakened Qing prestige:** Taiping Rebellion (1850-64)\n- **Century of humiliation,** rise of reform and nationalism\n- **Limit:** China was not directly colonised; the real \"opening\" was completed by later powers (Japan 1894, Boxer 1900)\n# Evaluation\n- **Not only opium:** trade, law and diplomacy were deeper causes (some historians stress \"clash of two systems\")\n- **Opium was the occasion;** the aim was open markets\n# Conclusion\n> The Opium Wars grew out of the opium trade, British demand for free trade and Chinese resistance; they broke the Canton system and began the era of unequal treaties that opened China to the Western powers.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "Evaluate the causes and nature of the Chinese Revolution of 1911.", "y": "2016, 2022", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "Describe the modernization of Japan after the decline of the Tokugawa shogunate. Discuss the rise of Japan as a world power.", "y": "2017, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Japan:** isolated under the Tokugawa shogunate (1603-1868); within 40 years of the **Meiji Restoration (1868)** became a modern industrial and military power\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2017, 2018\n# Decline of the Tokugawa\n- **Rigid feudal society,** Samurai discontent, peasant revolts\n- **Economic strain** and the rise of merchants\n- **Commodore Perry (1853):** American fleet; **Treaty of Kanagawa (1854)** opened ports\n- **Unequal treaties** (Harris Treaty, 1858) with Western powers\n- **Sonno-joi movement:** \"Revere the Emperor, expel the barbarians\"\n- **Satsuma and Choshu clans** brought down the Shogun\n# Meiji Restoration (1868)\n- **Emperor Mutsuhito (Meiji)** restored; capital shifted to Tokyo (Edo)\n- **Charter Oath (1868):** deliberative assemblies, public opinion, knowledge from the world\n# Reforms\n## Political\n- **Abolition of feudalism:** daimyo surrendered land (1869), han replaced by prefectures (1871)\n- **Constitution (1889):** modelled on Prussia (Ito Hirobumi), Emperor sovereign; **Diet (1890)**\n## Social\n- **Abolition of the samurai class privileges;** stipends commuted (1876)\n- **Equality before law,** end of class distinctions\n## Military\n- **Conscription (1873),** modern army (German model) and navy (British model)\n## Education\n- **Compulsory education (1872),** Tokyo University, foreign experts, students sent abroad\n## Economic\n- **Land tax reform (1873):** fixed cash tax, private ownership\n- **State-founded industries** (railways 1872, shipyards, mines), later sold to private firms\n- **Zaibatsu:** Mitsui, Mitsubishi\n- **Modern banking** and currency (Bank of Japan 1882)\n- **Rapid industrial growth** of textiles, shipbuilding\n## Legal\n- **Western-style codes** to end extraterritoriality\n# Rise of Japan as a World Power\n- **First Sino-Japanese War (1894-95):** defeated China; **Treaty of Shimonoseki:** Taiwan, indemnity; Triple Intervention\n- **Anglo-Japanese Alliance (1902)**\n- **Russo-Japanese War (1904-05):** Port Arthur, Mukden, **Tsushima;** **Treaty of Portsmouth (1905):** southern Sakhalin, Korea influence, South Manchuria railway\n- **Annexed Korea (1910)**\n- **First World War:** gains of German Pacific islands, Twenty-One Demands (1915)\n- **Washington Conference (1921-22):** recognised as a great power\n# Causes of Success\n- **Unity, discipline and national loyalty**\n- **Strong leadership:** Ito, Okubo, Kido, Yamagata\n- **Selective borrowing** from the West\n- **Education and literacy**\n- **No colonial rule** and economic independence\n- **Confucian values** and loyalty\n# Conclusion\n> Japan modernised through political centralisation, social equality, education, industrialisation and military strength, and by 1905 it had become the first Asian power to defeat a European one.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "Discuss the significance of the Russian Revolution of 1905.", "y": "2018", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "What do you understand by imperialism? Explain the partition of Africa by European powers.", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "What were the causes of the failure of the League of Nations?", "y": "2015", "a": "# Introduction\n- **League of Nations:** international organisation established by the **Paris Peace Conference (1919),** started 1920, headquarters at **Geneva**\n- **Founder idea:** President Wilson (Fourteen Points)\n- **Aim:** collective security, disarmament, peaceful settlement, international cooperation\n- **Failed to prevent the Second World War**\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015\n# Structure\n- **Assembly** (all members, one vote), **Council** (permanent: Britain, France, Italy, Japan; later Germany and USSR), **Secretariat**\n- **Permanent Court of International Justice**\n- **ILO** and mandate system\n# Successes\n- **Aaland Islands (1921),** Upper Silesia (1921), Greece-Bulgaria (1925)\n- **Help for refugees,** health, labour, mandates\n# Failures: Major Instances\n- **Vilna (1920),** **Corfu incident (1923):** Mussolini defied the League\n- **Manchuria (1931):** Japan's invasion; Lytton Report; Japan left (1933)\n- **Disarmament Conference (1932-34)** failed; Germany left (1933)\n- **Abyssinia (1935):** Italian invasion; weak sanctions (no oil), Hoare-Laval Pact\n- **Rhineland (1936),** Anschluss, Czechoslovakia (no League role)\n- **Spanish Civil War** (non-intervention)\n- **USSR expelled (1939)** after Finland attack\n# Causes of Failure\n## Absence of Major Powers\n- **USA never joined** (Senate rejected the Treaty of Versailles, 1919)\n- **Germany joined only in 1926** and left in 1933; **USSR 1934-39;** Japan and Italy left\n## Weak Structure\n- **Unanimity rule** in Council and Assembly\n- **No military force** of its own\n- **Sanctions (Article 16)** difficult to apply\n## Policies of Britain and France\n- **Self-interest:** reluctant to act, appeasement\n- **Rivalry between Britain and France**\n## Link with Versailles Treaty\n- **Seen as a victors' club,** defender of the status quo\n## Economic Depression\n- **Great Depression (1929)** turned states to nationalism and dictatorship\n## Rise of Dictatorships\n- **Hitler, Mussolini, Japanese militarists** defied it\n## Lack of Public Faith\n- **Weak collective security belief,** old methods of alliances returned\n# Conclusion\n> The League failed because it lacked the USA and great-power support, military force and unity, and could not control aggressive dictators; its failure led to the creation of the United Nations.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Discuss the main provisions of the Treaty of Versailles / Paris Peace Settlement.", "y": "2017, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Treaty of Versailles:** peace treaty with Germany signed on **28 June 1919** at the Hall of Mirrors, Versailles\n- **Part of the Paris Peace Settlement** (Paris Peace Conference, January 1919)\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2017, 2022 (also in the 2015 Unit V question)\n# The Paris Peace Conference\n- **Big Four:** Woodrow Wilson (USA), Lloyd George (Britain), Clemenceau (France), Orlando (Italy)\n- **Wilson's Fourteen Points (January 1918):** open diplomacy, self-determination, League of Nations, disarmament\n- **Clemenceau:** security of France, punish Germany\n- **Lloyd George:** compromise\n- **Germany was not invited** to negotiate; treaty was dictated (\"Diktat\")\n# The Five Treaties\n- **Versailles (1919):** Germany\n- **St Germain (1919):** Austria\n- **Neuilly (1919):** Bulgaria\n- **Trianon (1920):** Hungary\n- **Sevres (1920):** Turkey (replaced by Lausanne, 1923)\n# Main Provisions of the Treaty of Versailles\n## Territorial\n- **Alsace-Lorraine** returned to France\n- **Saar coal mines** to France for 15 years; Saar under the League, plebiscite after 15 years\n- **Eupen and Malmedy** to Belgium\n- **North Schleswig** to Denmark (plebiscite)\n- **Poland got West Prussia, Posen** (the Polish corridor), part of Upper Silesia\n- **Danzig** a free city under the League\n- **Memel** to Lithuania\n- **Colonies** taken and made mandates under the League\n## Military\n- **Army limited to 1,00,000 men,** conscription banned\n- **No air force, tanks, submarines,** small navy\n- **Rhineland demilitarised;** occupation by Allies for 15 years\n## Economic\n- **Reparations:** fixed in 1921 at 132 billion gold marks\n- **Loss of coal, iron resources** and merchant ships\n## War Guilt\n- **Article 231:** Germany and allies accepted responsibility for the war\n## Political\n- **League of Nations Covenant** was part of the treaty\n- **Union (Anschluss) with Austria forbidden**\n# Other Treaties: Brief Terms\n- **Austria-Hungary dissolved:** Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, Austria, Hungary, Poland created\n- **Turkey:** Arab lands as mandates\n# Criticism\n- **Dictated peace;** Germany not heard\n- **Violation of Wilson's principles:** self-determination ignored (Germans in Poland, Sudetenland, Tyrol)\n- **Harsh reparations** (Keynes, Economic Consequences of the Peace, 1919)\n- **War guilt humiliation** and national resentment\n- **Weak enforcement,** USA refused to ratify and stayed out of the League\n- **Marshal Foch:** \"This is not peace, it is an armistice for twenty years\"\n# Defence\n- **Not as harsh as Germany's Brest-Litovsk (1918)**\n- **Some self-determination** achieved\n# Consequences\n- **Rise of Hitler and Nazism** on the resentment\n- **Economic crisis** (inflation 1923)\n- **Instability in Europe,** weak new states\n- **One of the causes of the Second World War**\n# Conclusion\n> The Treaty of Versailles tried to punish Germany and keep peace, but its harsh and unequal terms created resentment, and so it helped lead to the Second World War.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Examine the causes of the Russian Revolution of 1917. How did Lenin solve the early problems of Russia?", "y": "2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Russian Revolution of 1917:** two revolutions, **February (March)** which overthrew the Tsar, and **October (November)** in which the Bolsheviks seized power\n- **Led by:** Lenin and the Bolshevik Party\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2019\n# Causes\n## Autocracy and Tsarist Failure\n- **Nicholas II:** weak, believed in autocracy; Duma (1906) weakened\n- **Tsarina Alexandra and Rasputin** ruled the court, scandal\n- **Corruption and inefficiency** in bureaucracy\n## Social and Economic Conditions\n- **Peasants:** land hunger, poverty, heavy debts\n- **Industrial workers:** long hours, low wages, bad housing (Petrograd, Moscow)\n- **Minorities** (Poles, Jews, Finns) oppressed by Russification\n## 1905 Revolution\n- **Bloody Sunday (January 1905),** soviets formed; limited reforms; October Manifesto\n## Influence of Revolutionary Ideas\n- **Marxism:** Plekhanov, Lenin; **Russian Social Democratic Labour Party** split (1903) into **Bolsheviks** and **Mensheviks**\n- **Social Revolutionaries** (peasants)\n## The First World War\n- **Heavy defeats** (Tannenberg and Masurian Lakes, 1914)\n- **About 1.7 million dead,** millions wounded or captured\n- **Shortages of food, fuel,** inflation, transport collapse\n- **Tsar took personal command (1915)** and shared the blame\n## Immediate Cause\n- **Bread riots in Petrograd (8-12 March 1917, International Women's Day)**\n- **Army refused to fire;** Tsar abdicated (15 March)\n# Course of the Revolution\n## February Revolution\n- **Provisional Government** (Prince Lvov, Kerensky) and **Petrograd Soviet** (dual power)\n- **Continued the war,** delayed land reform\n## Lenin's Return\n- **April Theses (1917):** \"Peace, Bread, Land,\" \"All power to the Soviets\"\n## Failures of the Provisional Government\n- **July Days,** **Kornilov affair (August-September)**\n- **Bolsheviks gained majorities** in Petrograd and Moscow soviets\n## October Revolution (24-25 October 1917)\n- **Military Revolutionary Committee (Trotsky),** Red Guards\n- **Winter Palace seized;** Kerensky fled\n- **Lenin headed Council of People's Commissars**\n# How Lenin Solved the Early Problems\n- **Decree on Peace;** **Treaty of Brest-Litovsk (March 1918)**\n- **Decree on Land:** land to peasants\n- **Workers' control** of factories and nationalisation of banks\n- **Constituent Assembly dispersed (January 1918)**\n- **Cheka (secret police)** against opponents\n- **Civil War (1918-21):** Red Army (Trotsky) vs Whites; foreign intervention; **War Communism**\n- **Kronstadt revolt (1921)** led to the **New Economic Policy (March 1921)**\n- **USSR formed (1922)**\n# Conclusion\n> The Revolution grew out of the Tsarist autocracy, social misery, revolutionary ideas and above all the strain of the First World War; Lenin's leadership and slogans of peace, land and bread brought the Bolsheviks to power.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Analyse the causes of the Great Economic Depression and its implications for the world.", "y": "2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Great Depression (1929-39):** the worst economic crisis of the modern world, starting in the USA\n- **Began with the Wall Street Crash (October 1929);** spread to Europe, Asia, Latin America\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2019, 2022\n# Causes\n## Economic Causes in the USA\n- **Overproduction and under-consumption:** factories made more goods than people could buy (unequal income)\n- **Stock market speculation:** margin buying, share prices rose artificially\n- **Wall Street Crash:** Black Thursday (24 October) and **Black Tuesday (29 October 1929)**\n- **Bank failures:** thousands of banks closed, savings lost, credit shrank\n- **Agricultural crisis:** falling farm prices, farm debts\n- **Hawley-Smoot Tariff (1930):** high tariff, other countries retaliated, world trade collapsed\n## International Causes\n- **War debts and reparations:** Germany paid reparations with American loans (Dawes Plan 1924, Young Plan 1929); when US loans stopped, the cycle broke\n- **Gold standard** spread the crisis\n- **Collapse of Credit-Anstalt (Austria, May 1931)**\n- **Britain left the gold standard (September 1931)**\n- **Fall in prices of raw materials** hurt colonies (India, Latin America)\n## Other Factors\n- **No international economic cooperation**\n- **Weak banking regulation**\n- **Unequal distribution of wealth**\n# Implications for the World\n## USA\n- **About 13 million unemployed** (about 25 percent), factories closed, breadlines\n- **Roosevelt's New Deal (1933):** banking reforms, public works (TVA), Social Security Act (1935), NIRA\n## Germany\n- **Mass unemployment (about 6 million),** collapse of Weimar democracy\n- **Rise of Hitler and Nazism (1933)**\n## Britain and France\n- **National Government (1931),** protection (Import Duties Act 1932)\n- **Ottawa Agreement (1932):** imperial preference\n- **France** felt it later (1931-36)\n## Japan and Italy\n- **Militarism and expansion** (Manchuria 1931)\n## Colonies\n- **Fall in agricultural prices,** unemployment (Indian peasants hit)\n## World Trade and Politics\n- **World trade fell by about two-thirds** (1929-33)\n- **Economic nationalism,** tariffs, currency blocs\n- **World Economic Conference (1933) failed**\n- **Rise of dictators,** a cause of the Second World War\n- **Keynes:** led to government spending ideas\n# Chronology of the Depression\n- **1929:** Wall Street Crash, banks fail\n- **1930:** Hawley-Smoot Tariff\n- **1931:** Credit-Anstalt collapse (Austria), German bank crisis, Britain leaves the gold standard\n- **1932:** Ottawa Agreement; unemployment peaks\n- **1933:** Roosevelt's New Deal, Hitler becomes Chancellor, London Economic Conference fails\n- **1936-39:** Recovery by rearmament\n# Failure of Classical Economics\n- **Laissez-faire and balanced budgets** failed\n- **Keynes's General Theory (1936):** government spending to create demand\n- **State intervention** became accepted\n# Impact on Asia and Latin America\n- **Fall in prices of raw materials** (jute, cotton, rubber, coffee)\n- **Latin America:** coups and nationalism\n- **India:** farm prices fell by half, debt of peasants, export of gold, political unrest\n- **Japan:** militarism and expansion\n# Conclusion\n> The Depression arose from overproduction, speculation, bank failure, protectionism and the weak international financial system; it destroyed democracy in Germany and helped bring about the Second World War.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Discuss the causes of the rise of Nazism in Germany.", "y": "2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Nazism (National Socialism):** extreme nationalist, racist and totalitarian movement of **Adolf Hitler;** the **NSDAP**\n- **Hitler became Chancellor on 30 January 1933**\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2019\n# Causes\n## Treaty of Versailles\n- **Humiliating terms:** war guilt, loss of territory, reparations, small army\n- **\"Stab in the back\" myth** (November criminals)\n## Weakness of the Weimar Republic\n- **Born in defeat,** unpopular with the army and elites\n- **Proportional representation:** many parties, unstable coalitions\n- **Article 48:** emergency powers misused\n- **Political violence** and lack of democratic tradition\n## Economic Crisis\n- **Hyperinflation (1923):** savings of the middle class destroyed\n- **Great Depression (1929):** about 6 million unemployed; collapse of confidence\n- **Nazi promise of jobs and bread**\n## Fear of Communism\n- **Spartacist revolt (1919),** strong German Communist Party (KPD)\n- **Support from business and middle class** for an anti-communist movement\n## Hitler and the Nazi Party\n- **Hitler's oratory and charisma;** Mein Kampf (1925)\n- **Beer Hall Putsch (1923) failed,** Hitler turned to legal means\n- **Propaganda (Goebbels),** radio, posters, rallies\n- **SA (stormtroopers)** and **SS**\n- **Ideology:** racial nationalism, anti-Semitism, Lebensraum, anti-Versailles\n- **Promised something to every class:** farmers, workers, middle class, youth\n## Support Base\n- **Middle class,** small farmers, ex-soldiers, students, unemployed youth\n- **Big industrialists** (Thyssen, Krupp) funded\n## Electoral Rise\n- **12 seats (1928),** **107 (September 1930),** **230 (July 1932)** (about 37 percent)\n## Elite Politics\n- **Intrigues of Papen, Schleicher,** Hindenburg's decision to appoint Hitler\n# Consolidation of Power\n- **Reichstag Fire (27 February 1933)**\n- **Enabling Act (23 March 1933)**\n- **Banned other parties;** Hitler became Fuhrer (August 1934)\n# Historians' Views\n- **Structural causes** (Depression, weak republic) vs **role of personalities**\n- **Hitler as a \"product\" of Versailles and Depression**\n# Conclusion\n> Nazism rose because of the Versailles humiliation, the weak Weimar Republic, the Depression, fear of communism and Hitler's propaganda, aided by the elite's miscalculation in handing him power.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Review the course of the Communist movement in China (1934-49) and the failure of the Kuomintang.", "y": "2015, 2019", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "How far is it true that the Treaty of Versailles sowed the seeds of the Second World War?", "y": "2015", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Identify the causes of the Second World War and Hitler role in it. Explain its main stages.", "y": "2016, 2018, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Second World War (1939-45):** global war between the **Allies** (Britain, France, USSR, USA, China) and **Axis** (Germany, Italy, Japan)\n- **Began:** 1 September 1939 with Germany's attack on Poland\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016, 2018, 2019\n# Long-term Causes\n## Treaty of Versailles\n- **Harsh terms,** war-guilt clause, reparations, loss of land\n- **Germany's desire for revenge**\n## Failure of the League of Nations\n- **Weak against aggression;** USA absent; no force\n- **Disarmament conference (1932-34)** failed\n## Rise of Dictatorships and Extreme Nationalism\n- **Mussolini (Fascism, 1922),** **Hitler (Nazism, 1933),** Japanese militarism (Tojo)\n- **Hitler's racial ideas and Lebensraum** (Mein Kampf)\n## Great Depression (1929)\n- **Unemployment and unrest,** helped the rise of Nazis\n- **Economic nationalism,** protection, trade war\n## Dissatisfaction of Italy and Japan\n- **Italy:** resented small gains in 1919\n- **Japan:** needed raw materials and markets\n## Alliance Systems\n- **Rome-Berlin Axis (1936),** **Anti-Comintern Pact (1936),** **Pact of Steel (May 1939),** **Tripartite Pact (1940)**\n# Immediate Causes\n## Aggression and Appeasement\n- **Japan invaded Manchuria (1931)** and China (1937)\n- **Italy attacked Ethiopia (1935)**\n- **Hitler's actions:** rearmament (1935), **Rhineland (1936)**, Spanish Civil War support (1936-39), **Anschluss with Austria (March 1938)**\n- **Munich Agreement (September 1938):** Sudetenland handed over (Chamberlain's appeasement)\n- **Occupation of Czechoslovakia (March 1939)**\n- **Nazi-Soviet Pact (23 August 1939)**\n- **Invasion of Poland (1 September 1939);** Britain and France declared war on 3 September\n# Role of Hitler\n- **Aim:** destroy Versailles, unite Germans, create living space in the East\n- **Used diplomacy and bluff,** exploited the Allies' weakness\n- **Rearmed Germany,** broke treaties\n- **Controlled foreign policy** and made the Pact with Stalin\n- **A. J. P. Taylor:** Hitler used opportunities; **other historians:** planned war\n# Main Stages of the War\n- **1939-41:** Blitzkrieg in Poland, Denmark, Norway, **fall of France (June 1940),** Battle of Britain\n- **1941:** **Operation Barbarossa** (June, attack on USSR), **Pearl Harbor (7 December 1941)**\n- **1942-43:** El Alamein, **Stalingrad,** turning point\n- **1944:** **D-Day (6 June 1944)**\n- **1945:** Germany surrendered (8 May), **atom bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki (August)**, Japan surrendered (2 September)\n# Other Factors\n- **Policy of appeasement** by Britain and France\n- **USA isolationism**\n- **Weak collective security**\n# Conclusion\n> The Second World War was caused by the resentment of the Versailles treaty, the failure of the League, the rise of aggressive dictators, the Depression and the policy of appeasement, with Hitler's aggression as the immediate trigger.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Discuss the circumstances leading to the establishment of the United Nations and its main functions.", "y": "2018, 2019, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **United Nations (UN):** international organisation formed after the Second World War to maintain peace and security\n- **Established:** 24 October 1945 (Charter signed at San Francisco on 26 June 1945)\n- **Headquarters:** New York\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2018, 2019, 2023\n# Circumstances Leading to Its Establishment\n## Failure of the League of Nations\n- **Could not stop aggression:** Manchuria (1931), Ethiopia (1935), Rhineland, Austria, Czechoslovakia\n- **No USA** membership and no armed force\n- **Unanimity rule** made it weak\n- **Second World War** showed the need for a stronger body\n## Wartime Declarations\n- **Atlantic Charter (August 1941):** Roosevelt and Churchill's principles\n- **Declaration by United Nations (1 January 1942):** 26 nations signed\n- **Moscow Conference (1943)** and **Tehran Conference (1943)** supported a world body\n## Planning Conferences\n- **Dumbarton Oaks (August-October 1944):** draft of the structure\n- **Yalta (February 1945):** voting formula (veto) in the Security Council\n- **San Francisco Conference (25 April - 26 June 1945):** 50 nations, Charter signed\n## Fear of Another War\n- **Atomic bomb** and destruction of the war\n- **Hope for collective security**\n# Structure of the UN\n- **General Assembly:** all members, one vote each\n- **Security Council:** 5 permanent (USA, USSR, Britain, France, China) with veto, plus non-permanent members\n- **Economic and Social Council (ECOSOC)**\n- **Trusteeship Council**\n- **International Court of Justice (The Hague)**\n- **Secretariat:** Secretary-General (Trygve Lie of Norway, first)\n# Aims (Purposes)\n- **Maintain international peace and security**\n- **Develop friendly relations** among nations\n- **International cooperation** in economic, social and humanitarian fields\n- **Promote human rights**\n# Initial Work (1945-1950)\n- **Iran question (1946)** and Greek frontier\n- **Indonesia question** (1947-49): support to independence\n- **Palestine:** partition plan (1947), Israel (1948), mediator Count Bernadotte\n- **Kashmir:** UN Commission, ceasefire (January 1949)\n- **Korean War (1950):** UN forces, Security Council (Soviet boycott), Uniting for Peace (1950)\n- **Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948)**\n- **Specialised agencies:** UNESCO, WHO, FAO, ILO, World Bank, IMF\n- **Berlin blockade** and arms control (UN Atomic Energy Commission)\n# Evaluation\n- **Successes:** avoided world war, decolonisation, humanitarian work\n- **Weaknesses:** veto power, Cold War rivalry, no standing army\n# Conclusion\n> The UN was created on the lessons of the League's failure and the Second World War; in its early years it handled many disputes and set up important agencies, though the Cold War limited it.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Analyse the factors that led to the rise and growth of the Cold War up to 1950.", "y": "2015, 2017, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Cold War:** the post-war tension and rivalry between the **USA** and **USSR** (and their blocs), without direct war between them\n- **Period:** 1945 to 1991\n- **Weapons:** propaganda, alliances, arms race, economic aid, proxy wars\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2017, 2022\n# Causes and Factors\n## Ideological Conflict\n- **Capitalism and democracy (USA)** against **communism (USSR)**\n- **Mutual distrust** since 1917 Russian Revolution; Allied intervention in Russia (1918-20)\n- **Stalin's fear** of capitalist encirclement; West's fear of communism\n## Wartime Differences\n- **Delay of the Second Front** (opened only in 1944) angered Stalin\n- **USA's secret atom bomb** project\n- **Yalta (February 1945)** and **Potsdam (July 1945)** conferences: disputes on Poland, Germany, reparations\n## Soviet Expansion in Eastern Europe\n- **Red Army occupied** Eastern Europe; communist governments in Poland, Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary, Czechoslovakia (1948)\n- **Churchill's \"Iron Curtain\" speech** at Fulton (March 1946)\n## Power Vacuum and Rivalry\n- **Britain and France weakened;** USA and USSR superpowers\n- **Atomic power** gave the USA confidence\n## Crises in Iran, Greece and Turkey\n- **Soviet troops in Iran (1946)**\n- **Greek civil war,** Turkish Straits demand\n# Growth of the Cold War (1945-1950)\n- **Containment policy** (George Kennan's Long Telegram and X article)\n- **Truman Doctrine (March 1947):** US aid to Greece and Turkey\n- **Marshall Plan (June 1947):** economic recovery of Europe; USSR refused and set up Cominform (1947) and Comecon (1949)\n- **Division of Germany:** Bizonia/Trizonia, new currency\n- **Berlin Blockade (June 1948 - May 1949)** and the **airlift**\n- **NATO (April 1949)** formed\n- **Federal Republic of Germany** (West, 1949) and **GDR** (East, 1949)\n- **Soviet atom bomb (1949)**\n- **Chinese Revolution (1949)** - communist victory\n- **Korean War (1950)**\n# Responsibility\n- **Traditional view:** Soviet expansion\n- **Revisionist view:** American economic aims\n- **Post-revisionist:** mutual misperception\n# Consequences\n- **Divided world** into two blocs\n- **Arms race and nuclear threat**\n- **Proxy wars** (Korea, Vietnam)\n- **Rise of Non-Aligned Movement** (1961)\n- **United Nations** hampered by veto\n# Eastern Europe and Germany (1945-49)\n- **Soviet-dominated governments** in Poland, Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary, Albania; **Czechoslovakia coup (February 1948)**\n- **Tito (Yugoslavia) broke with Stalin (1948)**\n- **Germany divided into four zones;** Bizonia (1947), Trizonia (1948), **currency reform** triggered the Berlin Blockade\n# Ideological and Atomic Dimension\n- **Zhdanov's \"two camps\" doctrine (1947)**\n- **Baruch Plan (1946)** for atomic control failed\n- **Soviet atomic test (August 1949),** the arms race\n- **NSC-68 (1950)** called for a big US arms build-up\n# Asia in the Early Cold War\n- **Chinese Communist victory (1949)**\n- **Korea divided at the 38th parallel;** Korean War (1950)\n- **Vietnam: First Indochina War (1946-54)**\n- **Japan** under US occupation, a US ally\n# Conclusion\n> The Cold War grew out of ideological rivalry, wartime mistrust, Soviet expansion in Eastern Europe and American containment, and by 1950 it had divided the world into two hostile blocs.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P1 - Modern World (1789-1950)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Examine the consequences of decolonisation after the Second World War.", "y": "2017", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "What do you understand by the Permanent Settlement of Land? Discuss its merits and demerits.", "y": "2015, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Permanent Settlement (Zamindari Settlement):** a land revenue system introduced in **Bengal, Bihar and Orissa** by **Lord Cornwallis** in **1793** (Bengal Regulation I of 1793)\n- **Main idea:** the revenue demand of the government was fixed **for ever**; zamindars became owners of land\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2019\n# Background\n- **Diwani (1765):** Company became revenue collector of Bengal\n- **Dual system (1765-72)** and its failure; the 1769-70 famine\n- **Warren Hastings' experiments:** Five-year settlement (1772), annual settlement (1777), farming system\n- **Heavy fall in revenue,** peasant misery, corruption\n- **Pitt's India Act 1784** ordered a permanent and fair settlement\n- **Philip Francis** (pro-zamindars), **John Shore** (careful study), **Cornwallis** (decision)\n- **Court of Directors and Henry Dundas** approved\n# Features of the Settlement\n- **Zamindars recognised as owners of land** (heritable and transferable)\n- **Revenue fixed permanently:** about Rs 2.68 crore (Rs 26.8 million) for Bengal\n- **10/11 of the rent** collected went to the Company and **1/11 to the zamindar**\n- **Sunset Law:** if revenue was not paid by sunset of the due date, the estate was auctioned\n- **Zamindars to issue pattas** to tenants and fix rent\n- **Tenants had no rights** of ownership; they were at the mercy of zamindars\n- **Zamindars lost police and judicial powers;** Company courts took over\n- **Collectors** limited to supervision of revenue\n# Extension of the System\n- **Banaras division (1795)** and parts of **Northern Madras (1802)**\n- **Not extended to** North-Western Provinces, Punjab, Bombay and most of Madras (Ryotwari and Mahalwari used)\n- **Reason:** the experience of its defects and high demand\n# Objectives and Causes\n- **Stable income** for the Company\n- **To create a loyal class** (political support in India)\n- **To increase agricultural productivity** (zamindars to invest)\n- **Reduce administrative costs**\n- **Separate revenue from justice,** reduce corruption\n- **Influence of British landed society** and physiocratic ideas\n# Merits\n## For the Company\n- **Fixed and regular revenue,** budgeting easy\n- **Low cost of administration**\n- **Loyal class** of zamindars (as in 1857)\n- **Revenue officers** freed for other work\n## For Zamindars\n- **Security of property,** incentive to improve land\n- **Growth of wealthy gentry**\n## For Agriculture\n- **Some land reclamation,** more cultivation (in theory)\n# Demerits\n## For Peasants\n- **Heavy rent, rack-renting,** enhancement of rent and illegal cesses (abwabs)\n- **No security of tenure,** eviction, bonded labour\n- **Rural indebtedness**\n## For the State\n- **Loss of revenue** as prices and cultivation grew (government could not enhance its share)\n- **Burden of famine relief,** no share in rising land values\n## For Zamindars\n- **Sunset law** ruined many old zamindars; estates auctioned (e.g. Burdwan, Nadia)\n- **New class of merchants and moneylenders** (Calcutta banians) bought estates\n## Social and Economic Effects\n- **Subinfeudation:** chain of intermediaries (patnidars, darpatnidars)\n- **Absentee landlordism** (living in Calcutta)\n- **Capital invested in land,** not in industry or trade\n- **Little agricultural improvement**\n- **Decline of village communities**\n## Legal\n- **Rent Act 1859, Bengal Tenancy Act 1885** later tried to protect tenants\n# Historians' Views\n- **Ranajit Guha,** A Rule of Property for Bengal (1963): influence of physiocrats and Whig ideas\n- **Eric Stokes** and **Irfan Habib:** burden on peasants\n- **Nationalist view:** exploitation; **imperialist view:** a stable system\n# Conclusion\n> The Permanent Settlement gave the Company fixed revenue and a loyal class, but it ruined peasants, created absentee landlords and parasitic intermediaries, and retarded agricultural progress, so it is seen mostly as a harmful experiment.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Examine the chief features of the Ryotwari settlement. What was the condition of cultivators under it?", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Ryotwari settlement:** land revenue system in which the settlement was made **directly with the ryot (cultivator)**\n- **Introduced in:** Madras (Thomas Munro) and Bombay (Elphinstone); later Assam, Berar\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016\n# Origin\n- **Experiment of Alexander Read in Baramahal (1792-99)** with Thomas Munro\n- **Munro as Governor of Madras (1820-27)** extended it\n- **Reasons:** no strong zamindars in the south; Cornwallis' system found costly and unsuitable\n- **Bombay:** Elphinstone (1819-27); **Pringle survey (1827-28);** **Wingate and Goldsmid (1836)** survey settlement\n# Features\n- **Direct settlement with the cultivator,** who was recognised as owner\n- **Revenue assessed on each field** (after survey and classification of soil)\n- **State share:** about 45-55 percent of net produce (Madras 50 percent; later reduced)\n- **Revision after 20-30 years**\n- **Cash payment**\n- **Cultivator could sublet, sell or mortgage;** had to pay on time or lose land\n- **Could relinquish** any part of land\n- **Government retained the ownership** in the sense of right to revenue\n# Merits\n- **No intermediaries,** direct tie with the peasant\n- **Security of ownership** for the cultivator\n- **Flexible:** remission possible, revision of assessment\n- **Government's income grew** with cultivation\n# Demerits\n- **Very high assessment** (almost half of produce) in early years, left little for the ryot\n- **Rigid collection** in bad harvests, no remission\n- **Corruption of petty officials**\n- **Moneylenders'** control, indebtedness\n- **Land alienation** and sale for arrears\n- **Deccan Riots (1875)** in Bombay, **Deccan Agriculturists Relief Act (1879)**\n- **Frequent surveys** and revisions increased uncertainty\n- **Condition of cultivators** remained poor; famines (1876-78)\n# Comparison with Other Systems\n- **Permanent Settlement:** with zamindars, fixed forever\n- **Mahalwari:** with village communities, periodic revision\n- **Ryotwari:** with the individual ryot, periodic revision\n# Historians\n- **Eric Stokes, Burton Stein, Irfan Habib:** burden on peasants\n- **Munro's idea of paternal government**\n# Conclusion\n> The ryotwari system recognised the peasant's ownership and removed zamindars, but its heavy assessment and rigid collection led to indebtedness and misery for cultivators.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Examine the characteristics of the Mahalwari system of land revenue. What were its merits and demerits?", "y": "2017, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Mahalwari System:** land revenue system in which the settlement was made with the **village (mahal)** or group of villages as a whole\n- **Introduced in:** North-Western Provinces (Agra and Oudh), parts of Punjab, Central Provinces\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2017, 2019\n# Background\n- **Permanent Settlement** was found costly (fixed revenue); **Ryotwari** was in Madras and Bombay\n- **Holt Mackenzie (1819)** suggested the village-based system for the North-West\n- **Regulation VII of 1822:** legal base\n- **Regulation IX of 1833 (Martin Bird):** a big step; \"father of the Mahalwari system\" in the North-West\n- **Saharanpur Rules (1855)** fixed the state share at 50 percent of net assets\n# Main Features\n- **Unit of settlement:** the village (mahal), the estate\n- **Joint responsibility:** the village community, headed by lambardars, was collectively responsible\n- **Revenue fixed for a period** (20-30 years), not permanent\n- **Revenue demand:** originally 80 percent (1822), reduced to 66 percent (1833), then 50 percent (1855)\n- **Detailed survey,** measurement and record of rights\n- **Assessment on estimated net produce**\n- **Landlords or village brotherhoods (zamindars)** were recognised as owners\n- **Lambardars** (representatives) collected revenue\n- **Individual cultivator's rights** recorded in the settlement\n- **Land could be sold or mortgaged** (transfer rights)\n# Aims\n- **Higher income** for government than Permanent Settlement\n- **Protect small proprietors,** village communities\n- **Revenue revision** to meet price rise\n# Merits\n- **Revenue could be revised,** gain for the government as prices rose\n- **Village community** retained\n- **Less exploitation** than Permanent Settlement; protection to peasants and tenants (in theory)\n- **Records of rights** and survey\n- **Reduced the power of zamindars** and middlemen\n- **More scientific method** of assessment\n# Demerits\n- **High revenue demand** at first (80 percent) ruined many villages\n- **Joint responsibility** was unjust: the honest paid for defaulters\n- **Rigid collection** in bad years, no remission\n- **Peasant indebtedness,** moneylender dominance\n- **Sale of land** by auction; **land alienation** to moneylenders\n- **Frequent revision** created uncertainty\n- **Corruption of revenue officers** and complicated rules\n- **Break-up of the village community**\n# Comparison\n- **Permanent Settlement:** fixed forever, zamindar owner, Bengal\n- **Ryotwari:** direct with cultivator (Munro, Madras and Bombay)\n- **Mahalwari:** with village community, periodic revision\n# Effects\n- **Revenue of the state grew,** but the rural economy suffered\n- **Land rights became marketable,** rise of the moneylender class\n# Conclusion\n> The Mahalwari system brought regular revision and recorded rights, but its early heavy demand and joint liability burdened the peasants.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Discuss the characteristics of Indian village communities in the mid-eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries.", "y": "2016", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Examine the characteristics of the traditional system of education in India in the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries.", "y": "2015, 2019", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Discuss the British educational policy in India from 1813 to 1860. What was Wood Despatch?", "y": "2019, 2025", "a": "# Introduction\n- **British educational policy (1813-60):** the Company's policy on education passed from Orientalism to Anglicism\n- **Wood's Despatch (1854):** the \"Magna Carta of English education in India\"\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2019, 2025\n# Early Efforts\n- **Calcutta Madrasa (1781)** (Warren Hastings), **Benares Sanskrit College (1791)** (Jonathan Duncan)\n- **Fort William College (1800)** (Wellesley)\n- **Christian missionaries:** Serampore (William Carey), schools and Bible\n# Charter Act of 1813\n- **Rs 1 lakh yearly** set aside for the education of Indians\n- **Orientalist vs Anglicist controversy** began\n# Orientalists vs Anglicists\n- **Orientalists (H. H. Wilson, Prinsep):** promote Sanskrit, Arabic, Persian\n- **Anglicists (Macaulay, Trevelyan, Bentinck):** Western knowledge in English\n- **General Committee of Public Instruction (1823)**\n# Macaulay's Minute (2 February 1835)\n- **\"A single shelf of a good European library is worth the whole native literature\"**\n- **English as medium** to create \"a class of persons Indian in blood and colour, English in taste, opinion, morals and intellect\"\n- **Bentinck's Resolution (7 March 1835):** funds for English education\n# Further Steps\n- **Adam's Reports (1835-38):** on vernacular education in Bengal and Bihar\n- **Lord Auckland's Minute (1839):** filtration theory (education trickles down)\n- **Thomason's scheme (NWP, 1843),** vernacular schools\n- **Hardinge's Resolution (1844):** preference in jobs to English educated\n- **English replaced Persian** as the official language (1837)\n# Wood's Despatch (1854)\n- **Sir Charles Wood,** President of the Board of Control\n- **Features:**\n- **Department of Public Instruction** in each province\n- **Universities** at Calcutta, Bombay and Madras (1857) on the London model\n- **Graded system:** primary (vernacular), middle (Anglo-vernacular), high school, college\n- **Grants-in-aid** for private schools\n- **Teacher training schools**\n- **Women's education** and vocational education encouraged\n- **Education for the masses,** vernaculars with English\n- **Muslim education** encouraged\n# Evaluation\n## Positive\n- **Modern education,** science and liberal ideas\n- **Rise of the middle class** and nationalist leaders\n- **Reform movements,** press\n## Negative\n- **Neglected mass education,** female education, technical education\n- **Elitist, filtration theory failed**\n- **Created clerks** for the British administration\n- **Divided society** English-educated and rest\n- **Destruction of indigenous schools** (pathshalas, maktabs)\n# Conclusion\n> British educational policy first supported Oriental learning, then chose Western education in English; Wood's Despatch laid the framework of modern education, but it served colonial interests and neglected the masses.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "What were the main trends in British social policy in India up to 1947?", "y": "2017, 2025", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Examine the conditions in which the traditional handicrafts declined. Discuss the causes and consequences of de-industrialisation.", "y": "2015, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Decline of Indian handicrafts and de-industrialisation:** fall of traditional industries (textiles, metal, shipbuilding) under British rule, and shifting of workers to agriculture\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2019\n# India's Handicrafts Before British Rule\n- **Famous Dacca muslin,** Murshidabad silk, Bengal cotton, Banaras brocade, Kashmir shawls, Surat, Masulipatnam\n- **Export to Europe** and Asia, rich artisan class\n- **Patronage** of courts and nobility\n# Causes of Decline\n## British Commercial Policy\n- **Company's monopoly,** low prices fixed for weavers, forced contracts (dadni), harassment\n- **Heavy duties on Indian cloth** in Britain (70-80 percent), ban on some goods\n- **Free entry for British goods** into India (Charter Act 1813, abolition of Company monopoly 1833), low duties\n## Industrial Revolution\n- **Cheap machine-made British goods** (Lancashire) flooded the Indian market\n- **Indian cloth could not compete** in price\n## Loss of Patronage\n- **Decline of princely courts and nobility** after conquest\n- **Fall of old urban centres** (Dacca, Murshidabad, Surat)\n## Transport and Colonial Economy\n- **Railways (1853)** carried British goods to the interior\n- **India made a supplier of raw material** (cotton, indigo, jute) and a market for goods\n## Administrative Policies\n- **Revenue system:** heavy land revenue forced artisans to farming\n- **Taxes and tolls** on artisan goods\n# Consequences of De-industrialisation\n- **Unemployment** of artisans, impoverishment\n- **Ruralisation:** workers moved to agriculture, more pressure on land (share of workers in agriculture rose)\n- **Decline of towns,** population fall (Dacca fell sharply from the early 1800s)\n- **Colonial dependence:** raw material exporter, importer of manufactures\n- **Drain of wealth** and poverty\n- **Destruction of village self-sufficiency**\n- **Rise of new classes** and later modern industry (cotton mills 1854, jute mills 1855)\n# Historians' Views\n- **Nationalists** (Naoroji, R. C. Dutt, Romesh): ruin of industries\n- **Morris D. Morris and others:** argue that decline was limited, that demand fell due to other reasons\n- **Bipan Chandra, Irfan Habib:** de-industrialisation was real and policy-driven\n# Evaluation\n- **Not all crafts declined;** some (luxury goods, local crafts) survived\n- **New modern industry** grew late and under foreign control\n# Conclusion\n> Under British policy, handicrafts declined because of unequal trade rules, machine competition, loss of patronage and the colonial economy; this impoverished artisans and made India more agricultural and dependent.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Write about the development of railways in India. What was the role of foreign capital?", "y": "2017, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Railways:** the most important technological change under British rule\n- **First line:** Bombay-Thane, **16 April 1853** (about 34 km)\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2017, 2019\n# Background and Motives\n- **Lord Dalhousie's Railway Minute (1853):** plan of a trunk network linking Bombay, Calcutta, Madras\n- **Commercial motive:** bring cotton and raw materials from the interior to ports\n- **Market for British goods** (steel, locomotives)\n- **Military and strategic motive:** quick movement of troops, after the Revolt of 1857\n- **Political and administrative control**\n- **Investment** for British capital\n# Stages of Growth\n- **1853-69: Guarantee system**\n- **Private companies (EIR, GIPR, Madras Railway)** raised capital in London\n- **Government gave a guaranteed 5 percent interest,** free land, and the right to buy after 25-50 years\n- **Losses fell on Indian taxpayers**\n- **1869-1900: State-owned lines** (Lytton), then mixed\n- **1880s onward:** guaranteed companies again, famine railways\n- **Mileage:** about 288 miles (1857), over 25,000 miles (1900), about 41,000 miles (1947)\n- **1905 Railway Board;** **1924 separation of the railway budget**\n# Role of Foreign Capital\n- **Capital came from British investors,** raised in London\n- **Government guarantee** removed risk; no incentive to economise\n- **Materials imported from Britain:** rails, engines, coaches (not made in India)\n- **Higher staff were British,** Indians in lower ranks\n- **Interest payments** became part of the **drain of wealth** (home charges)\n- **Capital cost very high** per mile, wasteful\n# Effects: Positive\n- **Integration of national market,** growth of internal trade\n- **Boosted commercialisation of agriculture** (cotton, jute, wheat)\n- **Famine relief,** movement of grain\n- **Created employment,** growth of coal, steel (Jamshedpur) and engineering\n- **Spread of ideas:** nationalism, mobility, mixing of people\n- **Port cities and towns** grew\n# Effects: Negative\n- **Benefited British industry** more than India (steel, coal, machine exports)\n- **Freight rates favoured imports and exports** over internal trade\n- **Heavy drain** through interest and profit\n- **Indian industry neglected;** Britain's iron and steel gained\n- **Strategic lines,** not always economic\n- **Racial discrimination** in travel and jobs\n- **Famine** continued despite railways\n# Views of Historians\n- **Marx:** railways will become the \"forerunner of modern industry\"\n- **Nationalists (Naoroji, R. C. Dutt, Digby):** railways served British interests, a drain\n- **Imperialist view:** modernised India\n# Conclusion\n> Railways connected India and aided trade and administration, but they were built with British capital and for British ends, and so the gains for India were limited and the cost was heavy.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Highlight the main trends in the tariff policy in India (1858-96) and its objectives.", "y": "2015", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "What were the causes of stagnant economic growth during the British period?", "y": "2017", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Examine the main features of tenancy legislation between 1860 and 1914.", "y": "2016", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Give a detailed account of peasant and tribal revolts in India between 1860 and 1919.", "y": "2025", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "What were the factors that contributed to the rise of social and religious reform movements in the nineteenth century?", "y": "2015, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Social and religious reform movements of the 19th century:** efforts to remove evils in Indian society and reform religion in the light of reason and modern ideas\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2019\n# Factors Responsible\n## British Rule and Western Education\n- **English education** introduced liberal ideas (liberty, equality, reason)\n- **Macaulay's Minute (1835),** universities (1857)\n- **A new English-educated middle class**\n## Contact with the West\n- **Enlightenment and rationalism,** science\n- **Western writers:** Mill, Rousseau, Voltaire, Bentham\n- **Orientalists** (William Jones, Max Muller, Asiatic Society 1784) revived pride in the past\n## Press and Communications\n- **Newspapers and printing:** Sambad Kaumudi, Kesari\n- **Railways, postal system** spread ideas\n## Challenge of Christian Missionaries\n- **Missionary criticism** of Hindu practices made Indians examine their faith\n## Social Evils\n- **Sati, female infanticide, child marriage, widow condition, caste discrimination, purdah, polygamy, idol worship, superstition**\n## Legal and Administrative Measures\n- **Rule of law and equality before law**\n- **Support from enlightened officials** (Bentinck)\n## Economic Changes\n- **Urbanisation, new professions,** middle class\n## Revolt of 1857 and Nationalism\n- **Desire for self-strengthening** and unity\n## Local Social Conditions\n- **Decline of old institutions,** need for change\n# Major Movements\n- **Brahmo Samaj (1828)** (Raja Rammohan Roy)\n- **Prarthana Samaj (1867)** (Ranade, Atmaram Pandurang)\n- **Arya Samaj (1875)** (Dayanand Saraswati)\n- **Ramakrishna Mission (1897)** (Vivekananda)\n- **Theosophical Society (1882, Adyar)** (Annie Besant)\n- **Aligarh Movement (1875)** (Sir Syed), **Deoband (1866)**\n- **Singh Sabha (1873)**\n- **Parsi reform:** Rahnumai Mazdayasnan Sabha (1851)\n- **Satyashodhak Samaj (1873)** (Phule), **SNDP (1903)** (Narayana Guru)\n# Reforms and Laws\n- **Abolition of sati (1829)**\n- **Hindu Widow Remarriage Act (1856)**\n- **Native Marriage Act (1872)**\n- **Age of Consent Act (1891),** **Sarda Act (1929)**\n# Nature and Limits\n- **Mostly urban and upper caste,** limited to educated elite\n- **Revivalist and reformist strands**\n- **Led to religious communal divisions** in some cases\n# Importance\n- **Social modernisation,** status of women\n- **Base for nationalism**\n# Common Features of the Reform Movements\n- **Use of reason and science** to examine tradition\n- **Opposition to idol worship, rituals, caste,** superstition\n- **Stress on women's education and rights**\n- **Use of English education** and the press\n- **Two approaches:** reformist (Brahmo, Prarthana) and revivalist (Arya Samaj, early Ramakrishna)\n- **Organisation:** Samajs, societies, schools, journals\n# Contribution to Women's Cause\n- **Rammohan Roy:** campaigned against sati (1829 law)\n- **Vidyasagar:** widow remarriage (1856), girls' schools\n- **Dayanand:** women's education, Vedic equality\n- **Phule:** first girls' school at Pune (1848), Savitribai Phule\n- **Parsi reformers (Malabari):** Age of Consent\n# Limitations\n- **Confined to the educated middle class** and urban areas\n- **Ignored peasants and lower castes** at first\n- **Revivalism** led to communal tension (Shuddhi, Tabligh)\n- **Social laws** were not strongly enforced\n# Muslim and Sikh Reform\n- **Aligarh (Sir Syed):** modern education and rational Islam\n- **Deoband:** religious purity\n- **Singh Sabha:** Sikh revival and education\n# Conclusion\n> English education, Western ideas, social evils, the press and missionary challenge together created the reform movements, which modernised Indian society and prepared the ground for the national movement.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "Why was the Brahmo Samaj formed? What was its contribution and why did it fail?", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Brahmo Samaj:** reformist religious and social movement founded in Calcutta by **Raja Rammohan Roy** on **20 August 1828** (as Brahmo Sabha)\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016\n# Causes of Formation\n## Religious and Social Conditions\n- **Idol worship,** rituals, polytheism, superstition\n- **Sati, child marriage, polygamy,** caste rigidity, ban on widow remarriage\n- **Position of women** poor\n## Influence of Western Ideas\n- **Rationalism, Unitarian Christianity,** liberalism\n- **English education,** Hindu College (1817)\n## Rammohan Roy's Earlier Work\n- **Atmiya Sabha (1815),** Tuhfat-ul-Muwahhidin (Persian), translations of the Upanishads\n- **Sambad Kaumudi (1821)**\n- **Need for an organisation** to promote monotheism\n# Principles of the Brahmo Samaj\n- **Worship of one formless God** (monotheism)\n- **Opposition to idol worship and sacrifice**\n- **Faith in the Vedanta and reason**\n- **Brotherhood of man,** against caste and untouchability\n- **Respect for all religions**\n- **Social reform** was a part of religion\n# Contributions\n## Religious\n- **Revival of Upanishadic monotheism**\n- **Prayer meetings** without idols\n## Social\n- **Campaign against sati:** abolished by Bentinck (1829)\n- **Against child marriage and polygamy;** for widow remarriage and women's education\n- **Opposed caste and untouchability**\n## Educational\n- **Support for English and scientific education;** Vedanta College (1825)\n## Press and Political\n- **Sambad Kaumudi,** freedom of press (Memorial 1823)\n- **Demand for judicial reforms,** separation of powers\n- **Planted the seeds of nationalism** and later leaders\n## Legal\n- **Native Marriage (Brahmo Marriage) Act (1872)**\n## Later Leaders\n- **Debendranath Tagore (revived, 1843; Tattvabodhini Sabha)**\n- **Keshab Chandra Sen:** Brahmo Samaj of India (1866), social reform (Indian Mirror)\n- **Rabindranath Tagore** later influence\n# Splits\n- **1866:** Adi Brahmo Samaj (Debendranath) and Brahmo Samaj of India (Keshab)\n- **1878:** Keshab's daughter's under-age marriage to the Maharaja of Cooch Behar caused **Sadharan Brahmo Samaj**\n# Why It Failed to Become a Mass Movement\n- **Urban elite and English educated base,** no peasants\n- **Hostility of orthodox Hindus (Dharma Sabha, Radhakant Deb)**\n- **Perceived as Westernised and anti-Hindu**\n- **Splits and personal quarrels**\n- **Keshab's mysticism** (New Dispensation)\n- **Rise of Arya Samaj** and Ramakrishna movement\n- **Lack of organisational strength** and mass appeal\n# Evaluation\n- **Pioneer of modern reform in India,** \"Father of Modern India\" (Rammohan)\n# Conclusion\n> The Brahmo Samaj was formed to reform Hindu religion and society in the light of reason; it gave India modern ideas and reforms, but remained an elite movement and declined after splits.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "Give a critical account of social legislation in India (1860-1939).", "y": "2016, 2025", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "What is meant by Sanskritization? What were the factors responsible for it?", "y": "2017, 2019, 2025", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Sanskritization:** concept given by **M. N. Srinivas** in **Religion and Society among the Coorgs of South India (1952)**; developed in Social Change in Modern India (1966)\n- **Meaning:** a process by which a **low caste or tribal group** changes its customs, rituals, beliefs and way of life in the direction of a **higher (twice-born) caste**, in the hope of a higher status\n- **Related concepts:** Westernisation, Secularisation, Dominant Caste\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2017, 2019, 2025\n# Features of Sanskritization\n- **Imitation of upper castes,** especially Brahmins, sometimes Kshatriyas and Vaishyas\n- **Positional change,** not structural change (individual castes rise, the hierarchy stays)\n- **Takes one or two generations** to be accepted\n- **Reference group** may be local dominant caste, not always a Brahmin\n- **Leads to claim** of higher varna status (as in Census returns)\n- **Not only Sanskrit:** includes local customs of the dominant caste\n# Forms of Sanskritization\n- **Adoption of vegetarianism** and teetotalism\n- **Giving up** animal sacrifice, meat and liquor\n- **Wearing sacred thread (janeu)**\n- **Worship of Sanskritic gods,** pilgrimage, Sanskrit rites\n- **Changing names and surnames** (Singh, Sharma, Varma)\n- **Imitating marriage and funeral rituals** (dowry, child marriage, ban on widow remarriage)\n- **Women's seclusion (purdah)**\n- **Endogamy** and strict caste purity\n# Examples\n- **Coorgs** (Srinivas's study)\n- **Ahirs/Yadavs, Kurmis, Koeris** claiming Kshatriya status\n- **Nadars (Shanars) of Tamil Nadu**\n- **Ezhavas of Kerala** (influenced by Sree Narayana Guru)\n- **Tribal groups** such as Bhils, Gonds, Oraons, Santhals\n- **Lingayat** and other movements\n# Factors Responsible for Sanskritization\n## Social Factors\n- **Desire for higher status** and social mobility\n- **Caste associations and sabhas** (caste federations)\n- **Influence of Brahmins** and priests as models\n## Political and Legal Factors\n- **British rule:** equality before law, abolition of caste disabilities (Caste Disabilities Removal Act 1850)\n- **Census (1871-1931):** caste ranking led castes to claim higher status\n- **Representation and reservations** raised political consciousness\n## Economic Factors\n- **Economic prosperity** and landholding of some lower castes\n- **Commercialisation** of agriculture and trade\n- **Migration** and urbanisation\n## Religious and Cultural Factors\n- **Reform movements:** Arya Samaj (Shuddhi), Brahmo Samaj, Ramakrishna Mission\n- **Bhakti tradition** and Sant movements\n- **Printing, schools, railways,** media spread Sanskritic ideas\n- **Dominant caste** model of the village\n## Weakening of Brahmin Dominance\n- **Western education** and new elite\n# Effects\n## Positive\n- **Social mobility** and change of identity\n- **Spread of cultural unity** (Great Tradition)\n- **Self-respect and educational growth** in some groups\n## Negative\n- **Strengthened the caste hierarchy** and Brahminical values\n- **Worsened status of women:** purdah, dowry, ban on widow remarriage\n- **Created imitation and division** among lower castes\n- **Did not remove economic and political inequality**\n- **Aggravated tensions** with dominant castes\n# Safety Valve Argument\n- **Sanskritization acted as a safety valve** by giving a channel of upward mobility through culture, and so reduced revolt against the system\n- **But it also** delayed radical challenge to caste (as by Phule, Periyar, Ambedkar)\n# Criticisms\n- **Ambedkar and Dalit thinkers** reject it as acceptance of Brahminical values\n- **Yogendra Singh and others:** Srinivas overstates the Brahminical model; **Kshatriya and local models** matter\n- **Not applicable** to many groups (Dalits denied entry)\n- **Ignores economic and political mobility;** concept ethnocentric\n- **De-Sanskritization,** assertion movements (Dravidian, Dalit)\n# Conclusion\n> Sanskritization explains caste mobility through imitation of upper-caste culture; it was spread by British rule, caste associations and economic change, but it strengthened hierarchy and hurt women, so its role in Indian society is mixed.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "Discuss the condition of women in the nineteenth century and the first half of the twentieth century.", "y": "2017", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Analyse the main trends in the development of Indian industries between the two World Wars.", "y": "2016, 2025", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Make a critical survey of the trade union movement in India (1919-47).", "y": "2016", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Discuss the impact of the Great Depression (1929-33) on the Indian economy and politics.", "y": "2019", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Discuss the main features of the Indian economy at the time of independence.", "y": "2015", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Trace the progress of the peasant movement in India from the First World War to Independence.", "y": "2019", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P2 - Social and Economic History of Modern India (1740-1950)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Review the contribution of Dr B. R. Ambedkar in the uplift of the Dalit classes.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Dr B. R. Ambedkar (1891-1956):** social reformer, jurist, the chief architect of the Constitution\n- **Born into a Mahar (untouchable) family at Mhow;** faced discrimination\n- **Education:** Bombay, **Columbia University (PhD 1927),** London School of Economics, Gray's Inn\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016\n# Early Organisation and Journalism\n- **Bahishkrit Hitakarini Sabha (1924)** for the depressed classes\n- **Journals:** Mooknayak (1920), Bahishkrit Bharat (1927), Janata (1930)\n# Movements for Social Equality\n- **Mahad Satyagraha (1927):** right to draw water from the Chavdar tank\n- **Burning of Manusmriti (December 1927)**\n- **Kalaram Temple entry movement at Nasik (1930)**\n- **Education:** People's Education Society (1945), Siddharth and Milind colleges\n# Political Role\n## Round Table Conferences (1930-32)\n- **Demanded separate electorates** for the depressed classes\n- **Communal Award (1932)** granted it\n## Poona Pact (September 1932)\n- **Agreement with Gandhi:** joint electorate with **reserved seats** (148 instead of 71)\n## Parties\n- **Independent Labour Party (1936)**\n- **Scheduled Castes Federation (1942)**\n# Writings\n- **Annihilation of Caste (1936)**\n- **Who Were the Shudras? (1946),** **The Untouchables (1948)**\n- **The Problem of the Rupee,** **Buddha and His Dhamma (1957)**\n# Role in the Constitution\n- **Chairman of the Drafting Committee (1947-49)**\n- **Article 17:** abolition of untouchability\n- **Fundamental rights,** equality (Articles 14-16), reservations (Articles 15(4), 16(4), 46)\n- **Free India's first Law Minister**\n- **Hindu Code Bill:** rights of women; resigned (1951) when it was delayed\n# Ideas\n- **Liberty, equality and fraternity,** social democracy\n- **Caste as graded inequality,** destruction of caste (Annihilation)\n- **Education, agitation, organisation** (\"Educate, Agitate, Organise\")\n- **Critic of Gandhi's Harijan approach**\n- **State socialism** for economic justice\n# Conversion to Buddhism\n- **14 October 1956 at Nagpur,** with lakhs of followers\n# Evaluation\n- **Gave voice and dignity to the Dalits,** secured political safeguards\n- **Critics:** differences with Congress and Gandhi\n- **Legacy:** constitutional rights, modern Dalit movement\n# Conclusion\n> Ambedkar's lifelong struggle through education, agitation, political organisation and the Constitution laid the legal and moral base for the upliftment of the Dalits and the creation of a democratic India.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Critically examine the causes of Babur success in establishing an empire in northern India. Describe the political condition of India on the eve of his invasion.", "y": "2015, 2016, 2017, 2019, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Babur (Zahiruddin Muhammad, 1483-1530):** founder of the Mughal Empire in India\n- **Ancestry:** Timur (father's side), Chingiz Khan (mother's side)\n- **Battle of Panipat (21 April 1526):** defeated Ibrahim Lodi, began Mughal rule\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2016, 2017, 2019, 2023\n# Babur's Early Career\n- **Born at Ferghana (1483),** became ruler at age 11\n- **Lost Samarqand** twice; wandering years\n- **Captured Kabul (1504),** a base for Indian plans\n- **Reasons for turning to India:** failure in Central Asia, Uzbeks' pressure (Shaibani Khan), wealth of India, Timur's claim, Kabul poor revenue\n- **Raids on India:** 1519 (Bhera), 1520, 1524 (Lahore), full invasion 1525-26\n# Political Condition of North India on the Eve of the Invasion\n## Delhi Sultanate\n- **Ibrahim Lodi (1517-26):** Afghan ruler, harsh with nobles\n- **Afghan nobility:** powerful and divided; revolts in Bihar (Darya Khan), Jaunpur\n- **Daulat Khan Lodi,** governor of Punjab, **Alam Khan** (uncle of Ibrahim), invited Babur\n- **Weak central authority,** poor finances and army\n## Regional Kingdoms\n- **Mewar:** Rana Sanga (Sangram Singh), strongest Rajput power, extended to Malwa, Gujarat border\n- **Bengal:** Nusrat Shah\n- **Gujarat:** Muzaffar Shah II (and later Bahadur Shah)\n- **Malwa:** Mahmud II, weak under Rajput influence\n- **Khandesh,** **Kashmir,** **Sind,** **Multan**\n- **Deccan:** Bahmani successor states (Ahmadnagar, Bijapur, Golconda, Berar, Bidar)\n- **Vijayanagara:** Krishnadevaraya in south\n## Condition of the Country\n- **Rich but disunited,** no unity against foreign invasion\n- **Rajputs and Afghans** in rivalry\n- **Social divisions,** peasants' burden\n# Battles and Conquest\n## First Battle of Panipat (21 April 1526)\n- **Babur's army:** about 12,000; Ibrahim: about 1 lakh men and 1,000 elephants\n- **Tulughma** (flank movement) and **araba** (cannons chained carts)\n- **Artillery:** Ustad Ali Quli and Mustafa Rumi\n- **Ibrahim Lodi killed,** Delhi and Agra occupied\n## Battle of Khanwa (17 March 1527)\n- **Rana Sanga** led Rajput confederacy\n- **Babur's speech:** declared jihad, gave up wine, remitted the Tamgha tax\n- **Result:** Rajput power broken, Babur called Ghazi\n## Battle of Chanderi (1528)\n- **Medini Rai** of Malwa defeated\n## Battle of Ghagra (1529)\n- **Afghans of Bihar and Bengal** (Mahmud Lodi) defeated\n- **Consolidation** of Mughal rule in north India\n# Causes of Babur's Success\n## Weakness of the Opponents\n- **Ibrahim Lodi's unpopularity** and the nobles' revolt\n- **No unity** between Afghans and Rajputs\n- **Out-dated military methods:** elephants and mass formation\n## Babur's Own Qualities\n- **Military genius and leadership:** discipline, courage, tactics\n- **Experience** of Central Asian wars\n- **Able generals:** Humayun, Hindu Beg, Mahdi Khwaja\n- **Personal charm** and skill in handling men\n## Military Superiority\n- **Use of artillery and matchlocks** (gunpowder), first in North India on a large scale\n- **Tulughma** (flank attack) and **araba** (cart barricade)\n- **Cavalry and archery**\n## Invitations and Opportunity\n- **Daulat Khan and Alam Khan** invited him\n- **Disunity** gave him a free hand\n## Other Factors\n- **Wealth of India** motivated soldiers\n- **Strong organisation** and supply\n- **Rana Sanga's mistakes:** delay in attacking, did not join with Ibrahim\n# Babur as Administrator\n- **Organised provinces** and revenue (jagirs to nobles)\n- **Gave land and gifts** to win loyalty\n- **Built gardens** (Aram Bagh), mosques, wells\n- **Currency:** Shahrukhi (silver) and kabuli\n- **Limited time:** about 4 years of rule; mostly military organisation\n- **Baburnama (Tuzuk-i-Baburi):** memoirs in Turkish, describes India, flora, fauna, people\n# Significance\n- **Foundation of Mughal Empire** (1526-1857)\n- **Gunpowder warfare** established\n- **Cultural link** with Central Asia and Persia\n- **Ended Lodi dynasty**\n# Conclusion\n> Babur succeeded because of the weakness and disunity of the Indian powers, his own generalship and superior military technique, thus laying the base of the Mughal Empire.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Discuss the achievements of Babur as an administrator.", "y": "2019, 2025", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Babur (1526-30):** founder of the Mughal Empire; ruled India only about four years\n- **Primarily a conqueror,** but also made some administrative arrangements\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2019, 2025\n# Limits on His Administration\n- **Short reign,** continuous wars (Panipat, Khanwa, Chanderi, Ghagra)\n- **Largely a military government**\n- **Dependent on loot and plunder** for finances\n- **Systematic structure** was developed later by Sher Shah and Akbar\n# Administrative Arrangements\n## Central and Provincial\n- **Emperor** at the head with nobles (begs) as advisers\n- **Provinces and parganas** entrusted to trusted nobles; **jagirs** given to nobles and army chiefs\n- **Provinces held by sons:** Humayun (Badakhshan), Kamran (Kabul, Qandahar), Askari\n## Revenue\n- **Lodi system continued:** parganas, qanungos, shiqdars\n- **Land revenue** and **tamgha tax** (abolished before Khanwa)\n- **Sources:** land, plunder, jizya from non-Muslims at times\n## Coinage\n- **Shahrukhi** (silver) coins as in Central Asia; **Kabuli** coins\n## Military\n- **Use of gunpowder:** artillery under Ustad Ali Quli, matchlocks\n- **Tulughma and araba** (battle tactics)\n- **Army of Turks, Afghans,** Mughals, some Indians\n## Communications\n- **Postal relays (yams)** for a fast mail service from Agra to Kabul\n- **Measurement of road distances**\n## Justice\n- **Islamic law** and Turko-Mongol customs; the emperor as final authority\n# Public Works and Culture\n- **Gardens:** Aram Bagh (Agra), Char-bagh style, wells and baolis\n- **Mosques:** Kabuli Bagh Mosque (Panipat), Jama Masjid (Sambhal), Babri Masjid (Ayodhya, built by Mir Baqi, 1528)\n- **Waterworks,** roads\n- **Baburnama (Tuzuk-i-Baburi):** autobiography in Turkish, rich source on India\n- **Patronage of poets and artists**\n# Evaluation\n- **Founder of the dynasty,** not an administrator in a full sense\n- **Conciliatory attitude toward Hindus limited,** relations with Rajputs hostile (Khanwa)\n- **Failed to organise the empire,** which Humayun inherited with difficulty\n- **Contributions:** laid the foundation for the Mughal administration, introduced the Central Asian military and court tradition\n# Historians' Views\n- **Rushbrook Williams:** Babur was a great conqueror; his administration was of a military nature\n- **Erskine:** admired his character\n- **Beveridge:** translated Baburnama\n# Conclusion\n> Babur was more a soldier and conqueror than an administrator, but his military, revenue and communication arrangements gave the Mughal empire its first base, and his gardens and memoirs enriched Indian culture.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Examine Humayun efforts in consolidating Mughal power after Babur. Why did he fail?", "y": "2024", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit I", "q": "Account for the rise of Sher Shah to power. Examine his land revenue system and administration.", "y": "2015, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Sher Shah Suri (Farid Khan, 1486-1545):** Afghan ruler who defeated Humayun and ruled North India (1540-45)\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2019\n# Early Career and Rise\n- **Born at Hissar/Sasaram;** father Hasan Khan held a jagir at Sasaram\n- **Early training:** studied at Jaunpur; improved his father's jagir\n- **Served Bahar Khan Lohani** (South Bihar); title **Sher Khan** for killing a tiger\n- **Joined Babur (1527-28)**; learned Mughal military methods\n- **Became regent of Bihar;** marriage to Lad Malika (Chunar fort)\n- **Conquered Bengal** (Gaur, 1538) and Rohtas\n# Battles\n- **Surajgarh (1534):** defeated Bengal and Bihar\n- **Chausa (June 1539):** defeated Humayun; took title **Sher Shah**\n- **Kannauj/Bilgram (May 1540):** defeated Humayun again; drove him out of India\n# Reasons for His Success\n- **Humayun's weakness:** careless, disunited Mughals\n- **Afghan support:** national feeling of Afghans against Mughals\n- **Strategic skill:** diplomacy, deception (Chausa)\n- **Control of Bihar and Bengal** resources\n- **Military ability,** use of guns, forts\n- **Administrative talent**\n# Was He a Statesman?\n- **Yes:** brilliant administration, roads, currency, justice\n- **Concern for the people's welfare**\n- **Tolerance** (Hindus in high posts: Brahmajit Gaur)\n- **Limits:** short rule (5 years), harsh towards enemies\n# Land Revenue System\n- **Measurement of land** (zabt) by rope (jarib)\n- **Pattas and qabuliyats** issued\n- **One-third of average produce** as revenue; a schedule of rates (rai) by crop and quality\n- **Payment in cash or kind**\n- **Remission in famine or loss**\n- **Land classified into good, medium, bad**\n- **Amin, Shiqdar, Qanungo, Karkun** helped\n- **Cash demand,** revenue fixed on area\n## Defects\n- **Burden on peasants:** high demand, cost of survey\n- **Corruption** of officials; harsh collection\n- **Fixed rates** did not suit changing price\n# Administration\n- **Central:** four ministers: Diwan-i-Wizarat, Diwan-i-Ariz, Diwan-i-Rasalat, Diwan-i-Insha\n- **Provinces (sarkars):** Shiqdar-i-Shiqdaran and Munsif-i-Munsifan\n- **Pargana:** Shiqdar, Amin, Qanungo, treasurer\n- **Army:** direct recruitment, branding (dagh), muster roll (chehra), about 1.5 lakh cavalry\n- **Justice:** Qazi and village panchayats; strict punishment\n- **Coins:** silver rupee (about 178 grains), copper dam\n- **Roads:** Grand Trunk Road (Sonargaon to Indus), sarais\n- **Trade:** abolished internal tolls\n# Evaluation\n- **Qanungo:** praises his policy; **Akbar built on his system**\n# Conclusion\n> Sher Shah rose through his own ability and Humayun's weakness; his sound administration and land revenue system made him a great ruler, and the base for Akbar's reforms.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Discuss the Rajput policy of Akbar. Was it successful?", "y": "2015, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Akbar (1556-1605):** greatest Mughal emperor\n- **Rajput policy:** a mix of **friendship, marriage alliance and conquest** with the Rajput rulers\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2019\n# Reasons for the Policy\n- **Strengthen Mughal power:** the Rajputs were brave warriors and rulers of Rajasthan\n- **Political support** against Afghans, Uzbeks and rebel nobles\n- **Dominate north India and the trade routes** to Gujarat\n- **Build a composite ruling class** (Hindu-Muslim cooperation)\n- **Personal belief** in tolerance (Sulh-i-kul)\n# Elements of the Policy\n## Marriage Alliances\n- **Bharmal (Raja of Amber)** gave his daughter in marriage (1562); the first Rajput alliance\n- **Others:** Bikaner, Jaisalmer, Jodhpur (Mota Raja Udai Singh's daughter to Jahangir)\n- **Rajput women kept their religion**\n## Employment in Service\n- **Bhagwan Das, Man Singh (6,000 mansab),** Todar Mal (finance), Birbal, Rai Singh\n- **Rajputs given mansabs** and governorships\n- **Rajput princes joined the army** and fought for the Mughals\n## Religious Tolerance\n- **Abolished pilgrim tax (1563)** and **jizya (1564)**\n- **Ban on forced conversion** of captives\n- **Sulh-i-kul** (universal peace)\n## Conquest of Rajput States\n- **Merta (1562)**\n- **Chittor (1568):** Rana Udai Singh fled; Jaimal and Patta heroic resistance\n- **Ranthambhor (1569):** Rai Surjan Hada accepted submission\n- **Kalinjar (1569)**\n- **Bikaner, Jaisalmer, Jodhpur** (Marwar, 1570 Nagaur Durbar) accepted suzerainty\n- **Mewar:** Rana Pratap resisted; **Battle of Haldighati (1576)**\n# Terms\n- **Rajput rulers kept their kingdoms** (watan jagirs) as vassals\n- **Accepted Mughal suzerainty,** paid tribute, gave military service\n- **Sent sons and relatives to the court**\n- **Marriage alliance** and imperial favours\n# Success of the Policy\n- **Strong support** of the Rajputs for about 150 years\n- **Military strength:** Rajput generals conquered Gujarat, Bengal, Kabul, Kashmir\n- **Hindu-Muslim cooperation,** composite culture\n- **Administrative gains:** Todar Mal's revenue system\n- **Stability** of the empire\n# Failure/Limits\n- **Mewar** (Rana Pratap) never submitted; Chittor remained a symbol\n- **Some Rajput resentment** (Chittor massacre of 1568)\n- **Policy depended on a tolerant emperor;** broke down under Aurangzeb\n# Evaluation\n- **Satish Chandra:** a policy of alliance with the main Indian ruling class\n- **Not purely liberal;** it was also practical and imperial\n- **Basis of Akbar's empire**\n# Conclusion\n> Akbar's Rajput policy of conciliation, marriage and service made the Rajputs pillars of the Mughal Empire, and was a major reason for the stability of his reign, although Mewar's resistance showed its limits.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Discuss the land revenue administration of Akbar.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Akbar's land revenue system:** the base of the Mughal economy and the empire, built on Sher Shah's measures\n- **Chief architect:** Raja Todar Mal\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016\n# Stages of Development\n- **Early period (1556-70):** Sher Shah's system (measurement, cash rates) continued\n- **Karori experiment (1573-74):** Muzaffar Khan; Crorees (karoris) assessed revenue of area yielding one crore dams\n- **Ain-i-Dahsala (Dahsala system, 1580):** Todar Mal's final scheme\n- **Revenue from 1582 reports;** Ain-i-Akbari describes it\n# Features of the Dahsala (Zabti) System\n## Measurement\n- **Land measured by Ilahi gaz** (about 41 digits) and **bamboo rope with iron rings (tanab)**\n- **Bigha** unit (Ilahi bigha)\n## Classification of Land\n- **Polaj:** cultivated every year\n- **Parauti:** left fallow for a year\n- **Chachar:** fallow for 3-4 years\n- **Banjar:** uncultivated for 5 years or more\n- **Soil quality:** good, middling, bad\n## Assessment\n- **Average produce of 10 years (dahsala)** for each crop, and local prices\n- **State's share one-third of average produce**\n- **Rates (dastur-ul-amal)** fixed in cash for each region (dasturs)\n- **Cash payment** of revenue\n## Other Systems\n- **Batai (ghalla bakhshi):** crop sharing\n- **Kankut:** estimation by experts\n- **Nasaq:** based on previous assessment (in Bengal)\n- **Zabti** used in Lahore, Multan, Allahabad, Agra, Awadh, Malwa, Gujarat, Bihar\n## Officials\n- **Amil (Amalguzar):** chief revenue collector\n- **Karori, Qanungo, Patwari, Chaudhari**\n- **Pattas and qabuliyats** given to peasants\n## Relief to Peasants\n- **Remission in drought and flood,** advance loans (taqavi) for seeds and cattle\n- **Encouraged cultivation** of waste land\n# Jagir and Khalisa\n- **Khalisa:** crown land directly under imperial control\n- **Jagirs** assigned to mansabdars for their salary\n- **Frequent transfer** to avoid local roots\n# Evaluation\n## Merits\n- **Scientific and uniform,** based on records\n- **Regular revenue** for the state\n- **Fixed rates** reduced arbitrary demand\n- **Promoted cultivation** and trade\n## Demerits\n- **Heavy and rigid** in practice; revenue could reach one-half in many areas (Irfan Habib)\n- **Role of zamindars** and intermediaries\n- **Corrupt amils,** peasants' hardship\n- **Cash payment** forced the peasant to the moneylender\n# Conclusion\n> Akbar's land revenue system, perfected by Todar Mal, measured and classified land and fixed cash rates on average produce; it gave the empire a stable income, though its practical burden on peasants could be heavy.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Discuss the cultural achievements of the age of Akbar.", "y": "2017", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Akbar's reign (1556-1605):** a period of great cultural synthesis of Indian and Persian traditions\n- **Policy:** Sulh-i-kul (universal peace), patronage of arts and learning\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2017\n# Architecture\n- **Humayun's Tomb (Delhi, 1565-72)** built under Haji Begum: first garden tomb, Persian style\n- **Agra Fort (1565-73),** red sandstone, Jahangiri Mahal\n- **Fatehpur Sikri (1571-85):** Jama Masjid, **Buland Darwaza (1576),** Panch Mahal, Diwan-i-Khas, Diwan-i-Am, Jodha Bai's palace, Birbal's house, Tomb of Salim Chishti\n- **Other forts:** Lahore, Allahabad, Ajmer\n- **Akbar's tomb at Sikandra** (completed under Jahangir)\n- **Fusion:** Hindu (Gujarat, Rajasthan) and Islamic elements, carved brackets, chhatris\n# Painting\n- **Imperial karkhana (atelier)** with Persian masters: **Mir Sayyid Ali, Abd-us-Samad**\n- **Indian artists:** Basawan, Daswanth, Miskin, Kesu Das, Lal\n- **Illustrated manuscripts:** Hamzanama, Akbarnama, Razmnama, Baburnama\n- **Features:** Persian technique with Indian themes, realism, portrait\n# Music\n- **Tansen** (Gwalior), **Baiju Bawra,** Ramdas; many ragas (Miyan ki Malhar, Darbari)\n- **Hindustani classical music** enriched\n# Literature\n## Persian\n- **Abul Fazl:** Akbarnama, Ain-i-Akbari\n- **Faizi:** poet laureate, Nal-Damayanti translation\n- **Badauni:** Muntakhab-ut-Tawarikh\n- **Abdur Rahim Khan-i-Khanan:** poet, Turki and Hindi\n- **Nizamuddin Ahmad:** Tabaqat-i-Akbari\n## Hindi and Regional\n- **Tulsidas (Ramcharitmanas, 1574),** Surdas, Raskhan, Rahim, Keshav Das\n## Translations (Maktab Khana)\n- **Mahabharata (Razmnama),** Ramayana, Atharva Veda, Rajatarangini, Lilavati, Panchatantra (Ayar-i-Danish)\n# Religion and Thought\n- **Ibadat Khana (1575),** discussions of scholars of all religions\n- **Din-i-Ilahi (1582)**\n- **Tarikh-i-Ilahi,** Ilahi calendar\n- **Abolished pilgrim tax and jizya**\n# Education\n- **Madrasas and maktabs,** syllabus including mathematics, history, agriculture, medicine\n- **Imperial library** with thousands of manuscripts\n# Importance\n- **Composite Indo-Persian culture,** foundation for later Mughal art\n- **Encouraged tolerance** and cultural exchange\n# Evaluation\n- **Mostly court-centred,** the elite benefited\n- **Akbar's ideas** shaped the Mughal ideal\n# Conclusion\n> Akbar's age saw a creative fusion of Hindu and Persian traditions in architecture, painting, music, literature and learning, making it a golden age of Mughal culture.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit II", "q": "Akbar was not only a conqueror but also the architect of the Mughal administrative system. Explain.", "y": "2025", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "Discuss the Central Asian policy of Shah Jahan. Why did it fail?", "y": "2015, 2017, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Central Asian policy of Shah Jahan:** campaign to conquer **Balkh and Badakhshan** (Central Asia) and to hold **Kandahar** against the Safavids of Persia\n- **Aim:** recover the ancestral homeland (Samarqand, Timur's land) and secure the north-west frontier\n- **Failed policy;** very costly\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2017, 2019\n# Background\n- **Timurid dream:** Babur and his descendants wished to regain Samarqand\n- **Uzbek ruler Nazr Muhammad** of Balkh (Bukhara) was weak, civil war among the Uzbeks\n- **Kandahar** had been lost under Jahangir (1622) to Shah Abbas of Persia; **Ali Mardan Khan** surrendered it to the Mughals in 1638\n# Objectives\n- **Prestige** of the dynasty\n- **Control of the north-west frontier** and trade routes\n- **Strategic frontier at Hindukush**\n- **Wealth of Central Asia** (horses)\n# The Balkh-Badakhshan Campaign (1646-47)\n- **Opportunity:** Uzbek civil war; Nazr Muhammad's son Abdul Aziz rebelled\n- **Prince Murad Baksh** led first, entered Balkh (June 1646); he left without permission and was disgraced\n- **Aurangzeb** took over (1647) and defeated Uzbeks; Nazr Muhammad fled to Persia\n- **Aurangzeb offered prayer** (namaz) in the field (act of courage)\n- **Mughals could not hold Balkh;** Uzbek guerrilla attacks (the cold, famine, lack of supplies)\n- **Withdrawal (October 1647);** Balkh returned to Abdul Aziz; heavy losses\n- **Cost:** crores of rupees spent for no gain, thousands of men lost\n# The Kandahar Campaigns\n- **Persians (Shah Abbas II)** retook Kandahar in **1649**\n- **Aurangzeb's siege (1649):** failed (lack of heavy guns)\n- **Second siege (1652)** with Sadullah Khan failed\n- **Dara Shukoh's siege (1653):** failed, though with large army and money\n- **Kandahar permanently lost**\n# Causes of Failure\n- **Distance and Hindukush mountains,** lines of communication long\n- **Climate and shortage of supplies**\n- **Guerrilla tactics** of Uzbeks, hostile population\n- **Lack of local support**\n- **Persian artillery strength,** Mughal artillery weak\n- **Poor leadership** (Dara) and divided command (Murad left)\n- **Financial drain** and inadequate planning\n# Consequences\n- **Huge financial loss,** strain on the treasury\n- **Loss of prestige,** exposed Mughal weakness\n- **Weakened army,** loss of life and horses\n- **Strained peasants** (higher revenue demand)\n- **Aurangzeb's growing fame** and Dara's failure influenced the war of succession\n- **Neglect of Deccan** and administration\n# Historians' Views\n- **Satish Chandra, Irfan Habib:** misjudged geography and costs\n- **Jadunath Sarkar:** a bad plan, with political results\n# Conclusion\n> Shah Jahan's Central Asian policy was an ambitious, costly mistake; the Mughals failed to hold Balkh and Kandahar, which drained the treasury and exposed the empire's military limits.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit III", "q": "Discuss the Deccan policy of Shah Jahan.", "y": "2019, 2023", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Examine critically the religious policy of Aurangzeb.", "y": "2019, 2025", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Aurangzeb (1658-1707):** orthodox Sunni Muslim, ruled 49 years\n- **His religious policy:** debated by historians, some call him a **bigot**, others a **political ruler**\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2019, 2025\n# Early Life and Background\n- **Strict religious education,** simple life\n- **Won throne through a war of succession** with the support of orthodox nobles\n- **Dara's liberalism** opposed\n# Measures: Islamic Orthodoxy\n- **Appointed muhtasibs (1659)** to enforce Sharia and punish unislamic acts\n- **Banned the kalima on coins,** prohibited music in court\n- **Stopped Navroz** festival, Jharokha darshan (1679) and Tuladan\n- **Banned drinking, gambling,** cannabis\n- **Compilation of Fatawa-i-Alamgiri**\n- **Qazis and ulama** given importance\n# Measures Against Hindus\n- **Jizya reimposed (1679)** on non-Muslims\n- **Customs duty:** 5 percent on Hindus versus 2.5 percent on Muslims (1665)\n- **Temples:** destruction of some, e.g. Vishwanath temple (Kashi, 1669) and Keshav Rai (Mathura, 1670), order about Somnath\n- **Hindu fairs and pilgrimages restricted**\n- **Restrictions on new temples**\n- **Preference to Muslim officers** in some posts (1670s)\n# Other Communities\n- **Sikhs:** execution of Guru Tegh Bahadur (1675), conflict with Guru Gobind Singh\n- **Shias:** restricted Muharram observances\n- **Sufis and Bohra Muslims** were also persecuted at times\n# Evidence of Tolerance\n- **Grants to temples:** Chitrakoot, Ujjain (Mahakal), Guwahati (Kamakhya), Vrindavan, Jain shrines (farmans)\n- **Many Hindu nobles:** about one-third of mansabdars, more than under Shah Jahan (Rajputs and Marathas)\n- **Jaswant Singh** in high office\n- **Jizya exempted** brahmins, women, children, poor\n- **Political causes of temple demolition** (rebellions: Mathura Jats, Bundelas)\n# Reasons for the Policy\n- **Personal piety and orthodoxy**\n- **Pressure of ulama** and Muslim nobles\n- **Political and economic reasons:** jizya to fill the treasury, control of rebellious elements\n- **Consolidating Muslim support** against the Deccan and Rajput revolts\n# Historians' Views\n- **Jadunath Sarkar:** bigot, reversed Akbar's policy; empire declined\n- **Satish Chandra:** political motives, jagirdari crisis was the real cause\n- **Athar Ali:** increased Hindu nobles\n- **M. Athar Ali, Irfan Habib:** agrarian crisis\n- **Bhimsen, Khafi Khan:** contemporary Persian writers\n# Effects\n- **Revolts:** Jats (1669, 1686), Satnamis (1672), Sikhs, Rajputs (Marwar and Mewar, 1679), Marathas\n- **Loss of Hindu support** and Rajput alliance\n- **Deccan war** drained resources\n- **Eventual decline** of the Mughal Empire\n# Critical Assessment\n- **Policy was not aimed at all Hindus** but contained discriminatory measures\n- **Orthodox but pragmatic;** the empire's weakness had agrarian, administrative and political causes\n# Conclusion\n> Aurangzeb's religious policy was orthodox and partly discriminatory, but its role in the Mughal decline should not be exaggerated, since political, economic and administrative factors were also important.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "The Deccan ulcer ruined Aurangzeb. Examine this statement critically.", "y": "2019, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **\"Deccan ulcer\":** phrase compared to Napoleon's Spanish ulcer; the long Deccan campaigns of Aurangzeb (1681-1707) drained the Mughal Empire\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2019, 2023\n# Aurangzeb's Deccan Policy\n- **Earlier Viceroy of Deccan** (1636-44, 1652-58)\n- **Conflict with Bijapur and Golconda** (shared with Shivaji)\n- **Moved to the Deccan in 1681** to fight Prince Akbar and Marathas; stayed 26 years till death (Ahmednagar, 1707)\n# Course of Campaigns\n- **Capture of Bijapur (1686)** and **Golconda (1687)**\n- **Sambhaji captured and executed (1689);** Maratha leadership: Rajaram, Tarabai\n- **Jinji, Satara** and Maratha forts\n- **Guerrilla war (ganimi kava):** Mughals took forts but lost the countryside\n# Consequences (Cost of the Deccan War)\n## Financial\n- **Huge expenditure,** treasury exhausted\n- **Plague and famine (1702-04)** in the Deccan\n## Military\n- **Armies tired,** over-extended, far from base\n- **Mansabdars unpaid;** dissatisfaction\n## Administrative\n- **Absence of the emperor** in the north for 26 years\n- **Rebellions in the north:** Jats, Sikhs, Satnamis, Bundelas, Rajputs\n- **Jagirdari crisis:** shortage of jagirs and unpaid salaries\n## Political\n- **Marathas expanded** and became stronger\n- **Mughal prestige** fell\n- **Succession struggle** after 1707\n# Other Causes of Decline\n- **Aurangzeb's religious policy** and revolts\n- **Agrarian crisis:** heavy revenue demand\n- **Factional nobility**\n- **Weak successors**\n# Historians' Views\n- **Jadunath Sarkar:** \"The Deccan ruined Aurangzeb\"\n- **Satish Chandra:** the Deccan problem and the jagirdari crisis\n- **Irfan Habib:** agrarian crisis as the main cause\n- **M. Athar Ali:** increase in Deccani nobles and conflicts\n# Evaluation\n- **Deccan campaigns were not the only cause,** but they accelerated the crisis\n- **Annexation of Bijapur and Golconda** removed buffers against the Marathas, which was an error\n# Conclusion\n> The Deccan war consumed Aurangzeb's resources and energy, aggravated the jagirdari and agrarian crisis and strengthened the Marathas; it was a major, though not the sole, cause of the empire's decline.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Discuss the issue of regional versus imperial interests during the reign of Aurangzeb.", "y": "2017, 2024", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit IV", "q": "Discuss the causes and consequences of the invasion of Nadir Shah on India.", "y": "2016, 2025", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Nadir Shah:** ruler of Persia (Afsharid dynasty, 1736), a great conqueror, \"Napoleon of Persia\"\n- **Invasion of India (1738-39):** defeated the Mughals and sacked Delhi\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016, 2025\n# Causes\n## Weakness of the Mughal Empire\n- **Weak emperor Muhammad Shah (\"Rangila\"),** factional nobles\n- **Poor defence of the north-west frontier,** neglected Kabul and Kandahar\n- **Faction in the court:** Irani vs Turani; **Saadat Khan** possibly invited Nadir\n- **Wealth of India** and Delhi's fame\n## Persian Ambitions\n- **Nadir conquered Kandahar (1738)** and wanted to control Afghan rebels\n- **Pretext:** Mughals sheltered Afghan fugitives and killed his envoy\n- **Need for money** for his army\n## Other Factors\n- **Marathas** busy in the south and Deccan\n- **Mughal military decay**\n# Course of the Invasion\n- **Ghazni, Kabul,** Peshawar, Lahore (governor Zakariya Khan submitted)\n- **Battle of Karnal (13 February 1739):** Mughal army defeated; **Saadat Khan** captured\n- **Muhammad Shah submitted;** Nadir entered Delhi (March 1739)\n- **Massacre of Delhi (22 March 1739):** about 20,000-30,000 people killed after a revolt\n- **Loot:** Peacock Throne, **Koh-i-Noor,** crores of rupees of treasure\n- **Treaty:** Mughals ceded territories west of the Indus (Kabul and Sind side)\n- **Nadir left (May 1739)**\n# Consequences\n## Political\n- **Mughal prestige collapsed;** the emperor's weakness exposed\n- **Provinces became independent:** Awadh, Bengal, Hyderabad\n- **North-west frontier lost,** road open for later invaders (Ahmad Shah Abdali)\n## Economic\n- **Treasury drained,** ruin of Delhi, trade fell\n- **Heavy taxation of peasants** to recover\n## Military\n- **Weak army** exposed; gap between the Mughals and Europeans\n## Rise of Others\n- **Marathas** expanded in the north\n- **Sikhs and Rohillas** gained\n## Later Events\n- **Abdali's invasions (1748-67),** Third Battle of Panipat (1761)\n# Historians\n- **Jadunath Sarkar:** called it a great blow to the empire\n- **Satish Chandra:** accelerated decline already present\n# Nadir Shah's Career\n- **Born (1688)** in Khorasan, of the Afshar tribe; rose as a soldier under Tahmasp II\n- **Defeated the Afghans (Ghilzai)** and Ottomans, restored the Safavids\n- **Deposed the Safavids (1736)** at Mughan and became Shah\n- **Master of Persia,** a brilliant but cruel general\n# Role of the Mughal Nobles\n- **Nizam-ul-Mulk (Asaf Jah)** negotiated with Nadir after Karnal\n- **Saadat Khan (Awadh)** was captured; he is said to have urged Nadir to take Delhi\n- **Khan-i-Dauran** was killed at Karnal\n- **Quarrels between Irani and Turani nobles** weakened defence\n- **Heavy ransom:** about 20 crore rupees demanded\n# The Massacre of Delhi\n- **22 March 1739:** rumour of Nadir's death led to revolt in Delhi; Persian soldiers attacked\n- **Nadir ordered a general massacre (qatl-i-am)** for hours; about 20,000-30,000 killed\n- **The city plundered;** Muhammad Shah begged for mercy\n# The Loot and the Settlement\n- **Peacock Throne, Koh-i-Noor,** jewels, horses, elephants, treasury of tens of crores\n- **Craftsmen and thousands taken away**\n- **Nadir remitted taxes in Persia for three years**\n- **Territories west of Indus** ceded; Muhammad Shah restored on the throne\n# Conclusion\n> Nadir Shah's invasion was possible because of the Mughal Empire's weakness, and it dealt a fatal blow to its prestige, wealth and frontier, speeding the empire's collapse.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Analyse the composition and role of the Mughal nobility.", "y": "2015, 2017, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Mughal nobility (Mansabdars):** the ruling class and steel frame of the empire\n- **Organised through the mansabdari system** (Akbar, c. 1570-71)\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2015, 2017, 2019 (and a Mansabdari question in 2024, 2025)\n# Composition of the Nobility\n## Racial and Religious Groups\n- **Turanis (Central Asians):** Chaghtai and Uzbek, early supporters of Babur and Humayun\n- **Iranis (Persians):** educated, cultured, administrators (e.g. Bairam Khan, Itimad-ud-daula, Asaf Khan)\n- **Afghans:** powerful before Akbar; Sher Shah's followers\n- **Indian Muslims (Shaikhzadas, Sayyids of Barha)**\n- **Rajputs and other Hindus:** Raja Bhagwan Das, Man Singh, Todar Mal, Birbal\n- **Marathas and Deccanis:** especially under Shah Jahan and Aurangzeb\n## Policy of Different Emperors\n- **Akbar:** balance of groups, took Rajputs and Hindus, merit-based\n- **Jahangir:** Iranis rose (Nur Jahan's family)\n- **Shah Jahan:** Iranis and Deccanis\n- **Aurangzeb:** increased Hindu and Maratha nobles (about one-third); Deccani and Maratha entry\n## Mansab System (Ranks)\n- **Zat (personal rank) and Sawar (cavalry rank)**\n- **Ranks from 10 to 10,000** (Akbar: nobles up to 5,000; princes higher)\n- **Three classes** under Shah Jahan based on sawar vs zat\n- **Dagh (branding) and chehra (descriptive roll)** for discipline\n## Pay and Jagirs\n- **Salary in cash or jagir** (revenue assignment)\n- **Jagirs transferred** regularly, to avoid local power\n- **Khalisa** (crown land) separate\n## Nature of the Nobility\n- **Not hereditary:** son had to start afresh, property escheated (zabt) on death\n- **Dependence on emperor:** appointed, promoted and dismissed by the emperor\n- **Mixed and bureaucratic** nobility, **military and civil functions**\n# Role of the Nobility\n## Military Role\n- **Furnished cavalry (sawars)** to the Mughal army\n- **Led campaigns:** Rajasthan, Deccan, Kandahar\n- **Garrison and fortress command**\n## Administrative Role\n- **Governors (subahdars), faujdars, diwans, qiladars**\n- **Revenue collection** through jagirs\n- **Law and order**\n## Political Role\n- **Faction fights** (Irani, Turani, Hindustani, Deccani groups)\n- **War of succession** (e.g. Shah Jahan's sons: Dara vs Aurangzeb)\n- **Power makers** after Aurangzeb (Sayyid brothers, Nizam-ul-Mulk)\n- **Rebellions** (Bairam Khan, Uzbeks, Mirza brothers, Khan-i-Jahan Lodi)\n## Cultural Role\n- **Patronage** of art, literature and architecture: Abdur Rahim Khan-i-Khanan, Asaf Khan, Itimad-ud-daula's tomb, Todar Mal's works\n- **Gardens, sarais, mosques, tombs**\n- **Fashion, luxury** and social life\n## Economic Role\n- **Consumers of luxury,** stimulated trade and craft\n- **Heavy pressure on peasants** for revenue, to maintain lifestyle\n- **Participation in trade** (some nobles and princes owned ships)\n# Problems and Decline\n- **Jagirdari crisis (Aurangzeb's later years):** shortage of jagirs (bejagiri), more mansabdars than available land\n- **Corruption and extravagance,** debts\n- **Factionalism** and rivalry\n- **Weak emperors** after 1707\n- **Satish Chandra, Athar Ali:** crisis of the jagirdari system as a cause of Mughal decline\n# Evaluation\n- **Strengths:** loyalty, merit, diversity, administrative skill\n- **Weaknesses:** dependence on revenue, heavy burden on peasants, factional disputes\n# Conclusion\n> The Mughal nobility was a multi-ethnic, salaried and mobile ruling class, which administered, defended and enriched the empire; its factionalism and the jagirdari crisis later weakened the empire.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Mansabdari system was the basis of the Mughal Empire military organisation. Review this statement.", "y": "2024, 2025", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Mansab:** rank; **Mansabdar:** holder of a rank in the Mughal service\n- **Introduced by Akbar** (about 1570-71), based on the Central Asian decimal system\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2024, 2025\n# Features\n- **Ranks** from 10 to 10,000 (66 grades); nobles up to 5,000, princes higher\n- **Two ranks:** **zat** (personal rank and salary) and **sawar** (cavalry to be maintained)\n- **Shah Jahan's classes:** first (sawar = zat), second (sawar = half or more), third (less than half)\n- **Salary:** in cash (naqdi) or assigned **jagir**\n- **Dagh (branding horses)** and **chehra (descriptive roll)** to prevent fraud\n- **Appointment, promotion, dismissal** by the emperor\n- **Civil and military duties** combined\n- **Not hereditary;** property escheated\n- **Ain-i-Akbari** (Abul Fazl) describes it\n# Military Basis\n- **Mansabdars supplied the Mughal cavalry** (sawars), horses, elephants, camels, carts\n- **Central army:** Ahadis, dakhili troops, artillery under the emperor\n- **Commanded armies:** Man Singh, Mirza Raja Jai Singh\n- **Frontier defence and campaigns** by contingents\n# Administrative Basis\n- **Governors, faujdars, diwans, qiladars** came from mansabdars\n- **Revenue collection** through jagirs\n- **Law and order**\n# Political Basis\n- **Created loyalty** to the emperor\n- **Balanced** Turanis, Iranis, Afghans, Rajputs, Marathas\n- **Prevented hereditary power** of nobles\n# Evaluation\n## Strengths\n- **Unified military and civil services**\n- **Merit-based,** diversified nobility\n- **Efficient control by emperor**\n## Weaknesses\n- **Corruption in muster (dagh and chehra)**\n- **Fake horses,** low quality troops (later reigns)\n- **Mansabdars' contingents** loyal to their commander in later days\n- **Jagirdari crisis (Aurangzeb):** shortage of jagirs\n- **Burden on peasants**\n- **Faction in nobility**\n# Other Military Elements\n- **Artillery** under Mir Atish; **Ahadis**; **matchlockmen**; **elephants**\n- **So mansabdari was the main, but not the only, military basis**\n# Origin and Development\n- **Origin:** Central Asian decimal system of Chengiz Khan and Timur (tens, hundreds, thousands); Persian and Delhi Sultanate influence\n- **Akbar (about 1570-71),** with Todar Mal and others; **1575 regularisation**\n- **Zat and sawar** introduced about 1594-95\n- **Jahangir:** du-aspah sih-aspah (two-horse three-horse) system for more sawar without more zat\n- **Shah Jahan:** monthly scale (shashmahi, chaharmahi) reduced actual salary, sawars cut to half or less\n- **Aurangzeb:** huge rise in number of mansabdars; shortage of jagirs\n# Pay and Expenses\n- **Ain-i-Akbari:** salary of a 5,000-rank mansabdar was about Rs 30,000 per month (illustrative); deductions for sawar\n- **Mansabdars paid for horses, elephants, camels, carts,** servants\n- **Salary in jagir (revenue assignment)** by estimated income (jama), later actual (hasil) lower\n- **Transfer of jagirs** every few years\n# Corruption and Check\n- **Dagh and chehra** needed for control, but bribery was common\n- **Mansabdars hired inferior horses,** borrowed soldiers at muster\n- **Paibaqi (reserve) land** shortage in later period\n# Conclusion of the Evaluation\n- **Mansabdari was the main basis** of army and administration, but not the only one (Ahadis, artillery, local forces)\n# Conclusion\n> The mansabdari system, with its zat and sawar ranks, supplied the army and the administration of the Mughal Empire; its decay through corruption and the jagirdari crisis weakened the empire.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Give a brief account of the central administration of the Mughals.", "y": "2016, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Mughal central administration:** highly centralised and bureaucratic system under the Emperor\n- **Developed by Akbar,** based on Sher Shah's and Central Asian-Persian traditions\n- **Repeated in exams:** 2016, 2023\n# The Emperor\n- **Supreme head:** political, military, judicial and religious head\n- **Divine kingship:** Akbar's theory of \"light from God,\" Jharokha darshan\n- **Appointed all officers,** mansabs\n- **Council of ministers** advised, but the emperor decided\n# Main Ministers and Officers\n- **Wakil (Prime Minister):** early importance (Bairam Khan); later reduced\n- **Diwan-i-Kul (Diwan):** head of revenue and finance (Todar Mal, Muzaffar Khan)\n- **Mir Bakshi:** military pay, recruitment, mansabs, intelligence\n- **Mir Saman (Khan-i-Saman):** royal household and karkhanas\n- **Sadr-us-Sudur:** religious endowments and charity (suyurghal)\n- **Qazi-ul-Quzat:** justice\n- **Muhtasib:** morals and public order\n- **Mir Atish (Darogha-i-Topkhana):** artillery\n- **Darogha-i-Dak Chowki:** post and intelligence (Waqia-navis)\n- **Mustaufi and Mushrif:** accounts and audit\n# Provincial Administration\n- **Subah (province)** under the Subahdar (Sipahsalar, Nazim)\n- **Provincial Diwan** (revenue), **Bakshi** (army), **Sadr and Qazi,** **Kotwal**\n- **Sarkar** under Faujdar and Amalguzar\n- **Pargana:** Shiqdar, Amil, Qanungo\n- **Akbar** divided empire into 12 subahs (later 15 and 21)\n# Features of the System\n- **Separation of powers** and check by rival officers (Diwan vs Bakshi)\n- **Mansabdari system** as base\n- **Jagir and khalisa**\n- **Paid officers,** transfers\n- **Intelligence network** to control officers\n- **Uniform coinage, weights and measures**\n# Evaluation\n- **Strengths:** efficient, military and civil unity, stable for about 150 years\n- **Weaknesses:** corruption, over-centralisation, depends on emperor's ability\n# Evolution of the Central Administration\n- **Babur and Humayun:** loose, military administration; ministers often powerful\n- **Sher Shah:** four departments (Diwan-i-Wizarat, Ariz, Rasalat, Insha); Akbar borrowed\n- **Akbar:** after Bairam Khan's fall (1560), reduced the Wakil's power, divided powers among Diwan, Bakshi, Mir Saman, Sadr\n- **Todar Mal:** rationalised finance; Akbar's Diwan-i-Kul\n- **Jahangir and Shah Jahan:** Wakil and Diwan became more powerful again (Asaf Khan, Sadullah Khan)\n- **Aurangzeb:** personal rule, but a weak ministry\n# Judicial Administration at the Centre\n- **Emperor** as the highest court of appeal, held open court (Diwan-i-Am)\n- **Qazi-ul-Quzat** (Chief Qazi), **Mufti, Mir Adl, Darogha-i-Adalat**\n- **Sharia, Urf (custom), Zawabit (imperial regulations)**\n- **Muhtasib** enforced morality\n# Karkhanas and Household Departments\n- **Royal workshops** (karkhanas) under the Mir Saman: weapons, textiles, jewellery, stables\n- **Imperial household** and harem administration\n- **Mushrif and Mustaufi:** accounts audit\n# Weaknesses\n- **Dependence on a capable emperor:** weak emperors led to power of the Wazir\n- **Factional nobles** and succession wars\n- **Corruption and delay**\n# Conclusion\n> The Mughal central administration was a well-organised and centralised bureaucracy under a powerful emperor, with ministers checking one another; it gave the empire long stability, though it was weakened by corruption and weak rulers later.", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Write a critical note on the development of Mughal architecture with special reference to Akbar.", "y": "2016", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Analyse the importance of historians of the reigns of Akbar and Jahangir.", "y": "2015", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}, {"p": "History P3 - Medieval India (1526-1740)", "u": "Unit V", "q": "Examine the Mughal control over European traders and its weakening by the end of the seventeenth century.", "y": "2025", "a": "", "s": "Modern History"}];
